  "explanation": "<p>Take $q$ and $k$ with independent components of mean $0$ and variance $1$. Then \\[ q\\cdot k = \\sum_{i=1}^{d_k} q_i k_i, \\qquad \\operatorname{Var}(q\\cdot k) = d_k, \\qquad \\text{typical magnitude} = \\sqrt{d_k}. \\] The quantity that grows with dimension is the <strong>standard deviation</strong>, and it grows as $\\sqrt{d_k}$, not $d_k$. Dividing by the standard deviation is what restores a constant scale whatever $d_k$ is.</p><p><strong>Dividing by $d_k$ would over-correct</strong>, shrinking the scores towards zero and flattening the softmax into a near-uniform distribution — the opposite failure, and just as useless.</p><p><strong>Both distributions sum to one</strong> — the softmax normalises either way, so the total bar height is identical. What changes is the <em>sharpness</em>. On the left the scores are large enough that the softmax saturates: one weight goes to $1$, the rest to $0$, and in that regime the softmax's gradient is essentially zero, so no learning signal flows back through the attention weights. Dividing by $\\sqrt{d_k}$ keeps the scores near $\\pm 1$ whatever the head dimension, which is where the softmax stays soft.</p>",
