  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: A dilated convolution has effective kernel size $K_{\\text{eff}} = \\mathbf{D(K-1)+1}$. The receptive-field recurrence is $R_l = \\mathbf{R_{l-1} + (K_{\\text{eff},l}-1)\\,J_{l-1}}$, and the jump recurrence is $J_l = \\mathbf{J_{l-1}\\,S_l}$.</strong> <strong>Read each one.</strong> $K_{\\text{eff}}$: a dilated kernel has $K$ taps with $D-1$ gaps between them, so it spans $D(K-1)+1$ positions — $D = 1$ recovers $K_{\\text{eff}} = K$. $J_l$: the jump is how far apart, <em>in input pixels</em>, two adjacent units of layer $l$ are; it is the running product of all strides so far. $R_l$: adding a layer widens the field by $K_{\\text{eff}}-1$ steps, and each step is worth $J_{l-1}$ input pixels — <strong>the jump of the layer below, not the current one</strong>. Getting that index wrong is the commonest error, and Q42 is where it shows.</p><p>Start the recursion at $R_0 = 1$, $J_0 = 1$. <strong>Padding never appears</strong> — it moves where outputs sit, not how far back they reach.</p>",
