  "explanation": "<p><strong>1st is the only false one, and it is worth being clear why.</strong> Shared weights would require the same function to be applicable to both inputs. One encoder consumes a pixel grid, the other a token sequence; there is no single set of weights that is defined on both. What CLIP shares is not the <em>weights</em> but the <em>output space</em> — and the contrastive loss is what forces the two separately-parameterised encoders to agree about where things live in it.</p><p><strong>Why the 4th sits uneasily beside the 3rd.</strong> Zero-shot classification at test time still rests on massive <em>paired</em> supervision at training time — 400 million image–text pairs. “Zero-shot” describes the downstream task, not the pre-training.</p>",
