  "exam": 2,
  "num": 7,
  "week": "W8",
  "explanation": "<p>A <strong>shortcut</strong> is any low-level cue that solves the proxy task without requiring the semantic understanding the task was designed to elicit. If one exists, the network will find it, and pre-training silently produces nothing useful.</p><table class='xt'><tr><th><strong>shortcut</strong></th><th><strong>what it leaks</strong></th><th><strong>the fix</strong></th></tr><tr><td>mean/variance matching</td><td>which patches were adjacent, from colour statistics alone</td><td>normalise each patch independently</td></tr><tr><td>edge continuation</td><td>the same, from lines running across the cut</td><td>leave a gap between patches</td></tr><tr><td>chromatic aberration</td><td><em>absolute</em> position in the frame, because the lens splits colour more towards the edges</td><td>jitter the colour channels, or crop centrally</td></tr></table><p><strong>Why the 2nd is not a shortcut.</strong> The eight positions in context prediction are sampled uniformly, so there is no imbalance — and even if there were, class imbalance is an ordinary training nuisance, not a way to solve the task without understanding it.</p>",
