  "explanation": "<p>The gradient is with respect to <strong>$\\mathbf{x}$, not $\\theta$</strong>. Option (a) is the gradient used in maximum-likelihood <em>training</em> — also called the score in statistics, which is exactly why the distinction has to be made deliberately here.</p><p>Dimensionally: $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}}\\log p$ is a vector living in <em>data</em> space, one component per pixel. That is what makes it usable as a direction to move a sample in.</p>",
