  "week": "W8",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: The InfoNCE bound on mutual information is $\\mathrm{MI} \\ge \\mathbf{\\log N} - \\mathcal{L}$, so the number of candidates sets a hard <strong>ceiling</strong> (an upper bound the method cannot exceed however well it trains).</strong> Here $N$ is the number of candidates: one positive plus $N-1$ negatives.</p><p><strong>Read the inequality carefully, because it is the reverse of the usual intuition.</strong> The loss $\\mathcal{L}$ is non-negative, so the bound can never exceed $\\log N$. Even a <em>perfect</em> contrastive model with $\\mathcal{L} = 0$ certifies only $\\log N$ nats of mutual information. At $N = 8192$ that is $\\ln 8192 = 9.01$ nats, or $13$ bits.</p><p>So a batch or queue of $8192$ can never demonstrate more than $13$ bits of shared information, however good the representation actually is — and this is the formal reason contrastive methods keep reaching for larger $N$. It is also the reason MoCo's queue was such an effective idea (Q6): it raises $N$ without raising the batch size.</p>",
