  "exam": 2,
  "num": 31,
  "week": "W9",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: The trade-off between learning new tasks and retaining old ones is called the <strong>stability–plasticity</strong> dilemma. The four families of continual learning method are <strong>regularisation-based</strong>, <strong>replay-based</strong>, <strong>optimisation-based</strong> and <strong>architecture-based</strong>.</strong> The four families again, with the one-line distinction that separates them:</p><table class='xt'><tr><th><strong>regularisation</strong></th><th>penalise <em>moving</em> parameters that mattered before; store no old data (EWC, LwF)</th></tr><tr><td><strong>replay</strong></td><td>store, or generate, some old <em>data</em> and keep training on it</td></tr><tr><td><strong>optimisation</strong></td><td>constrain the <em>gradient</em> — project updates so they cannot increase loss on old tasks (GEM)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>architecture</strong></td><td>give each task its own <em>parameters</em>: new branches, masks, or adapters</td></tr></table><p>Each family intervenes at a different point in the training loop — the data, the loss, the gradient, the parameters — which is the cleanest way to hold all four in memory.</p>",
