  "explanation": "<p>The logic chain: you want $w(x) = p_{\\text{target}}(x)/p_{\\text{source}}(x)$, but you cannot evaluate either density. A classifier trained to tell source from target gives you the ratio for free, because \\[ \\frac{1 - c(\\text{src}\\mid x)}{c(\\text{src}\\mid x)} = \\frac{p(\\text{tgt}\\mid x)}{p(\\text{src}\\mid x)} \\;\\propto\\; \\frac{p_{\\text{target}}(x)}{p_{\\text{source}}(x)} \\] by Bayes, with the class priors absorbed into the constant. So: train the discriminator, convert its output into weights, apply the weights, and only then train the thing you actually wanted. Steps (c) and (d) are throwaway machinery in service of (a).</p>",
