  "num": 42,
  "week": "W9",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Answer: $\\mathbf{14}$ GB, then $\\mathbf{56}$ GB; ratio $\\mathbf{4\\times}$</strong></p><p><strong>Weights.</strong> $7\\times10^9$ parameters $\\times$ $2$ bytes $= 1.4\\times10^{10}$ bytes $= \\mathbf{14}$ GB (decimal), which is $13.0$ GiB.</p><p><strong>Weights $+$ gradients $+$ Adam's two states.</strong> One gradient and two optimiser states per trainable parameter, all at the same precision, gives four copies: \\[ 4 \\times 14 = \\mathbf{56}\\ \\text{GB}, \\qquad \\text{ratio } \\mathbf{4\\times}. \\]</p><p><strong>State the assumption, because it is doing real work.</strong> The $4\\times$ holds only if the optimiser states are stored at the <em>same</em> precision as the weights. In practice mixed-precision training keeps Adam's moments — and often a master copy of the weights — in fp32, which gives $14 + 14 + 56 = 84$ GB, a ratio of $6\\times$ rather than $4\\times$. Either answer is correct if the assumption is stated; neither is correct if it is not.</p><p><strong>And note what is still missing from both figures:</strong> activations. They can rival the weights in size and are the reason gradient checkpointing exists.</p>",
