  "exam": 3,
  "num": 7,
  "week": "W2",
  "explanation": "<p>The one asymmetry is where $Q$ comes from as against $K$ and $V$. In self-attention all three come from the same sequence; in cross-attention the queries come from the decoder and the keys and values from the encoder, which is precisely how the decoder reads the source.</p><p><strong>2nd is false, and it is the useful consequence.</strong> $Q$ is $n_q \\times d$ and $K$ is $n_k \\times d$; the score matrix is $n_q \\times n_k$ and nothing requires $n_q = n_k$. That is why a $7$-word source can be translated into a $12$-word target, and why DETR's $100$ object queries can attend to a few thousand image tokens (Q13).</p><p><strong>4th is false, and it is worth being exact about.</strong> Cross-attention does not <em>replace</em> self-attention in the decoder — it is added beside it. Each decoder layer runs masked self-attention over the target so far, <em>then</em> cross-attention into the encoder, then the feed-forward network. Three sub-layers, not two.</p>",
