  "explanation": "<p>Every option is true here. Check rather than assume — two questions in this paper have all four correct, and refusing to mark the last one on principle costs you both.</p><p><strong>1st and 3rd are the mechanism and its purpose.</strong> Setting future scores to $-\\infty$ makes their softmax weights exactly zero, so position $t$ sees only $1 \\dots t$. Without that, the model predicting token $t$ could simply look at token $t$, and the loss would go to zero while the model learned nothing.</p><p><strong>2nd is why training is parallel.</strong> Because the mask enforces causality <em>inside</em> one forward pass, all positions of a target sequence can be scored at once, rather than generated one at a time as at inference.</p><p><strong>4th is the BERT/GPT distinction, stated from the architecture's side.</strong> The original transformer's encoder is deliberately <em>bidirectional</em> — its self-attention is unmasked, every position attends both ways. Only the decoder masks. BERT keeps the unmasked, encoder-style attention and instead masks tokens in the <em>input</em>; GPT keeps the mask on the <em>attention</em>. Same word, two different mechanisms (Q9, Q52).</p>",
