  "explanation": "<p>Propagating with a raw $\\tilde A$ sums the neighbours' features. A node with $100$ neighbours then receives a vector roughly $100$ times larger than a node with one, purely because of its degree and regardless of what those neighbours contain. Stack a few layers and the scale differences compound.</p><p><strong>What the two $D^{-1/2}$ factors do.</strong> $\\tilde D^{-1/2}\\tilde A\\tilde D^{-1/2}$ divides each edge by $\\sqrt{d_i d_j}$, discounting the contribution by <em>both</em> endpoints' degrees — so a message from a hub is damped, and so is a message into a hub. The symmetric form keeps the operator symmetric, which the eigen-analysis behind spectral GNNs relies on.</p><p><strong>(c) is inverted:</strong> the self-loop is deliberately <em>added</em> before normalising ($\\tilde A = A + I$), so that a node keeps its own features.</p>",
