  "explanation": "<p>Centre loss adds $\\lambda \\sum_k \\lVert \\phi(x_k) - c_{y_k}\\rVert^2$ to the softmax objective: each sample is pulled towards a learned centre for its own class.</p><p><strong>Why it matters historically.</strong> It is the step that identified the real problem — softmax gives <em>separability</em>, and what an embedding needs is <em>compactness</em> (Q56). Centre loss attacks compactness directly; the angular-margin losses that followed (Q29) attack it in a way that also controls the gap between classes — which is what option (a) describes, and is a later idea, not this one.</p><p><strong>One detail worth knowing.</strong> The centres are learned, but more slowly than the network weights, so they behave like a stable moving average of where each class sits. If they moved as fast as the embeddings, the target would chase the samples and neither would settle.</p>",
