  "explanation": "<p><strong>Only one option is correct, and the first is a trap worth falling into once.</strong> A Siamese network's two branches <em>share</em> their weights — they are the same network applied twice. Independent weights would break the whole construction: $\\phi_1(x)$ and $\\phi_2(x)$ would differ, so even the distance from an input to <em>itself</em> would not be zero.</p><p><strong>2nd is the correct one, and it is the line back to the Mahalanobis distance.</strong> $\\mathcal{D}^2 = (x_i-x_j)^{\\top} M (x_i-x_j)$ with $M = W^{\\top}W$ is exactly $\\lVert Wx_i - Wx_j \\rVert^2$: a Euclidean distance after a <em>linear</em> map. A Siamese network replaces $W$ with a deep non-linear $\\phi$ and learns it. The factorisation is also what keeps $M$ positive semi-definite, so the result is still a distance.</p><p><strong>3rd and 4th are false.</strong> The usual losses are contrastive and triplet, neither of which is a classification loss; and a contrastive pair is labelled <em>similar or dissimilar</em>, so same-class pairs are not merely allowed but essential — they are the half that supplies the “pull” (Q26).</p>",
