  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: Self-attention is permutation <strong>equivariant</strong>, which is exactly why a transformer needs <strong>positional encoding</strong>. In the original transformer, layer normalisation is applied <strong>after</strong> the residual addition (the “post-LN” arrangement).</strong> <strong>Equivariant, not invariant — the distinction is the point.</strong> Permute the input and the output permutes identically; no information is destroyed, but no notion of <em>where</em> is created either. The layer treats its input as a set, so order has to be injected as content.</p><p><strong>Post-LN is what the 2017 paper did</strong>, and it is why the original needed a learning-rate warm-up to train stably: the residual path passes through a normalisation, so gradients to early layers are attenuated. Most modern implementations move to pre-LN — normalise <em>before</em> the sub-layer, leaving the residual path clean — which trains without warm-up. Knowing which one is being asked about is the whole question.</p>",
