  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: Global attention over $N$ patches is <strong>quadratic</strong> in $N$, whereas Swin's window attention is <strong>linear</strong>. Shifting the partition between successive blocks exists so that <strong>information can cross window boundaries</strong>.</strong> <strong>Why window attention is linear and not merely smaller.</strong> Fix the window at $M \\times M$ patches. The number of windows grows with $N$, but the cost <em>inside</em> each is a constant $M^4$, so the total is $(N/M^2)\\cdot M^4 = N M^2$ — linear in $N$ with a constant set by the window, not by the image. Q46 puts numbers on it.</p><p><strong>And why the shift is not optional.</strong> Without it, the partition never changes, so two patches in different windows can never exchange information no matter how many layers you stack — the model would be a set of independent local transformers. The shift by $\\lfloor M/2 \\rfloor$ moves every boundary into the interior of a window in the next block, so a path exists between any two patches after enough layers.</p>",
