  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: A point-cloud network must be invariant to <strong>point permutation</strong>, <strong>spatial (rigid) transformation</strong> and <strong>sampling density</strong>. PointNet obtains the first of these with a shared per-point MLP followed by a <strong>symmetric</strong> function — concretely, <strong>max pooling</strong>.</strong> <strong>Why the shared MLP is not the invariant part.</strong> Applying the same MLP to every point is <em>equivariant</em>: shuffle the points and the features shuffle with them. The invariance appears only at the pooling step, because $\\max$ over a set does not depend on the order the set is presented in. Exactly the same division of labour as in a GNN, where layers are equivariant and only the readout is invariant (Q24).</p><p><strong>And the other two are handled elsewhere:</strong> the T-Net for spatial transformation (Q20), and PointNet++'s multi-scale grouping for sampling density.</p>",
