  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: In a GAT the coefficient $\\alpha_{ij}$ is obtained by applying a <strong>softmax</strong> to the scores $e_{ij}$ over <strong>the neighbourhood $\\mathcal{N}(i)$</strong>. The two remedies for vanishing gradients in a deep GNN are <strong>self-loops</strong> and <strong>skip connections</strong>.</strong> <strong>Over the neighbourhood, not over all nodes.</strong> That restriction is what keeps a GAT sparse: $\\alpha_{ij}$ exists only where an edge does, so the cost is $O(|\\mathcal{E}|)$ and not $O(|\\mathcal{V}|^2)$. A transformer is the same computation with the neighbourhood taken to be everything.</p><p><strong>Why those two remedies specifically.</strong> A self-loop guarantees that $\\mathbf{h}^{(l)}_i$ contributes to $\\mathbf{h}^{(l+1)}_i$ directly, so there is a path that does not pass through any neighbour. A skip connection does the same <em>across</em> layers, exactly as in a ResNet: the identity branch has derivative $1$, so the gradient reaches early layers without being multiplied by a weight matrix at every step. Both add a route whose Jacobian is the identity — which is the same cure as gated units in an RNN (Q50).</p>",
