  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: The triplet loss is $\\mathcal{L} = \\mathbf{\\max\\bigl(0,\\ \\mathcal{D}(a,p) - \\mathcal{D}(a,n) + m\\bigr)}$. A negative is <strong>hard</strong> when $\\mathcal{D}(a,n) < \\mathcal{D}(a,p)$, and <strong>semi-hard</strong> when $\\mathcal{D}(a,p) < \\mathcal{D}(a,n) < \\mathcal{D}(a,p) + m$.</strong> <strong>Read the hinge.</strong> The loss is zero as soon as $\\mathcal{D}(a,n) \\ge \\mathcal{D}(a,p) + m$ — the negative is at least $m$ further away than the positive. Anything closer than that is inside the margin and produces gradient.</p><p><strong>So the three categories in Q27 are just the three regions of this one inequality</strong>, and the margin $m$ is what makes the middle region exist at all. Without it there would be only “already correct” and “wrong”, and the model would stop as soon as the ordering was right by any amount — with no gap, a single distance threshold could not be chosen (Q56).</p>",
