  "explanation": "<p><strong>Clearing the gradients first is the step people leave out</strong>, and in PyTorch it is a real bug rather than a stylistic point: <code>backward()</code> <em>accumulates</em> into <code>.grad</code> rather than overwriting it. Forget <code>zero_grad()</code> and step $n$ is taken on the sum of the first $n$ gradients, so the effective learning rate grows without bound and training diverges for no visible reason.</p><p><strong>Everything else is forced by the chain rule.</strong> You cannot compute a loss before a prediction exists, cannot differentiate a loss that has not been computed, and cannot apply an update before you have the gradient.</p>",
