  "explanation": "<table class='xt'><tr><th><strong>voxel grid</strong></th><th>memory is cubic in resolution, and nearly all of it holds empty space (Q47, Q54)</th></tr><tr><td><strong>point cloud</strong></td><td>an unordered set with no grid, so “the neighbour to the left” is undefined and a standard convolution has nothing to slide over</td></tr><tr><td><strong>range view</strong></td><td>a perspective projection, so apparent size falls with distance and one kernel size cannot fit both (Q19)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>octree</strong></td><td>adaptive and compact, but traversal is pointer-chasing, which is exactly what a GPU is worst at — the reason OctNet is a <em>hybrid</em></td></tr></table><p><strong>Reading the four together is the point of the question.</strong> Every 3D representation buys one thing with another: the grid buys convolution with memory, the point cloud buys memory with structure, the projection buys a 2D CNN with geometry, and the octree buys memory with access speed. There is no canonical 3D representation, and this is why.</p>",
