  "explanation": "<p><strong>Answer: $\\mathbf{235{,}146}$</strong></p><table class='xt'><tr><th><strong>layer</strong></th><th><strong>arithmetic</strong></th><th><strong>parameters</strong></th></tr><tr><td>$784 \\to 256$</td><td>$784\\cdot256 + 256$</td><td>$200{,}960$</td></tr><tr><td>$256 \\to 128$</td><td>$256\\cdot128 + 128$</td><td>$32{,}896$</td></tr><tr><td>$128 \\to 10$</td><td>$128\\cdot10 + 10$</td><td>$1{,}290$</td></tr><tr><td></td><td></td><td>$\\mathbf{235{,}146}$</td></tr></table><p><strong>Note where the parameters are.</strong> The first layer holds $85\\%$ of them, purely because $784$ is large — a fully connected layer costs one weight per (input, output) pair, and nothing is shared. Compare Q43: a convolution touching a $224\\times224$ image needed $9\\,472$. That gap is the parameter argument for convolutions, in two numbers.</p>",
