  "id": "e04q02",
  "exam": 4,
  "num": 2,
  "week": "W1",
  "explanation": "<p>Separate the two ideas that a convolution bundles together, because they save different things.</p><p><strong>Local connectivity</strong> says an output unit looks at $k^2C_{\\text{in}}$ inputs instead of all of them. That removes both multiplications and weights: fewer connections means fewer of each.</p><p><strong>Weight sharing</strong> says the <em>same</em> $k^2C_{\\text{in}}$ weights are reused at every spatial position. The number of <em>distinct</em> weights collapses — a conv layer's parameter count is independent of the input resolution, which is the remarkable part — but the multiplication still has to be performed at every position. Sharing a number does not save you from using it.</p><p>So the FLOPs are $O(HW\\cdot k^2 C_{\\text{in}}C_{\\text{out}})$, with $HW$ still in there, while the parameters are $k^2C_{\\text{in}}C_{\\text{out}} + C_{\\text{out}}$, with $HW$ absent.</p><p><strong>Why (d) is not even in the right category.</strong> Weight sharing is about which weights are equal, not about which inputs are reached. The receptive field is unchanged if you untie every position's weights.</p>",
  "type": "single",
