  "id": "e04q04",
  "exam": 4,
  "num": 4,
  "week": "W2",
  "explanation": "<p>Take $q$ and $k$ to have independent components with mean $0$ and variance $1$. Then $q\\cdot k = \\sum_{i=1}^{d_k} q_ik_i$ is a sum of $d_k$ independent terms each of variance $1$, so it has variance $d_k$ and standard deviation $\\sqrt{d_k}$. At $d_k = 64$ the scores are routinely $\\pm 8$; at $d_k = 512$, $\\pm 23$.</p><p>Now put that into a softmax. The gap between the largest score and the rest grows with $\\sqrt{d_k}$, so the softmax output approaches a one-hot vector. Its Jacobian is $\\operatorname{diag}(p) - pp^{\\top}$, which vanishes as $p$ becomes one-hot. <em>No gradient reaches the attention weights at all.</em> Dividing by $\\sqrt{d_k}$ restores unit variance and keeps the softmax in the region where it still has a derivative.</p><p><strong>Why not (b).</strong> The softmax makes the weights sum to one, and it does that whatever constant you divide by. Scaling changes the <em>temperature</em>, not the normalisation.</p><p><strong>Why not (d).</strong> Nothing here touches the $O(n^2 d)$ cost — the same number of pairs is scored either way.</p>",
