  "exam": 4,
  "num": 6,
  "week": "W2",
  "explanation": "<p>Segment-level recurrence, relative positions, and extended context are Transformer-XL's three innovations, so the 1st, 3rd and 4th are its content.</p><p><strong>Why relative encoding is not an optional extra.</strong> Once you attend over a cached segment, absolute positional encodings become incoherent: position $3$ of the current segment and position $3$ of the cached one carry the <em>same</em> encoding while being far apart in the text. Relative encoding indexes by the distance between two tokens, which stays meaningful across the boundary. Segment recurrence forces the change; the two go together.</p><p><strong>The 2nd is false, and it is the point of the design.</strong> The cached states are treated as fixed inputs — stop-gradient. If gradients ran back through them the graph would grow with every segment and the memory saving would vanish, which is the whole reason for caching rather than simply using a longer window.</p><p><strong>Context fragmentation, precisely.</strong> Chop a document into fixed segments and the first tokens of each segment have no context at all — the model must predict them cold. Attending over the previous segment's states removes exactly that, so the 4th is true.</p>",
