  "id": "e04q09",
  "exam": 4,
  "num": 9,
  "week": "W3",
  "explanation": "<p>The vector-attention layer is \\[ \\mathbf{y}_i = \\sum_{\\mathbf{x}_j \\in \\mathcal{X}(i)} \\underbrace{\\rho\\bigl(\\gamma(\\varphi(\\mathbf{x}_i) - \\psi(\\mathbf{x}_j) + \\delta)\\bigr)} _{\\text{how much}} \\odot \\underbrace{\\bigl(\\alpha(\\mathbf{x}_j) + \\delta\\bigr)}_{\\text{what}}, \\qquad \\delta = \\theta(\\mathbf{p}_i - \\mathbf{p}_j). \\] The two occurrences do different jobs, and dropping either one loses something specific.</p><p><strong>Inside the weights</strong>, $\\delta$ lets the attention depend on where the neighbour is: a point $2$ cm away can be weighted differently from one $20$ cm away even when their features are identical. Remove it and the layer becomes blind to geometry when deciding whom to listen to.</p><p><strong>Added to the value</strong>, $\\delta$ puts the geometry into what is actually summed. Remove it and the output is a weighted average of <em>features only</em> — the network could know that a neighbour matters a great deal without ever encoding the direction it lies in. For a point cloud, where the relative position of a neighbour <em>is</em> most of the information, that is a serious loss.</p><p><strong>Why not (d).</strong> Permutation invariance comes from summing over the neighbourhood, and a sum is symmetric whatever is inside it. The subtraction $\\varphi(\\mathbf{x}_i) - \\psi(\\mathbf{x}_j)$ is what makes the attention a <em>vector</em> rather than a scalar — it produces a weight per channel, so different feature channels can be mixed in different proportions. That is the “vector” in vector attention, and it is unrelated to invariance.</p>",
  "type": "single",
