  "exam": 4,
  "num": 12,
  "week": "W4",
  "explanation": "<p>PointNet++ needs a subset of points to act as centroids for local neighbourhoods. What it wants from that subset is <em>coverage</em> — centroids spread over the object's surface.</p><p><strong>The 4th states the failure of the obvious alternative, and the 1st states the fix.</strong> Random sampling draws in proportion to density, so a LiDAR scan — dense near the sensor, sparse far away — yields almost all its centroids from the near field. FPS instead picks each new point to be as far as possible from those already chosen, which spreads them by <em>geometry</em> rather than by how thickly the region happened to be sampled.</p><p><strong>The 2nd is false and is what FPS costs you.</strong> Random sampling is $O(1)$ per point; FPS maintains a distance-to-chosen-set for every point and updates it at each of the $S$ selections, so it is $O(NS)$. You buy coverage with compute, and it is worth it.</p><p><strong>The 3rd is false and confuses two mechanisms.</strong> Permutation equivariance comes from applying a shared function per point and aggregating with a symmetric operation. FPS is a selection rule; note in fact that the <em>set</em> it returns is permutation-invariant only up to the choice of seed, which is why implementations fix or randomise the seed deliberately.</p>",
