  "id": "e04q13",
  "exam": 4,
  "num": 13,
  "week": "W5",
  "explanation": "<p>A node's neighbourhood is a <strong>multiset</strong> — a collection in which repeats matter but order does not. The aggregator's job is to reduce it to one vector, and the choice determines what the layer can and cannot tell apart.</p><p><strong>The 2nd and 3rd are the same fact, stated abstractly and concretely.</strong> Take a neighbourhood of three nodes with feature $a$ and another of six nodes with feature $a$. Mean returns $a$ for both. Max returns $a$ for both. Sum returns $3a$ and $6a$ — it has counted. Since a great many graph properties are counting properties (degree, how many carbon atoms a bond joins), this is a genuine gap in expressive power, and it is the argument behind Graph Isomorphism Networks.</p><p><strong>The 4th is the price.</strong> Sum is unbounded: a hub with $10^4$ neighbours produces features $10^4$ times larger than a leaf's. Feed that into a deep stack and the activations and gradients scale with degree, which is unstable. This is precisely why the GCN normalises, using $c_{ij} = 1/\\sqrt{\\tilde D_{ii}\\tilde D_{jj}}$ — and why the GCN, in buying stability, gives up the counting ability the sum had.</p><p><strong>The 1st is false</strong>, and the reason is worth saying plainly: addition is commutative, so the sum over a neighbourhood does not depend on the order the neighbours are listed in. Sum, mean and max are all permutation-invariant. They differ in expressiveness, not in symmetry.</p>",
