  "explanation": "<p>$L = D - A$ has one zero eigenvalue per connected component, and no others. So the null space has dimension $k = 3$ and \\[ \\operatorname{rank}(L) = N - k = 9 - 3 = 6 . \\]</p><p><strong>Why the multiplicity counts components.</strong> $L$ is positive semi-definite with the quadratic form $x^{\\top}Lx = \\sum_{(i,j)\\in\\mathcal{E}}(x_i-x_j)^2$. That is zero exactly when $x$ takes the same value at both ends of every edge — that is, when $x$ is constant on each connected component. The indicator vectors of the components are therefore a basis for the kernel, and there are $k$ of them.</p><p>Useful companion facts from the same object: $\\operatorname{trace}(L) = \\sum_i \\deg(i) = 2|\\mathcal{E}|$, and $L\\mathbf{1} = 0$ always, so a connected graph still has one zero eigenvalue.</p>",
