  "id": "e04q18",
  "exam": 4,
  "num": 18,
  "week": "W7",
  "explanation": "<p>Fusion depth is one axis with a single trade-off along it: <em>the earlier the modalities meet, the more they can interact — and the more alike they must be to meet at all.</em></p><p><strong>Early</strong> fusion concatenates raw inputs and needs homogeneous modalities. <strong>Late</strong> fusion runs a separate model per modality and combines only the predictions. <strong>Intermediate</strong> fusion combines features, handles heterogeneous inputs, and is the transformer default.</p><p><strong>So the 1st, 2nd and 3rd each attach to a different point on that axis than late fusion.</strong> Late allows the <em>least</em> interaction, not the most — by the time you are averaging two predictions, each model has already committed. Homogeneity is early fusion's requirement. Intermediate is the transformer default.</p><p><strong>The 4th is late fusion's real advantage and worth keeping.</strong> Each branch produces a usable output on its own, so a dead camera leaves the LiDAR branch still answering; you drop a vote and carry on degraded. Early fusion cannot do this at all — its first layer expects a tensor of fixed shape, and a missing modality is not a smaller input but an ill-formed one. For a safety-critical system this can outweigh late fusion's weaker interaction, which is why the “worst” option on the interaction axis is still deployed.</p>",
