  "week": "W7",
  "explanation": "<p>The four models differ along two axes: how the image is turned into tokens, and whether there is one stream or two. Line the runtimes up against the axes and the answer is immediate.</p><table class='xt'><tr><th><strong>Model</strong></th><th><strong>Image tokens</strong></th><th><strong>Streams</strong></th><th><strong>Runtime</strong></th></tr><tr><td>ViLT</td><td>linear patch projection</td><td>single</td><td>$\\sim\\!15$ ms</td></tr><tr><td>Pixel-BERT</td><td>CNN grid features</td><td>single</td><td>$\\sim\\!60$–$160$ ms</td></tr><tr><td>UNITER</td><td>region features</td><td>single</td><td>$\\sim\\!900$ ms</td></tr><tr><td>ViLBERT</td><td>region features</td><td>dual</td><td>$\\sim\\!920$ ms</td></tr></table><p>Sort by tokenisation and the runtimes sort with it. Sort by stream count and they do not: UNITER is single-stream and takes $900$ ms, ViLT is single-stream and takes $15$. The two region-feature models differ from each other by $20$ ms — the cost of the second stream — and from ViLT by nearly a factor of sixty.</p><p><strong>The cause is the object detector.</strong> Region features require running a detector over the image before the transformer sees anything; about $880$ of UNITER's $900$ ms is spent there. Region features carry two further costs worth naming: the detector's class list is fixed, so anything outside it is invisible, and the background between the boxes is discarded outright. ViLT's contribution is to observe that a linear projection of raw patches — the cheapest tokeniser imaginable — is good enough, which removes the detector and with it the bottleneck.</p>",
