  "id": "e04q20",
  "exam": 4,
  "num": 20,
  "week": "W7",
  "explanation": "<p>Write out the outer product with the appended ones, for $\\mathbf{x}_A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{2}$ and $\\mathbf{x}_B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{2}$: \\[ [\\mathbf{x}_A,1]^{\\top}\\!\\cdot[\\mathbf{x}_B,1] = \\begin{pmatrix} a_1b_1 & a_1b_2 & a_1 \\\\ a_2b_1 & a_2b_2 & a_2 \\\\ b_1 & b_2 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\] Everything the question asks about is visible in that matrix.</p><p><strong>2nd:</strong> the top-left block holds every $a_ib_j$ — each feature of $A$ meets each feature of $B$. That is the bilinear part, and it is what lets the meaning of one modality depend on the other.</p><p><strong>1st:</strong> the last column and last row hold the bare $a_i$ and $b_j$, and the corner holds $1$. So a single linear map $W$ over this matrix has access to the bimodal terms, the unimodal terms and a bias. Bilinear fusion, without the appended ones, has the cross terms <em>only</em> — so it cannot express “ignore the caption and just classify the image”, which is sometimes exactly right.</p><p><strong>4th follows from 1st:</strong> setting the bilinear part of $W$ to zero leaves $w_1\\mathbf{x}_A + w_2\\mathbf{x}_B$, which is additive fusion. Tensor fusion contains it.</p><p><strong>3rd is the cost, and it is the reason this is not the default.</strong> A third modality makes the object $\\bigotimes_{i=1}^{3}[\\mathbf{x}_i,1]$, of size $\\prod_i (d_i+1)$. Growth is <em>multiplicative</em>, not additive: $128 \\times 768 \\to 512$ is already about $5.1\\times10^{7}$ weights, and adding a $2048$-dimensional video stream multiplies that by $2049$ to about $10^{11}$. That is the number that kills it.</p><p><strong>A trap worth naming while you are here.</strong> Concatenating two vectors and passing them through one linear layer is <em>not</em> a richer operation — $W[\\mathbf{x}_A;\\mathbf{x}_B] = W_A\\mathbf{x}_A + W_B\\mathbf{x}_B$, which is additive fusion written differently. If you want interaction you have to multiply somewhere.</p>",
