  "num": 21,
  "week": "W8",
  "explanation": "<p>\\[ MI\\bigl[f(x), f(x^{+})\\bigr] \\;\\ge\\; \\log N - \\mathcal{L} \\] Set $\\mathcal{L} = 0$, its best possible value. The bound still only certifies $\\log N$. That is the 1st, and the reason is combinatorial rather than statistical: an $N$-way multiple-choice question has $N$ distinguishable answers, so answering it perfectly can demonstrate at most $\\log N$ of information. Nothing about the encoder enters that argument.</p><p><strong>The 2nd is the same statement rearranged.</strong> $N = 2^{\\text{bits}}$, so each additional bit doubles the number of candidates. Going from $8$ to $16$ certified bits means going from $256$ to $65{,}536$ negatives. This is the entire economic argument for MoCo's queue: the exact gradient of a batch-contrastive loss needs all $N$ activations held for the backward pass, so SimCLR pays with a batch of $8192$; MoCo stores $65{,}536$ <em>feature vectors</em> with no gradient at about $32$ MB, and decouples $N$ from the batch size entirely.</p><p><strong>The 3rd is the trap, and it is the one worth internalising.</strong> The bound measures the <em>task</em>, not the encoder. A kitchen scale that reads to $100$ kg cannot tell you that something weighs $150$ kg — and that is a fact about the scale, not the object. A perfect score on a $256$-way task certifies $8$ bits whether your encoder is DINOv2 or nearly useless.</p><p><strong>The 4th is false.</strong> It is a lower bound, and a loose one in general; it is not an estimate of the mutual information.</p>",
