  "exam": 4,
  "num": 24,
  "week": "W9",
  "explanation": "<p>The adapted layer is $\\mathbf{h} = W\\mathbf{x} + \\frac{\\alpha}{r}BA\\mathbf{x}$. With $B = 0$ the second term is exactly zero, so at step $0$ the adapted model <em>is</em> the pre-trained model — bit for bit, not approximately.</p><p>That matters for a specific reason. LoRA is used when the pre-trained model is already good and the target dataset is small. Starting from a perturbed version of it would throw away part of what you are paying to keep, and with few labels there may not be enough signal to recover it. Zero initialisation makes fine-tuning a strict continuation.</p><p><strong>Why $A$ is random rather than also zero.</strong> If both were zero the gradient with respect to both would be zero as well — $\\partial(BA)/\\partial B \\propto A$ and vice versa — and the adapter would never leave the origin. One factor has to break the symmetry; the convention is to randomise $A$ and zero $B$.</p><p><strong>Why not (b).</strong> Both $A$ and $B$ are trained; only $W$ is frozen.</p><p><strong>And a related trap.</strong> $\\alpha$ and $r$ are not independent knobs: the update is scaled by $\\alpha/r$, so raising the rank while holding $\\alpha$ fixed <em>weakens</em> each update. Report both or neither.</p>",
