  "num": 31,
  "week": "W2",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: A self-attention layer costs $\\mathbf{O(n^2 d)}$ per layer with a maximum path length of $\\mathbf{O(1)}$, whereas a recurrent layer costs $\\mathbf{O(n d^2)}$ with path length $\\mathbf{O(n)}$. Self-attention is the cheaper of the two exactly when $\\mathbf{n < d}$.</strong> <strong>Where each cost comes from.</strong> Attention scores every pair of positions, which is $n^2$ of them, each costing $O(d)$. A recurrent layer visits each position once and does an $O(d^2)$ matrix–vector product there. Set $n^2d < nd^2$ and divide by $nd$: the condition is $n < d$. With $d = 512$ that covers most sentences, which is why the quadratic cost was acceptable for translation and stopped being acceptable for documents and images.</p><p><strong>Path length is the more interesting column.</strong> It is the number of steps a gradient must travel between the two most distant positions. Attention connects any two positions directly, so it is $O(1)$ and there is no exponential factor to vanish. A recurrence needs $n$ steps, which is the vanishing-gradient problem restated as a graph distance. A convolution sits between them at $O(\\log_k n)$.</p><p><strong>The trap in this table</strong> is reading $O(1)$ sequential operations as “attention is faster”. It is $O(1)$ <em>sequential</em> steps and $O(n^2d)$ <em>work</em>: massively parallelisable, not cheap.</p>",
