  "id": "e04q32",
  "exam": 4,
  "num": 32,
  "week": "W4",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: A dense voxel grid's memory grows with the <strong>cube</strong> of the resolution, so doubling $r$ costs <strong>8</strong> times as much. OctNet starts from the observation that activations concentrate <strong>near surfaces</strong>. Octree Generating Networks label each cell <strong>empty</strong>, <strong>occupied</strong> or <strong>mixed</strong>, and subdivide only the <strong>mixed</strong> ones.</strong> <strong>The cubic law is the reason the entire lecture exists.</strong> Every family of 3D method is an attempt to stop paying it. Concretely: a chair occupies $10.41\\%$ of a $32^3$ grid, $5.09\\%$ of a $64^3$ grid and $2.41\\%$ of a $128^3$ grid — as resolution rises the <em>proportion</em> of useful voxels falls, so you are paying eight times more to store a larger fraction of nothing.</p><p><strong>Why “near surfaces” and not “where the object is”.</strong> The interior of a solid object is as uninformative as the air around it; what a network needs is the boundary. That is a stronger statement than sparsity and it is what justifies a depth-3 hybrid grid-octree rather than a general adaptive tree.</p><p><strong>Why three labels and not two.</strong> With only <em>empty</em> and <em>occupied</em> there is no way to say “I cannot tell at this resolution, look closer”. <em>Mixed</em> is precisely that instruction, and subdividing only the mixed cells is what makes the cost track the surface area rather than the volume. Note that every decoder level carries its own loss, so the coarse levels are supervised rather than merely being a means to the fine ones.</p>",
