  "num": 33,
  "week": "W6",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: The Mahalanobis distance is \\[ \\mathcal{D}_M^2 = \\mathbf{(\\mathbf{x}_1-\\mathbf{x}_2)^{\\top}M(\\mathbf{x}_1-\\mathbf{x}_2)}, \\] with $M$ required to be <strong>symmetric positive semi-definite</strong>. It cannot be learned directly because <strong>a gradient step leaves that feasible set</strong>, so it is factorised as $M = \\mathbf{W^{\\top}W}$, which turns the distance into $\\mathbf{\\lVert W\\mathbf{x}_1 - W\\mathbf{x}_2\\rVert^2}$ — that is, into a <strong>Siamese</strong> network.</strong> <strong>Follow the algebra, because the architecture falls out of it rather than being chosen.</strong> Substituting $M = W^{\\top}W$: \\[ (\\mathbf{x}_1-\\mathbf{x}_2)^{\\top}W^{\\top}W(\\mathbf{x}_1-\\mathbf{x}_2) = \\bigl(W(\\mathbf{x}_1-\\mathbf{x}_2)\\bigr)^{\\top}\\bigl(W(\\mathbf{x}_1-\\mathbf{x}_2)\\bigr) = \\lVert W\\mathbf{x}_1 - W\\mathbf{x}_2\\rVert^2 . \\] The last step is linearity, and it is the whole trick: instead of a joint function of the pair, you have <em>the same map applied separately to each input</em> and then an ordinary Euclidean distance. One encoder, tied weights, applied twice — a Siamese network is what you get, not what you decided on.</p><p><strong>Why the constraint was the obstacle.</strong> Positive semi-definiteness is a condition on the whole spectrum of $M$; there is no cheap projection back onto that set after a gradient step, so unconstrained optimisation is not available. Factorising removes the problem entirely because <em>every</em> $W$ yields a legal $M$. This is the same move as 3DGS's $\\Sigma = RSS^{\\top}R^{\\top}$ (Q29) — reparametrise the constraint away rather than fight the optimiser.</p><p><strong>Why the weights must be tied.</strong> Both inputs have to land in the same space, or a distance between them means nothing. The one principled exception is cross-modal: CLIP has untied encoders because pixels and tokens have no shared parameterisation to tie.</p>",
