  "exam": 4,
  "num": 35,
  "week": "W10",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: The forward diffusion step is $q(\\mathbf{x}_k \\mid \\mathbf{x}_{k-1}) = \\mathcal{N}\\bigl(\\mathbf{\\sqrt{1-\\beta_k}\\,\\mathbf{x}_{k-1}},\\ \\mathbf{\\beta_k I}\\bigr)$. Writing $\\alpha_k = \\mathbf{1-\\beta_k}$ and $\\bar\\alpha_k = \\mathbf{\\prod_{s\\le k}\\alpha_s}$, the closed form is \\[ \\mathbf{x}_k = \\mathbf{\\sqrt{\\bar\\alpha_k}\\,\\mathbf{x}_0 + \\sqrt{1-\\bar\\alpha_k}\\,\\varepsilon}, \\] and DDPM trains by regressing $\\mathbf{\\lVert \\varepsilon - \\varepsilon_\\theta(\\mathbf{x}_k,k)\\rVert^2}$.</strong> <strong>Read the forward step as two operations, in order.</strong> Multiply by $\\sqrt{1-\\beta_k} < 1$, which shrinks the signal towards the origin; then add noise of variance $\\beta_k$. The shrink is what makes this variance-<em>preserving</em>: without it the variance would grow without bound, which is the variance-exploding alternative that NCSN uses. This is one of only two formulas the lecture marks red.</p><p><strong>Why the closed form matters so much in practice.</strong> Training needs $\\mathbf{x}_k$ for a random $k$, and simulating $k$ successive steps for every example would be ruinous. Because Gaussians compose, the whole chain collapses to a single draw — one multiply, one noise sample, whatever $k$ is. Note the coefficients satisfy $\\bar\\alpha_k + (1-\\bar\\alpha_k) = 1$ by construction, so they are always the sine and cosine of some angle; if your two numbers do not square to one you have made an arithmetic error.</p><p><strong>And why the target is $\\varepsilon$.</strong> You could regress onto $\\mathbf{x}_0$, onto $\\mathbf{x}_{k-1}$, or onto $v$ — all are equivalent up to a transformation of the same information. They differ in the loss landscape they present, not in what they can express; $\\varepsilon$ was DDPM's choice and $v$ tends to work best now.</p>",
