  "week": "W11",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: The rendered colour along a ray is $C(\\mathbf{r}) = \\int_{t_n}^{t_f} \\mathbf{T(t)}\\ \\mathbf{\\sigma(\\mathbf{r}(t))}\\ \\mathbf{\\mathbf{c}(\\mathbf{r}(t),\\mathbf{d})}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$, where $T(t) = \\mathbf{\\exp\\bigl(-\\int_{t_n}^{t}\\sigma(\\mathbf{r}(s))\\,\\mathrm{d}s\\bigr)}$ is the <strong>transmittance</strong>. Discretised, this is the standard <strong>alpha-blending</strong> formula, with $\\alpha_i = \\mathbf{1 - \\exp(-\\sigma_i\\delta_i)}$.</strong> <strong>Read the integrand as a probability statement</strong>, because that is what makes it memorable rather than a formula to be recalled. $T(t)$ is the probability that the ray gets as far as $t$ without being absorbed. $\\sigma(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ is the probability it is absorbed in the next slice, <em>given</em> that it arrived. Their product is the probability that the ray terminates right here — and that is the weight the colour at this point deserves. Integrating a colour against a probability of stopping is an expectation.</p><p><strong>Two consequences worth stating.</strong> $T$ can only fall: it is a survival probability, so empty space leaves it flat and nothing can make it rise. And a dense region contributes little if $T$ has already fallen before reaching it, which is occlusion, expressed arithmetically rather than as a special case — see Q59.</p><p><strong>$\\sigma$ takes position only; $\\mathbf{c}$ takes position and direction.</strong> That asymmetry is deliberate. Whether something is there cannot depend on where you look from; what colour it appears can, and that is how view-dependent specular highlights are represented.</p><p><strong>The same discretised line serves NeRF and 3DGS.</strong> Only the source of $\\alpha_i$ differs: NeRF gets $\\sigma_i$ from an MLP, 3DGS from a Gaussian's local density.</p>",
