  "num": 41,
  "week": "W7",
  "explanation": "<p>Two independent axes, and the table is easier to hold than four separate facts:</p><table class='xt'><tr><th></th><th><strong>single stream</strong></th><th><strong>dual stream</strong></th></tr><tr><td>region features</td><td>UNITER</td><td>ViLBERT</td></tr><tr><td>CNN grid features</td><td>Pixel-BERT</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>linear patch projection</td><td>ViLT</td><td>—</td></tr></table><p><strong>Single against dual.</strong> A single-stream model concatenates image and text tokens into one sequence and lets ordinary self-attention mix them. A dual-stream model runs a per-modality transformer first and then couples them with cross-attention layers — ViLBERT's TRM followed by Co-TRM.</p><p><strong>Read the axes in the names.</strong> ViLBERT is <em>Vision-and-Language BERT</em>; UNITER is <em>UNiversal Image-TExt Representation</em>; ViLT is <em>Vision-and-Language Transformer</em>, and the missing “BERT” is a hint that it dropped the region-feature machinery.</p><p><strong>The axis that matters for cost is the first one</strong> — see Q19.</p>",
