  "id": "e04q45",
  "exam": 4,
  "num": 45,
  "week": "W5",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Answer: $3$ components; $\\operatorname{rank}(L) = \\mathbf{5}$; $\\operatorname{trace}(L) = \\mathbf{12}$; multiplicity of $0$ is $\\mathbf{3}$</strong></p><p><strong>Components.</strong> Follow the edges: $\\{1,2,3\\}$ joined by $1\\!-\\!2$ and $2\\!-\\!3$; $\\{4,5\\}$ joined by $4\\!-\\!5$; $\\{6,7,8\\}$ joined by $6\\!-\\!7$, $7\\!-\\!8$ and $6\\!-\\!8$, which is a triangle. Three components.</p><p><strong>Degrees</strong>, in order: $1,2,1,\\ 1,1,\\ 2,2,2$. They sum to $12 = 2|\\mathcal{E}|$, which is the handshake lemma and doubles as a check that you read the edge list correctly — $|\\mathcal{E}| = 6$.</p><p><strong>Then the two spectral facts follow without computing a single eigenvalue.</strong> The multiplicity of $0$ equals the number of components, $3$; hence $\\operatorname{rank}(L) = N - k = 8 - 3 = 5$. And $\\operatorname{trace}(L) = \\sum_i \\deg(i) = 12$, since $L = D - A$ has the degrees on its diagonal and $A$ has zeros there.</p><p><strong>The trace as a cross-check.</strong> The trace is also the sum of the eigenvalues. Three of them are $0$, so the remaining five must sum to $12$ — if you do compute the spectrum and it does not, you have made an error.</p>",
  "type": "numeric",
