  "id": "e05q02",
  "exam": 5,
  "num": 2,
  "week": "W1",
  "explanation": "<p>The four differ only in which axes the statistics are taken over:</p><table class='xt'><tr><th></th><th><strong>averaged over</strong></th><th><strong>reach for it when</strong></th></tr><tr><td>BatchNorm</td><td>$(N,H,W)$, per channel</td><td>large batches, fixed-size images</td></tr><tr><td>LayerNorm</td><td>$(C,H,W)$, per sample</td><td>variable-length sequences</td></tr><tr><td>InstanceNorm</td><td>$(H,W)$, per sample <em>and</em> channel</td><td>the per-channel statistics are the signal</td></tr><tr><td>GroupNorm</td><td>$(H,W)$ and a channel group</td><td>the batch is small</td></tr></table><p><strong>Why (b) rather than merely “it is the one for images”.</strong> In a style-transfer network the mean and variance of each feature channel, within one image, largely <em>are</em> the style — that is the empirical finding the method rests on. Normalising them away per image removes the source image's style and leaves the content, which is precisely the operation you want before injecting a new style. The choice is not about batch size at all; it is that the statistic being removed is the quantity of interest.</p><p><strong>Why not (a).</strong> BatchNorm would mix statistics across the images in the batch — which is to say, across <em>styles</em>. That is the one thing you must not do here.</p><p><strong>Why not (c) or (d).</strong> Both are right answers to different questions: LayerNorm when the input length varies, GroupNorm when the batch is too small for reliable batch statistics. Neither has anything to say about style.</p>",
