  "id": "e05q03",
  "exam": 5,
  "num": 3,
  "week": "W2",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>The 1st is false, and the 4th says why.</strong> A transformer layer has exactly two mechanisms and they do not overlap. Attention is the <em>only</em> place where information crosses between positions. The feed-forward network is applied to each token's vector independently — $\\mathrm{FFN}(x) = \\mathrm{ReLU}(xW_1+b_1)W_2+b_2$, with no index running over other positions — so it computes <em>within</em> a token and cannot mix. That division is worth being able to state in one line, because a great deal of transformer variant design is just a change to one half of it.</p><p><strong>The 2nd is what “position-wise” means</strong>, and it is the same weight-sharing idea as a convolution: one $W_1, W_2$ for the whole sequence, applied everywhere. If each position had its own weights the layer could not handle a sequence longer than it was trained on.</p><p><strong>The 3rd.</strong> $d_{\\text{ff}} = 4d_{\\text{model}}$ is the standard, so $2048$ at $d = 512$ and $3072$ at $d = 768$. Note the consequence: the feed-forward network holds <em>twice</em> the parameters of the attention block ($8d^2$ against $4d^2$), so most of a transformer's weights are in the part that does not attend to anything.</p>",
