  "exam": 5,
  "num": 4,
  "week": "W2",
  "explanation": "<p>Mask two adjacent tokens of “San Francisco” and BERT predicts each from the <em>same</em> unmasked context, independently. Position one has “New” and “San” both plausible; position two has “York” and “Francisco” both plausible; nothing in the objective couples the two decisions. The factorisation is \\[ p(x_{i}, x_{j} \\mid \\tilde{\\mathbf{x}}) \\approx p(x_{i}\\mid \\tilde{\\mathbf{x}})\\; p(x_{j}\\mid \\tilde{\\mathbf{x}}), \\] and “New Francisco” has a perfectly respectable probability under it.</p><p><strong>Contrast the autoregressive factorisation</strong>, which is exact: $p(\\mathbf{x}) = \\prod_i p(x_i \\mid \\mathbf{x}_{1:i-1})$. Having generated “New”, the model conditions on it before choosing the next token, so the joint is represented properly. This is the structural advantage GPT has over BERT, and it is separate from the bidirectionality advantage that runs the other way.</p><p><strong>Why not (a).</strong> MLM is the objective that <em>is</em> parallel over the sequence — every masked position is predicted in one pass, which is exactly why BERT trains efficiently. Autoregressive <em>generation</em> is the serial one.</p><p><strong>Why not (c).</strong> Self-attention gives an $O(1)$ path between any two positions; long-range dependency is the transformer's strength, not its limitation.</p>",
