  "id": "e05q05",
  "exam": 5,
  "num": 5,
  "week": "W2",
  "explanation": "<p>BPE is a greedy compression algorithm run once over the corpus, before any training. Start with the alphabet of characters; count every adjacent pair in the corpus; merge the most frequent pair into a new symbol; repeat until the vocabulary reaches its target, around $50{,}000$.</p><p><strong>What it buys, and why neither alternative works.</strong> A word-level vocabulary (c) has a fixed list and cannot represent anything outside it — every new proper noun, typo or compound becomes <code>[UNK]</code>, and information is destroyed before the model sees it. A character-level vocabulary (d) can represent anything but makes sequences several times longer, which is expensive when attention is quadratic. BPE interpolates: frequent words end up as single tokens, rare ones decompose into pieces that are still meaningful, and nothing is ever out of vocabulary because the characters remain as a fallback.</p><p><strong>Why not (b).</strong> Whitespace splitting is a word-level scheme with all of (c)'s problems, and it also fails outright on languages that do not delimit words with spaces.</p>",
