  "num": 6,
  "week": "W3",
  "explanation": "<p>token: <span>1st</span>]</p><p><strong>The 1st is the definition.</strong> It is a single learnable vector prepended to the patch sequence. Because self-attention is all-to-all, it attends to every patch and every patch attends to it, so after $L$ layers its representation is a learned aggregate of the whole image — and that is the vector the classification head reads. Borrowed directly from BERT's <code>[CLS]</code>.</p><p><strong>The 2nd is false, and the difference is the point of having it.</strong> An average is a fixed, unweighted reduction. The <code>[class]</code> token <em>learns</em> what to pool and how to weight it, differently at every layer, and the weights depend on the image. Global average pooling over the patch tokens is a real alternative that works about as well, but it is a different operation, not a description of this one.</p><p><strong>The 3rd is false and gets the causality backwards.</strong> Self-attention is permutation equivariant on its own; adding a token does not create that property. If anything the <code>[class]</code> token is a place where the symmetry is deliberately broken, since it is a distinguished position.</p><p><strong>The 4th is false.</strong> Positional encodings are added to <em>every</em> token, the <code>[class]</code> token included (it gets its own learned one). It does not carry them on behalf of the others.</p><p>Worth remembering alongside: the famous DINO visualisation is of the <code>[class]</code> token's attention, and DINO's weakness is precisely that only this token is supervised — the patch tokens get no direct signal, which is what iBOT adds.</p>",
