  "id": "e05q08",
  "exam": 5,
  "num": 8,
  "week": "W4",
  "explanation": "<p>\\[ f(x_1,\\dots,x_n) = \\gamma \\circ g\\bigl(h(x_1),\\dots,h(x_n)\\bigr) \\] $h$ is a shared per-point MLP, $g$ is max pooling, $\\gamma$ is an MLP on the result. <strong>$f$ is permutation-invariant if and only if $g$ is symmetric</strong> — that is the 1st, and it is the whole theorem. Max, min, sum and mean all qualify; PointNet uses max.</p><p><strong>The 2nd is the segmentation trick, and it is worth seeing why it is needed.</strong> The global feature after max pooling is a single $1024$-vector for the whole cloud, which is what you want for classification and useless for labelling individual points — every point would receive the same answer. Concatenating it back onto each point's own $64$-dimensional local feature gives $N \\times (64 + 1024)$: each point now sees itself <em>and</em> the object it belongs to, which is exactly the information a per-point label needs.</p><p><strong>The 3rd is false and is PointNet's known limitation.</strong> Points are processed independently and then pooled globally; there is no intermediate scale at all. PointNet++ adds one, with farthest point sampling to choose centroids and a ball query to group their neighbours — and everything hierarchical about it exists to fix this line.</p><p><strong>The 4th.</strong> The T-Net predicts a $64\\times64$ feature transform, and without a constraint that matrix is free to become an arbitrary distortion. $\\lVert I - TT^{\\top}\\rVert_F^2$ pushes it towards orthogonal, i.e. towards a rigid motion, which is the transformation class the invariance was supposed to be about. Note the T-Net is a spatial <em>transformer</em> — it predicts a matrix and applies it — and not a form of attention.</p>",
