  "id": "e05q09",
  "exam": 5,
  "num": 9,
  "week": "W4",
  "explanation": "<p>A range view is a spherical projection from the sensor: dense, and it keeps the $z$ axis. Its defect is scale. The same car subtends nine pixels at ten metres and one at thirty, so a convolution — which has one kernel size, shared over the image — has to learn the same object again at every scale it can appear at. That is a fixed and avoidable waste of capacity.</p><p>A bird's-eye view is an <em>orthographic</em> projection onto the ground plane. Orthographic means no perspective divide, so a car occupies the same number of cells wherever it is. The metric space is preserved, which also means a box predicted in BEV is already in the units the downstream planner wants.</p><p><strong>What it costs, so the trade is visible.</strong> BEV is sparse at distance — the LiDAR returns thin out — and it collapses height, so overhanging structure is flattened. The lecture's judgement is that scale preservation matters more for detection, and that is the answer.</p><p><strong>Why not (a) or (c).</strong> Both are true of the <em>range</em> view: it is the dense one, and it is the one that keeps $z$. They are the range view's advantages, which is why the question is a real choice rather than a recital.</p>",
