  "id": "e05q15",
  "exam": 5,
  "num": 15,
  "week": "W6",
  "explanation": "<p>The four axioms are $\\mathcal{D}(x,y) = 0 \\iff x = y$; $\\mathcal{D} \\ge 0$; $\\mathcal{D}(x,y) = \\mathcal{D}(y,x)$; and $\\mathcal{D}(x,z) \\le \\mathcal{D}(x,y) + \\mathcal{D}(y,z)$.</p><p><strong>The triangle inequality is the one with consequences.</strong> It is what makes “close” behave transitively enough to be useful: if $a$ is near $b$ and $b$ is near $c$, then $a$ cannot be arbitrarily far from $c$. Without it a ranking is not consistent — you could have a set of items each of which is nearest neighbour to the next in a cycle — and a cluster has no meaning, since membership would not propagate at all. Every indexing structure that makes nearest-neighbour search sublinear also relies on it, to prune branches.</p><p><strong>Why the others are weaker.</strong> Non-negativity and symmetry are hygiene: a distance that could be negative, or that depended on the order of its arguments, would be confusing, but neither says anything about three points at once. Identity of indiscernibles is the one routinely and deliberately <em>dropped</em> — an embedding in which two photographs of the same person sit at distance $0$ is a pseudo-metric, and that is a feature, not a defect.</p>",
