  "id": "e05q16",
  "exam": 5,
  "num": 16,
  "week": "W6",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>The 3rd is the algebra everything else follows from.</strong> A softmax logit is an inner product, and an inner product in polar form is $W_j^{\\top}\\phi = \\lVert W_j\\rVert\\,\\lVert\\phi\\rVert\\cos\\theta_j$: two lengths and an angle.</p><p><strong>The 1st and 2nd are what that costs you.</strong> Cross-entropy only ever asks that the correct class's logit be the largest. It can achieve that by making $\\lVert\\phi\\rVert$ large, or by nudging $\\theta_y$ slightly below the others — <em>separable</em>. It never asks that samples of one class be <em>close together</em>, so the learned features spread into long radial spokes from the origin. The consequence is exactly the 2nd, and it is fatal for retrieval: two faces of the same person far out along the spoke can be further apart than either is from a different person's cluster near the origin. Separable is not discriminative.</p><p>That is what the fixes address. Centre loss adds $\\lambda\\sum\\lVert\\phi - \\mathbf{c}_y\\rVert^2$ and shortens the spokes, but creates no gap between classes. Angular-margin losses go further: normalise both $\\lVert W\\rVert$ and $\\lVert\\phi\\rVert$ to $1$ so the logit <em>is</em> $\\cos\\theta_j$ — the two lengths are gone and only the angle is left — then demand a margin in that angle.</p><p><strong>The 4th is false, and it is the opening argument of the whole lecture.</strong> A classifier's answer set is welded into its output layer: a new identity needs a new column in $W$ and a retraining run. Enrolling someone with one forward pass and one stored vector is what an <em>embedding</em> buys, and it is the reason similarity learning exists.</p>",
