  "id": "e05q17",
  "exam": 5,
  "num": 17,
  "week": "W7",
  "explanation": "<p>The reliable test is to <strong>count the outputs</strong>.</p><p><em>Fusion</em> produces a shared representation and <strong>one</strong> joint output. <em>Coordination</em> keeps the representations separate, produces <strong>one output per modality</strong>, and merely constrains them to be aligned. <em>Fission</em> decomposes a shared representation back per modality.</p><p>CLIP has an image encoder and a text encoder that never exchange a single activation. Each produces its own embedding; the contrastive loss compares them, pulling matched pairs together and pushing the rest apart. Two encoders, two outputs, a loss between them: coordination.</p><p><strong>Why this is worth being precise about.</strong> The property that makes CLIP useful follows directly from it. Because the encoders are independent, you can embed a million images once and store them, then answer a text query with a single text forward pass. A fusion model must run the joint network on every (image, text) pair, which is why retrieval over a large collection is impossible with one. It is also why CLIP's encoders are the one principled exception to Siamese weight tying: pixels and tokens have no shared parameterisation to tie.</p>",
