  "id": "e05q18",
  "exam": 5,
  "num": 18,
  "week": "W7",
  "explanation": "<p>\\[ \\mathrm{FiLM}(F_{i,c}) = \\gamma_{i,c}F_{i,c} + \\beta_{i,c}, \\qquad \\gamma,\\beta \\text{ predicted from the other modality.} \\] <strong>The 2nd is the definition, and the 4th is why anyone uses it.</strong> A small network reads the conditioning modality — a question, say — and emits a scale and a shift per feature channel of the other. The visual features are then modulated: channels relevant to the question are amplified, the rest suppressed. It is genuine multiplicative interaction, which puts it above additive fusion, and it costs two numbers per channel, which puts it far below bilinear or tensor fusion in price.</p><p><strong>Note where it sits on the interaction ladder.</strong> Additive fusion fixes each modality's contribution in advance. FiLM lets one modality <em>change what the other means</em>, which is the qualitative step. Attention goes further again by making the mixing weights depend on the input in a learned, position-dependent way.</p><p><strong>The 1st is false</strong> — that is early fusion, and it would require the modalities to be homogeneous, which an image and a sentence are not.</p><p><strong>The 3rd is false.</strong> Late fusion combines <em>predictions</em>, after each branch has committed. FiLM operates on intermediate feature maps, well inside the network.</p>",
