  "id": "e05q19",
  "exam": 5,
  "num": 19,
  "week": "W7",
  "explanation": "<p>Train jointly on a corpus with ten million image–text pairs and fifty thousand image–depth pairs, and the gradient is dominated by the first. The shared parameters are tuned almost entirely for the abundant modality, and the rare one ends up contributing nothing — <em>dominance</em>, one of the four outcomes, and the one that looks like success because the aggregate score is fine.</p><p><strong>Multi-stage training is the remedy the lecture names.</strong> Pre-train on the abundant data, then adapt in a later stage on the scarce modality, so that each gets a phase in which its gradient is not being drowned. The general form of this argument recurs whenever data volumes differ by orders of magnitude.</p><p><strong>Why not (b).</strong> The scarce modality is usually the one you added the second sensor for. Discarding it is giving up the reason the model is multimodal.</p><p><strong>Why not (c).</strong> Early fusion is a choice about <em>where</em> the modalities meet and makes no difference to how much data each has; if anything it worsens things, because a missing or under-trained modality corrupts the joint representation from the first layer.</p><p><strong>Why not (d).</strong> A larger learning rate on a small dataset overfits it. The problem is the number of gradient contributions, not their size, and turning up the step size trades one failure for another.</p>",
