  "exam": 5,
  "num": 20,
  "week": "W8",
  "explanation": "<p>A shortcut is a way to solve the pretext task that does <em>not</em> require understanding the image, and the three named in the lecture are worth knowing individually because each has its own fix.</p><table class='xt'><tr><th><strong>Shortcut</strong></th><th><strong>What it exploits</strong></th><th><strong>Fix</strong></th></tr><tr><td>low-level statistics</td><td>patches match by mean and variance</td><td>normalise each patch</td></tr><tr><td>edge continuity</td><td>lines run on across the true seam</td><td>jitter and pad the patches</td></tr><tr><td>chromatic aberration</td><td>colour fringing encodes <em>absolute</em> position</td><td>drop or jitter channels</td></tr></table><p><strong>The 2nd is false, and inverting it is the entire lesson.</strong> A shortcut makes the pretext accuracy <em>high</em>. That is what makes it dangerous: the training curve looks excellent, the loss is low, and the encoder has learned to measure colour fringing. There is no signal anywhere in the pretext task's own metrics that anything is wrong.</p><p>Hence the rule: <strong>pre-training is judged downstream</strong>, by linear probing or fine-tuning, never by the proxy's own score.</p><p><strong>The 4th is the strategic point.</strong> Each of these was found, and patched, by someone noticing it. There is no procedure that enumerates the remaining ones, and the search is unbounded — you are competing against a network optimising hard for any regularity you left in. That, together with the need for domain knowledge to invent good tasks in the first place, is why the field moved to one generic objective: contrastive learning, where the positive is just another augmentation of the same image.</p>",
