  "id": "e05q21",
  "exam": 5,
  "num": 21,
  "week": "W8",
  "explanation": "<p>The queue holds $65{,}536$ key vectors, computed at many different past training steps. For the InfoNCE loss to mean anything, the query's similarity to a key computed a thousand steps ago has to be comparable with its similarity to one computed just now — they are competing answers to the same multiple-choice question.</p><p><strong>If the key encoder were updated by gradient descent</strong> it would move as fast as the query encoder, and a key from a thousand steps ago would have been produced by what is effectively a different network. The comparison would be meaningless and the loss would be optimising against an inconsistent target.</p><p>The momentum update $\\theta_k \\leftarrow m\\theta_k + (1-m)\\theta_q$ with $m$ near $1$ makes the key encoder drift slowly, so the whole queue stays approximately consistent. That is the mechanism, and it is the enabling trick for the whole design.</p><p><strong>Why not (a).</strong> The memory saving comes from the <em>queue</em> storing feature vectors rather than activations — $65{,}536 \\times 128 \\times 4$ bytes is about $32$ MB, against SimCLR holding $8192$ examples' worth of backward graph. That is a separate point from why the encoder is momentum-updated.</p><p><strong>Why not (b).</strong> They are perfectly well defined; they are deliberately not taken, via a stop-gradient. This is the same pattern as DINO's EMA teacher — a slowly-moving target that does not receive gradient, for the same family of reasons.</p>",
