  "id": "e05q22",
  "exam": 5,
  "num": 22,
  "week": "W8",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>1st — the rule, and Q20 is why it exists.</strong> The proxy head is discarded; only the encoder was ever the point; and a high proxy score may mean a shortcut was found. The only measurement that counts is how the encoder performs on a task you actually care about.</p><p><strong>2nd and 3rd — and they answer genuinely different questions.</strong> Linear probing freezes the encoder and trains a single linear layer on top. If that works, the information is not merely present but <em>linearly separable</em> in the representation — a strong statement about its structure. Fine-tuning updates everything, and asks the weaker but more practical question of whether these weights are a good place to start. An encoder can be excellent by the second measure and mediocre by the first.</p><p><strong>4th — and this is the practical warning.</strong> Full fine-tuning is the default people reach for, and with a few hundred labels it overfits and destroys exactly the general features you paid a large pre-training run to obtain. The sensible order is: probe first, and escalate only if the probe is clearly capacity-limited. The six adaptation strategies — linear probing, non-linear probing, adapters, prompt tuning, full fine-tuning, and prompting or in-context learning — are ordered roughly by how much data each needs.</p>",
