  "id": "e05q23",
  "exam": 5,
  "num": 23,
  "week": "W9",
  "explanation": "<p>Identity forwards, $\\times(-\\lambda)$ backwards. It is a layer that does nothing to the data and inverts the gradient passing through it.</p><p><strong>Why such a strange object is the neat solution.</strong> You want two things at once: a domain classifier that is as good as possible, and an encoder that makes the domain classifier as bad as possible. That is a minimax problem, \\[ \\min_\\theta \\max_\\phi \\bigl(\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{task}} - \\lambda\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{domain}}\\bigr), \\] which normally means alternating two optimisers. Insert a GRL between the encoder and the domain classifier and the forward loss is a plain <em>sum</em>, $\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{task}} + \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{domain}}$, minimised by one ordinary optimiser in one pass. The classifier's own weights sit downstream of the layer and receive an ordinary gradient, so they minimise $\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{domain}}$. The encoder sits upstream and receives the negated one, so it maximises it. The minus sign lives in the layer rather than in the training loop.</p><p><strong>Why not (c).</strong> Negating in the forward pass too would change the features the classifier sees and break the arrangement entirely — the asymmetry <em>is</em> the mechanism.</p><p><strong>Why not (b).</strong> The classifier is trained normally; it is the encoder whose gradient is flipped, and getting this direction the wrong way round is the standard error here.</p>",
