  "id": "e05q24",
  "exam": 5,
  "num": 24,
  "week": "W9",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>The 1st states the problem.</strong> Train $G: X_S \\to X_T$ against a discriminator and the discriminator asks one question: does this look like a target-domain image? It never asks whether it looks like <em>this particular</em> source image. A generator that ignores its input entirely and produces one convincing target-domain image every time satisfies that objective completely. Nothing in a plain GAN loss ties output to input.</p><p><strong>The 4th states the fix.</strong> Require $F(G(x_S)) \\approx x_S$ and $G(F(x_T)) \\approx x_T$: a translation should be reversible. Information the generator discards cannot be restored by the inverse, so the cycle loss pressures it to preserve content while changing style.</p><p><strong>The 3rd is false, and it is the reason cycle consistency is interesting at all.</strong> It works with <em>unpaired</em> data — a pile of GTA5 frames and a pile of Cityscapes frames, with no correspondence between them. Paired data would make the whole problem a supervised regression.</p><p><strong>The 2nd is false, and the distinction is a safety argument rather than a technicality.</strong> Cycle consistency <em>discourages</em> hallucination; it does not forbid it. Deleting a distant pedestrian costs an $L_1$ reconstruction penalty proportional to a handful of pixels — negligible against the adversarial term — and if the reverse mapping can plausibly put something back, the cycle closes anyway. For an autonomous-driving pipeline that is exactly the failure you cannot tolerate, and it is why sim2real work does not treat the guarantee as absolute.</p>",
