  "id": "e05q25",
  "exam": 5,
  "num": 25,
  "week": "W9",
  "explanation": "<p>Transfer learning is a bet that the source task's knowledge is relevant. Negative transfer is that bet losing: the pre-trained initialisation leaves you <em>worse off</em> than random initialisation would have.</p><p><strong>Why it happens.</strong> The source features encode regularities that hold in the source domain and not the target one, and fine-tuning starts in a basin shaped by them. With a small target dataset there is not enough signal to climb back out, so the model inherits the wrong inductive bias and keeps it. This is the mechanism behind the lecture's worked pair: medical images to a rare disease transfers well because both task and domain are close; social media photographs to satellite imagery does not, because the domain similarity is low — and there the target dataset was already large enough not to need the help.</p><p><strong>Why not (a).</strong> A target task can be intrinsically harder and score lower with transfer having helped a great deal. The comparison that defines negative transfer is against <em>not transferring</em>, not against the source.</p><p><strong>Why not (c).</strong> That is catastrophic forgetting, which is about the <em>source</em> task's performance after adaptation. Negative transfer is about the target's. They are different failures and both are on the list of four — alongside dataset bias and lost generalisation.</p>",
