  "id": "e05q26",
  "exam": 5,
  "num": 26,
  "week": "W10",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>The 1st is false and the direction matters.</strong> Annealing runs $\\sigma_L \\to \\sigma_1$, <em>large to small</em>. The reason is the whole motivation for NCSN: a learned score is only accurate where training data was, and sampling begins in a low-density region where it is worst. At a large $\\sigma$ the noised distribution is spread out, so the score is trustworthy almost everywhere — but it describes a heavily blurred version of the data. Descending the noise levels sharpens the target gradually, and the score is only ever consulted where it was trained. Starting small would mean trusting a score in exactly the region it knows nothing about.</p><p><strong>The 2nd is what makes it one procedure rather than several.</strong> The state carries over: the final sample at $\\sigma_i$ initialises the chain at $\\sigma_{i-1}$. Without that each level would restart from noise and the work would be thrown away.</p><p><strong>The 3rd.</strong> $\\alpha_i = \\delta\\,\\sigma_i^2/\\sigma_L^2$, so the step shrinks with the square of the noise scale — halve $\\sigma$ and the step drops to a quarter. That is the right scaling because the distribution at a small $\\sigma$ has finer structure; a step sized for the blurred distribution would step straight over it.</p><p><strong>The 4th is false and is the practical trick.</strong> One network, conditioned on the noise level: $s_\\theta(\\mathbf{x}, \\sigma)$, with $\\sigma$ supplied through a sinusoidal embedding exactly as a transformer takes position. Training a separate model per level would be unaffordable and would share nothing between levels that are obviously related.</p>",
