  "num": 31,
  "week": "W1",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: An RNN's state update is $a^{(t)} = \\mathbf{b + Wh^{(t-1)} + Ux^{(t)}}$ and $h^{(t)} = \\mathbf{\\tanh(a^{(t)})}$. Unrolling puts the product of $\\mathbf{\\partial h^{(k)}/\\partial h^{(k-1)}}$ into the gradient, which behaves like $\\mathbf{\\gamma^{t}}$. A factor below $1$ gives <strong>vanishing gradients</strong> and a factor above $1$ gives <strong>exploding gradients</strong>; the standard remedy for the second is <strong>gradient clipping</strong>.</strong> <strong>Why the same matrix appears once per step, and why that is the whole problem.</strong> Backpropagation through time differentiates a composition of $t$ copies of the <em>same</em> function, so the chain rule produces \\[ \\prod_{k=1}^{t}\\frac{\\partial h^{(k)}}{\\partial h^{(k-1)}} = \\prod_{k=1}^{t}\\operatorname{diag}\\bigl(\\tanh'(a^{(k)})\\bigr)W \\;\\sim\\; \\gamma^{t}. \\] A product of $t$ near-identical factors is an exponential in $t$. There is no middle ground: unless $\\gamma$ sits almost exactly at $1$, the gradient either dies or blows up, and it does so at a rate that makes the difference between $t=10$ and $t=100$ absolute.</p><p><strong>Why $\\tanh$ and not something unbounded.</strong> It keeps the state in $[-1,1]$, so the forward pass stays stable however long the sequence. The cost is $\\tanh' \\le 1$, with equality only at $0$ — so the diagonal factor is a contraction almost everywhere, and vanishing is the common case.</p><p><strong>Why the two failures need different remedies, which is the trap.</strong> Exploding gradients are a <em>scale</em> problem: the direction is informative and only the magnitude is wrong, so rescaling the gradient to a fixed norm fixes it completely and costs nothing. Vanishing gradients are an <em>information</em> problem: the signal from step $t-100$ has been multiplied into numerical nothing, and there is no scaling that recovers what is no longer there. That is why the cure is architectural — LSTM and GRU gating provide an additive path along which the state can travel without being multiplied by $W$ at every step. Truncated BPTT is a third thing again, a memory remedy, not a gradient one.</p>",
