  "week": "W5",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: With $\\tilde A = \\mathbf{A + I}$ and $\\tilde D$ the degree matrix of <strong>$\\tilde A$</strong>, the GCN layer is \\[ H^{(l+1)} = \\mathbf{\\sigma\\bigl(\\tilde D^{-1/2}\\tilde A\\tilde D^{-1/2}H^{(l)}W^{(l)}\\bigr)}. \\] $L$ layers give an <strong>$L$-hop</strong> receptive field, and typical depth is <strong>2–3 layers</strong> because of <strong>over-smoothing</strong>.</strong> <strong>Read the layer as three steps.</strong> Select the neighbourhood, including the node itself ($\\tilde A = A+I$); propagate, scaled by degree ($\\tilde D^{-1/2}\\tilde A\\tilde D^{-1/2}$); aggregate and apply the non-linearity. Per node: \\[ \\mathbf{h}^{(l+1)}_i = \\sigma\\!\\left(\\sum_{j\\in\\mathcal{N}(i)\\cup\\{i\\}} \\frac{\\mathbf{h}^{(l)}_j W^{(l)}}{\\sqrt{\\tilde D_{ii}\\tilde D_{jj}}}\\right). \\] <strong>Why the self-loop is not optional.</strong> Without $I$, a node's new representation is built <em>entirely</em> from its neighbours and it loses itself — $AX$ has no term for $\\mathbf{x}_i$. That is plainly wrong for node classification, where the node's own features are usually the strongest evidence. And $\\tilde D$ must be built from $\\tilde A$, not from $A$; see Q11.</p><p><strong>Why depth is the tension of the whole lecture.</strong> Each layer extends the receptive field by one hop, so reaching a node three edges away requires three layers. Each layer also averages a node with its neighbours, and repeated averaging converges — under symmetric normalisation towards something proportional to $\\sqrt{\\deg+1}$, so the representations end up encoding degree and nothing else. Depth is simultaneously the only source of range and the cause of collapse, which is why GCNs are two or three layers deep where a CNN is fifty.</p>",
