  "num": 34,
  "week": "W7",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: Additive fusion is $\\mathbf{z} = \\mathbf{w_1\\mathbf{x}_A + w_2\\mathbf{x}_B}$; element-wise fusion is $\\mathbf{\\mathbf{w}(\\mathbf{x}_A \\odot \\mathbf{x}_B)}$, in which feature $i$ meets <strong>feature $i$</strong>. Bilinear fusion is $\\mathbf{W(\\mathbf{x}_A^{\\top}\\!\\cdot\\mathbf{x}_B)}$, in which <strong>every feature meets every feature</strong>. Concatenating two vectors and applying one linear layer is secretly <strong>additive</strong> fusion.</strong> <strong>The ladder is ordered by how much interaction each rung permits</strong>, and each step up costs parameters.</p><p><em>Additive</em> lets each modality vote with a fixed weight. It can never express “what this image means depends on the caption”, because the contributions are decided in advance.</p><p><em>Element-wise</em> multiplies, so one modality can gate the other — but only channel by channel, feature $i$ against feature $i$. That presumes the two feature spaces are aligned index by index, which is a strong and usually unearned assumption.</p><p><em>Bilinear</em> forms the full outer product, so every pair interacts. Expressive, and quadratic in the dimensions.</p><p><em>Tensor</em> fusion appends a $1$ to each vector first, which recovers the unimodal terms and a bias alongside the bimodal ones.</p><p><strong>The last blank is the one that catches people, and it is worth verifying rather than believing.</strong> Concatenate and apply $W$: \\[ W\\begin{bmatrix}\\mathbf{x}_A\\\\\\mathbf{x}_B\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}W_A & W_B\\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix}\\mathbf{x}_A\\\\\\mathbf{x}_B\\end{bmatrix} = W_A\\mathbf{x}_A + W_B\\mathbf{x}_B . \\] It splits. “Concatenate and put an MLP on top” feels like fusion and is additive fusion with learned weights — the first <em>non-linearity</em> does mix them, but the fusion operation itself does not. If you want interaction you have to multiply somewhere.</p>",
