  "id": "e05q35",
  "exam": 5,
  "num": 35,
  "week": "W9",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: LoRA writes $W + \\Delta W \\approx \\mathbf{W + BA}$, with $B \\in \\mathbf{\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times r}}$ and $A \\in \\mathbf{\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d}}$. The layer computes $\\mathbf{h} = \\mathbf{W\\mathbf{x} + \\tfrac{\\alpha}{r}BA\\mathbf{x}}$. It is initialised with $A$ <strong>random</strong> and $B$ <strong>zero</strong>, so that $\\Delta W$ is <strong>exactly zero</strong> at step $0$; the compression ratio is <strong>$d/2r$</strong>.</strong> <strong>The hypothesis, stated plainly.</strong> Fine-tuning <em>is</em> the addition of some $\\Delta W$. LoRA assumes that for a well pre-trained model this update has low intrinsic rank — that adapting to a new task moves the weights within a low-dimensional subspace rather than everywhere. If so, $\\Delta W = BA$ with $r \\ll d$ loses little, and costs $2dr$ instead of $d^2$.</p><p><strong>Why there is no inference penalty.</strong> $BA$ is a $d\\times d$ matrix once multiplied out, so after training you can fold it in: $W' = W + \\frac{\\alpha}{r}BA$, and deploy a single weight matrix. That is a real advantage over adapters, which insert extra layers and therefore extra latency at every forward pass. It also means task switching costs only the swapping of a small pair of matrices.</p><p><strong>Where it is applied</strong>, in practice: the projection matrices of the transformer's self-attention layers.</p><p><strong>Two traps, both about $\\alpha$ and $r$.</strong> They are not independent — the update is scaled by $\\alpha/r$, so raising $r$ while holding $\\alpha$ fixed makes each update <em>weaker</em>, and a rank sweep that does not adjust $\\alpha$ is measuring two things at once. And $d/2r$ is a ratio of <em>trainable parameters</em>, not of memory; see Q54 of Exam 04 for why the VRAM saving is about $4\\times$ rather than $d/2r$.</p>",
