  "week": "W10",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: Classifier-<em>based</em> guidance uses Bayes to write $s(\\mathbf{x}\\mid\\mathbf{c}) = \\mathbf{s(\\mathbf{x})} + \\mathbf{\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}}\\log p(\\mathbf{c}\\mid\\mathbf{x})}$. Classifier-<em>free</em> guidance instead computes $s_\\gamma(\\mathbf{x}\\mid\\mathbf{c}) = \\mathbf{(1-\\gamma)s(\\mathbf{x}) + \\gamma\\,s(\\mathbf{x}\\mid\\mathbf{c})}$, which for $\\gamma>1$ is <strong>extrapolation</strong> rather than interpolation, at the cost of <strong>two</strong> network evaluations per step.</strong> <strong>Where the first line comes from.</strong> Take logs of $p(\\mathbf{x}\\mid\\mathbf{c}) \\propto p(\\mathbf{x})p(\\mathbf{c}\\mid\\mathbf{x})$ and differentiate with respect to $\\mathbf{x}$; the normalising constant is independent of $\\mathbf{x}$ and disappears, as always. So a conditional score is an unconditional score plus the gradient of a classifier — which is a genuinely elegant result, and it is why the technique was tried first.</p><p><strong>Why it was abandoned anyway.</strong> That classifier has to be trained on <em>noisy</em> inputs, at every noise level the sampler will visit, because that is what it will be evaluated on. And its class list fixes what you can condition on, which rules out free text. Both restrictions are structural, not engineering problems.</p><p><strong>What classifier-free guidance does instead.</strong> Train one network with the conditioning sometimes replaced by a learned null token $\\mathbf{c}_\\varnothing$, so the same weights provide both $s(\\mathbf{x}\\mid\\mathbf{c})$ and $s(\\mathbf{x})$. Then form the combination above. No classifier, any conditioning signal, and $\\gamma$ is a test-time knob that needs no retraining. The price is two forward passes per sampling step — which is cheaper overall than training and running a separate noisy classifier, but is <em>not</em> cheaper per step, and that distinction is a standard exam trap.</p>",
