  "id": "e05q38",
  "exam": 5,
  "num": 38,
  "week": "W4",
  "explanation": "<p>Sample, group, encode, pool — PointNet++ in four words. It is a PointNet applied to a <em>neighbourhood</em> instead of to the whole cloud, and repeating it is what gives the hierarchy PointNet lacked.</p><p><strong>Sampling first, by farthest point sampling, not at random.</strong> Random sampling draws in proportion to density, so a LiDAR scan yields nearly all its centroids from the near field. FPS spreads them by geometry. It costs $O(NS)$ against $O(1)$, and the coverage is worth it.</p><p><strong>Grouping by a ball query, not by $k$NN.</strong> A fixed radius gives a fixed <em>physical</em> scale, so “the neighbourhood” means the same thing in a dense region and a sparse one. $k$NN would silently expand to metres in sparse regions and shrink to centimetres in dense ones — the network would be looking at a different-sized object depending on where it stood.</p><p><strong>Then the shared MLP per point, then the max pool per group.</strong> This pair is exactly PointNet's recipe, applied within the group: a shared map keeps it equivariant, a symmetric reduction makes the group's output invariant to the order its members were listed in. Reversing them — pooling before the MLP — would destroy the per-point information before it had been used.</p><p>Where the variants sit: MSG concatenates several radii at the same layer, MRG combines several abstraction levels, and input point dropout during training is what makes the result robust to sampling density.</p>",
  "type": "order",
  "stem": "Order one PointNet++ set-abstraction layer:",
  "items": [
   "group the neighbours of each centroid with a ball query",
   "choose the centroids by farthest point sampling",
   "max pool within each group",
   "apply a shared MLP to every point of every group"
  ],
