  "id": "e05q40",
  "exam": 5,
  "num": 40,
  "week": "W11",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>The ray comes from the pose, and this is the step that needs the intrinsics</strong> (Q29): $\\mathbf{d}_{\\text{cam}} = ((u-c_x)/f,\\ (v-c_y)/f,\\ 1)$, then rotate into the world. The ray is $\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\mathbf{o} + t\\mathbf{d}$.</p><p><strong>Then sample along it, jittered.</strong> Divide $[t_n,t_f]$ into $N$ bins and draw one sample uniformly inside each. The jitter is not a numerical nicety: with fixed depths the network only ever sees the same $N$ positions along each ray and can become a lookup table at those positions, leaving the field between them unconstrained. Random offsets force it to be a genuinely <em>continuous</em> function of position, which is what you are actually training.</p><p><strong>Query the MLP for $(\\mathbf{c},\\sigma)$ at each sample.</strong> Note the asymmetry: $\\sigma$ depends on position only, $\\mathbf{c}$ on position and view direction. Whether something is there cannot depend on where you look from; what colour it appears can.</p><p><strong>Then composite.</strong> $\\hat C = \\sum_i T_i\\alpha_i\\mathbf{c}_i$ with $T_i = \\prod_{j<i}(1-\\alpha_j)$ and $\\alpha_i = 1 - \\exp(-\\sigma_i\\delta_i)$.</p><p>The supervision is <strong>only</strong> the pixel colour — there is no 3D ground truth anywhere in the loop. Geometry emerges because a single density field has to explain every training view at once, and multi-view consistency is a strong enough constraint to pin it down.</p>",
