  "id": "e05q41",
  "exam": 5,
  "num": 41,
  "week": "W4",
  "explanation": "<table class='xt'><tr><th><strong>Family</strong></th><th><strong>Methods</strong></th><th><strong>Free space?</strong></th><th><strong>Quantised?</strong></th></tr><tr><td>dense voxel</td><td>VoxNet, ScanNet, ScanComplete</td><td>51</td><td>51</td></tr><tr><td>hierarchical</td><td>OctNet, OGN</td><td>51</td><td>51</td></tr><tr><td>sparse</td><td>SC/SSC, Minkowski Engine</td><td>partial</td><td>51</td></tr><tr><td>point-based</td><td>PointNet, PointNet++</td><td>55</td><td>55</td></tr></table><p><strong>The two columns on the right are the whole taxonomy in miniature</strong>, and they explain why no family wins. Representing free space — knowing that a region was observed and is empty — is exactly what completion and mapping need, and it is what point clouds cannot do: a point cloud says where surfaces are and is silent about everywhere else. Quantisation is the opposite ledger: grids snap coordinates and lose sub-voxel precision, points do not.</p><p><strong>Read the list as a chain of failures rather than four options.</strong> Dense grids are simple and cubic; hierarchies make empty space cheap but not free; sparse convolutions make it free and keep quantisation; point-based methods drop the grid and lose free space and accuracy; projection sidesteps 3D and loses geometry. See Q51.</p>",
