  "id": "e05q42",
  "exam": 5,
  "num": 42,
  "week": "W10",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>All four exist because of one obstacle: normalisation.</strong> A network cannot simply output $p_\\theta(\\mathbf{x})$, because forcing it to integrate to one over all of data space is intractable. Each family is a different way around that, and the taxonomy is really a list of evasions.</p><p><strong>Autoregressive</strong> factorises: $p(\\mathbf{x}) = \\prod_i p(x_i\\mid\\mathbf{x}_{1:i-1})$. Each conditional is over a single variable, often a categorical one, so <em>that</em> normalisation is a softmax and is trivial. The price is sequential generation.</p><p><strong>Latent variable</strong> pushes the difficulty into a variable you never observe: sample $\\mathbf{z}$ from a fixed prior, then $\\mathbf{x}$ from $p(\\mathbf{x}\\mid\\mathbf{z})$. The prior is chosen, so it needs no normalising, and $h \\ll d$ gives a compressed bottleneck.</p><p><strong>Flow-based</strong> makes the map <em>deterministic and invertible</em>, with $h = d$, so the change-of-variables formula gives the exact density from the prior and a Jacobian determinant. The constraint that every layer be invertible with a tractable determinant is severe.</p><p><strong>Energy-based</strong> writes $p = \\frac1Z e^{-\\beta E}$: the exponential guarantees positivity and $Z$ guarantees normalisation — and $Z$ is exactly the intractable object. This is the family the score sidesteps entirely, by differentiating $\\log p$ with respect to $\\mathbf{x}$ so that $\\nabla\\log Z = 0$.</p><p><strong>The distinction worth keeping</strong> is flow against latent-variable: a flow <em>transports</em> a sample deterministically, a latent-variable model <em>samples</em> from a conditional. Same diagram, different arrow.</p>",
