  "id": "e06q06",
  "exam": 6,
  "num": 6,
  "week": "W2",
  "explanation": "<p>$O(n^2) \\to O(n\\sqrt{n})$.</p><p><strong>Where the $\\sqrt n$ comes from.</strong> Full attention lets every position reach every other in one hop, at $n^2$ pairs. Factorise it instead: give each position about $\\sqrt n$ connections in one pattern (say, a local window) and about $\\sqrt n$ in a second (say, a strided one), so that any two positions are joined by a path of length two. Each position now scores $O(\\sqrt n)$ others rather than $O(n)$, and the total is $n\\sqrt{n}$. You have traded one hop for two, and paid $\\sqrt n$ instead of $n$ for it.</p><p>The lecture names three ways of combining the two patterns: interleave them across layers, merge them into one head, or give different heads different patterns.</p><p><strong>Why not (a) or (b).</strong> $O(n)$ and $O(n\\log n)$ are real complexities in this literature — linear attention and the log-depth hierarchies — but they are other methods. Longformer's local-plus-global pattern is the one the lecture names as linear.</p>",
  "type": "single",
