  "exam": 6,
  "num": 8,
  "week": "W3",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>The 2nd is the method, in four steps:</strong> serialise the points along a space-filling curve; cut the resulting sequence into windows; run windowed attention; shuffle the ordering and repeat. Once the cloud is a sequence, the machinery is ordinary transformer machinery.</p><p><strong>The 1st is false, and reversing it is the paper's actual claim.</strong> PTv3 gives up geometric exactness — a window of a serialised sequence is a cruder neighbourhood than a true kNN ball — and gets efficiency and scale in return, and wins. <em>Simplicity, efficiency and scale often beat correctness</em>: the recurring theme of the whole lecture, and the same argument as Swin's windows against global attention.</p><p><strong>The 3rd is false and has the two curves the wrong way round.</strong> Z-order (Morton) is cheaper to compute; the <strong>Hilbert</strong> curve preserves locality better, because consecutive points on it are always spatially adjacent while Z-order occasionally makes a long jump. The trade-off is exactly that, and shuffling between serialisations across layers is what limits the damage either way.</p><p><strong>The 4th is false.</strong> Positional information arrives through xCPE, a sparse convolution acting as an efficient relative positional encoding.</p>",
