  "exam": 6,
  "num": 9,
  "week": "W3",
  "explanation": "<p>A ResNet-50 run to its last stage returns a map at $1/32$ of the input resolution with $2048$ channels. A $1\\times1$ convolution then projects those $2048$ down to $256$, which is $d_{\\text{model}}$ for the transformer.</p><p><strong>Why both numbers matter.</strong> The $\\div 32$ is what makes the sequence short enough to attend over at all: a $800\\times1066$ image becomes about $25\\times34 = 850$ tokens, and attention is quadratic in that. At full resolution it would be $850{,}000$ tokens and hopeless. The $2048 \\to 256$ is the same $1\\times1$ trick as Q3 — a channel bottleneck, here because attention costs $O(n^2 d)$ and $d = 2048$ would be eight times the price for no benefit.</p><p><strong>What it costs.</strong> A $\\div 32$ map is coarse, and DETR is known to be weak on small objects for precisely this reason; Deformable DETR's answer is to attend over several feature levels instead.</p>",
