  "id": "e06q18",
  "exam": 6,
  "num": 18,
  "week": "W7",
  "explanation": "<p>Play the audio of “ba” over video of lips saying “ga” and a listener hears “da”. Not the audio, not the video, and not a blend — a third syllable, present in neither input.</p><p><strong>Why that is an argument about architecture.</strong> Any fusion of the form $w_1 x_A + w_2 x_B$ produces an output in the span of its inputs. If neither input carries “da”, no weighting of them does. Producing it requires a <em>joint</em> computation in which one modality changes how the other is interpreted — multiplication, gating, attention — and that is the dividing line between additive fusion and everything above it.</p><p><strong>Where it sits in the four outcomes.</strong> Inconclusive, dominated, modulated, and <strong>emergent</strong>. McGurk is emergent, and emergent is the only one that justifies the cost of a multimodal model: information derivable from neither modality alone. If the outcome is dominated, you have paid for aligned paired data — the real bottleneck — and could have used the strong modality by itself.</p><p><strong>Why not (a) or (c).</strong> The effect is not about which modality wins; it is about neither winning. <strong>Why not (b).</strong> Late fusion combines two committed predictions and is the architecture <em>least</em> able to produce a third answer.</p>",
