  "id": "e06q20",
  "exam": 6,
  "num": 20,
  "week": "W7",
  "explanation": "<p>Queries from one modality, keys and values from the other. Read it as a question and an answer: the query says <em>what am I looking for</em>, and it is asked of the other modality's content.</p><p><strong>Why that asymmetry is the whole mechanism.</strong> A caption token asking “which image region am I about?” produces $Q$ from the text and $K, V$ from the image, and the output is a text-shaped vector informed by the image. Swap the roles and you get image-shaped vectors informed by the text, which is a different and equally useful layer — ViLBERT's co-attention runs both, which is why it is dual-stream.</p><p><strong>The comparison that makes it stick.</strong> Self-attention takes $Q$, $K$ and $V$ from the same sequence. Cross-attention changes exactly one thing: where $K$ and $V$ come from. It is the same operation as the original transformer decoder's second sub-layer, which takes $Q$ from the decoder and $K, V$ from the encoder — so multimodal cross-attention is not a new mechanism, it is that one applied across modalities instead of across the two halves of a translation model.</p><p><strong>Why not (d).</strong> Concatenating first is the single-stream design, in which ordinary self-attention over the joint sequence does the mixing. That is UNITER and ViLT; it is a real alternative, and it is not what cross-attention means.</p>",
