  "id": "e06q21",
  "exam": 6,
  "num": 21,
  "week": "W8",
  "explanation": "<p>The encoder produces $h = f(x)$; a small MLP produces $z = g(h)$; the contrastive loss is applied to $z$; and $h$ is what you keep. All four statements are that arrangement, and the fourth is why it exists.</p><p><strong>The argument, which is the part worth understanding.</strong> The contrastive objective is invariance to the augmentations: it asks that two crops of one image land in the same place. Optimising it directly on $h$ would push $h$ to <em>discard</em> everything the augmentations change — colour, orientation, scale. Some of that is exactly what a downstream task wants; a colour-jitter-invariant representation is a poor starting point for classifying flowers by colour.</p><p>Interposing $g$ gives the loss somewhere else to do its discarding. $z$ becomes the invariant, compressed thing the objective demands, and $h$ — one layer upstream — is allowed to retain information that $g$ then throws away. The measurement bears it out: linear probes on $h$ beat probes on $z$ by a wide margin, which is the empirical form of the 3rd and 4th.</p><p><strong>The general pattern.</strong> Every self-supervised method keeps only the encoder and discards the head: the jigsaw classifier, the MAE decoder, the DINO projection. The head is scaffolding for the objective, not part of the answer.</p>",
