  "id": "e06q22",
  "exam": 6,
  "num": 22,
  "week": "W8",
  "explanation": "<p>Both mask patches. <strong>MAE reconstructs pixels</strong>, with an $L_2$ loss on the masked patches. <strong>iBOT predicts features</strong> — specifically, the teacher network's output on the masked patches.</p><p><strong>Why the target matters more than it sounds.</strong> Pixels contain a great deal that is not semantic: sensor noise, exact texture, the precise shade of a shadow. A model spending capacity to reproduce them is doing work no downstream task will reward. A feature target has already been abstracted, so the objective is closer to the thing you actually want. The cost is that the target is now a moving one produced by a network you are also training, which is why iBOT inherits DINO's whole apparatus — EMA teacher, stop-gradient, centering and sharpening — and why MAE, whose target is fixed, is so much simpler.</p><p><strong>Why not (b), which is the near miss.</strong> iBOT is DINO plus masked image modelling, so <em>iBOT</em> is the one with the teacher; MAE has no teacher at all, only an encoder and a throwaway decoder. The option has the two the wrong way round, as does (c).</p><p><strong>Where this leads.</strong> DINO supervises only the global <code>[CLS]</code> token, so the patch tokens receive no direct signal — that is the gap iBOT fills, and DINOv2 is iBOT engineered and scaled to $142$M curated images.</p>",
