  "week": "W8",
  "explanation": "<p>$\\tau$ divides the similarities before the softmax, so it is an inverse sharpness: $p_+ = \\sigma\\bigl((\\cos_+ - \\cos_-)/\\tau\\bigr)$ in the two-candidate case.</p><p><strong>The 1st is false, and it is the one people get backwards.</strong> <em>Small</em> $\\tau$ sharpens — dividing by $0.05$ magnifies the gaps, so the softmax concentrates on the closest candidates. Large $\\tau$ flattens towards uniform. The lecture's sweep at a gap of $0.05$ makes it concrete: $p_+ = 0.731 / 0.623 / 0.525 / 0.506$ at $\\tau = 0.05 / 0.1 / 0.5 / 2.0$.</p><p><strong>The 3rd is what that buys, and it is a nice way to see it.</strong> With a small $\\tau$ the loss is dominated by the negatives nearest the anchor, because they are the only ones with appreciable weight in the denominator. That is hard-negative mining — achieved not by selecting anything, but by a temperature. It is soft, automatic and needs no search, which is part of why contrastive learning displaced explicit mining.</p><p><strong>The 4th.</strong> $0.07$ to $0.1$ are the values in the papers.</p><p><strong>The 2nd is false.</strong> One scalar, shared by every pair in the batch.</p>",
