  "id": "e06q25",
  "exam": 6,
  "num": 25,
  "week": "W9",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>The 2nd is false, and inverting it is the point of the question.</strong> With very limited target data you want <em>fewer</em> trainable parameters, not more — so you freeze the backbone and train a head. Fine-tuning is for when the target dataset is large enough to support the capacity you are unlocking.</p><p><strong>The 3rd is the reason, stated as a principle.</strong> Unfreezing a layer adds capacity, and capacity without data overfits. Worse than ordinary overfitting: the general features you paid a large pre-training run for are destroyed in the process, so you end up below where a frozen backbone would have left you.</p><p><strong>The 1st and 4th are the shared frame.</strong> Both are step two of transfer learning; both begin from the same pre-trained model; they differ only in how much of it is allowed to move. It is a continuum rather than a binary, and the intermediate options are the useful ones — unfreeze the last block, or use adapters or LoRA, which add a little capacity without touching the original weights.</p><p>This is the same judgement as Week 8's “probe first, escalate only if the probe is clearly capacity-limited”. Two lectures, one decision rule.</p>",
