  "id": "e06q26",
  "exam": 6,
  "num": 26,
  "week": "W10",
  "explanation": "<p>A probability density has to be non-negative and integrate to one over all of $\\mathbb{R}^d$. Non-negativity is easy — exponentiate. Normalisation is not: it requires $Z = \\int e^{-\\beta E(\\mathbf{x})}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{x}$, an integral over the whole of data space, and for anything but a toy model there is no way to compute it.</p><p><strong>This one obstacle organises the entire lecture.</strong> Every family is a different way around it:</p><table class='xt'><tr><td>autoregressive</td><td>factorise into conditionals, each normalised by a softmax</td></tr><tr><td>latent variable</td><td>push it into a variable you never observe, prior chosen</td></tr><tr><td>flow-based</td><td>make the map invertible, so change-of-variables gives the density</td></tr><tr><td>energy-based</td><td>accept $Z$ and be unable to evaluate it</td></tr></table><p><strong>And it is why the score exists.</strong> $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}}\\log p$ deletes $Z$ exactly, since $Z$ is a number and $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}}\\log Z = 0$. Not approximated — absent. What is left is an unconstrained vector field a network can regress onto, and everything in the second half of the lecture follows from that.</p><p><strong>Why not (b).</strong> Nothing here is about gradients vanishing. <strong>Why not (d).</strong> A network outputs whatever the last activation allows; positivity is the easy half.</p>",
