  "id": "e06q27",
  "exam": 6,
  "num": 27,
  "week": "W10",
  "explanation": "<p>A continuous normalising flow is $\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{z}_t/\\mathrm{d}t = v_t(\\mathbf{z}_t)$: a learned vector field that transports noise into data. The obstacle was always <em>training</em> it — classically you had to solve the ODE during training and differentiate through the solver, which is expensive.</p><p><strong>The 1st is what flow matching changes.</strong> Regress $v_\\theta$ onto a target velocity directly. No ODE solve in the loop, no divergence terms, just $\\lVert v_{\\theta,t} - v_t \\rVert^2$ — a plain squared error, the same shape of objective as DDPM's noise prediction.</p><p><strong>The 2nd is the obstacle that remains, and its fix.</strong> The true marginal $v_t$ is not known: it depends on the whole data distribution. But a <em>conditional</em> path — from noise to one specific data point — has a velocity you can write down. Regressing onto conditional velocities has the same minimiser as regressing onto the true marginal one, which is the theorem the method rests on. That move should look familiar: it is denoising score matching's trick, of replacing an unknown target with a computable conditional one that shares an optimum.</p><p><strong>The 3rd is false, and backwards.</strong> Diffusion is a <em>special case</em> of flow matching — particular choices of probability path give the variance-exploding and variance-preserving processes. That generality is the reason for the interest.</p><p><strong>The 4th is false.</strong> No discriminator anywhere; that is the GAN family.</p>",
