  "id": "e06q29",
  "exam": 6,
  "num": 29,
  "week": "W11",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>1st — and it is the most surprising thing about the method.</strong> The loss is an $L_2$ between rendered and observed pixel colours. There is no depth map, no mesh, no 3D supervision anywhere. Geometry appears because one density field has to explain every training view at once, and multi-view consistency is a strong enough constraint to pin down where the surfaces are.</p><p><strong>2nd — and the asymmetry is deliberate.</strong> $\\sigma(\\mathbf{x})$ takes position only; $\\mathbf{c}(\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{d})$ takes position and viewing direction. Whether something is there cannot depend on where you look from — allowing it to would let the model fake inconsistent views instead of building consistent geometry. What may depend on direction is how it looks, which is how specular highlights are represented.</p><p><strong>3rd — and it is the response to the cost in Q48 of exam 05.</strong> Samples in empty space contribute exactly zero and still cost a network evaluation. So: a coarse pass, then a probability density along the ray built from its weights $T_i\\alpha_i$, then the fine pass samples from that. Effort goes where the weight is.</p><p><strong>4th — the limitation that framed everything after it.</strong> A NeRF's weights <em>are</em> a scene. There is no transfer to a new one, training takes up to days, and the geometry is implicit — locked in the weights, not available to edit or simulate. Those three costs are what 3D Gaussian splatting answers.</p>",
