  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: DETR's matching cost is $L_{\\text{match}} = \\mathbf{-\\hat p_j(c_i)} + \\mathbf{L_{\\text{box}}(b_i, \\hat b_j)}$, solved by the <strong>Hungarian</strong> algorithm, which carries <strong>no</strong> gradient. In the Hungarian loss the class term is summed over <strong>all $N$ slots</strong> and the box term over <strong>the matched slots only</strong>.</strong> <strong>Why the minus sign.</strong> Matching <em>minimises</em> total cost, so a prediction that is confident about the right class must make the cost <em>lower</em>. Confidence is $\\hat p_j(c_i) \\in [0,1]$, rising with quality, so it enters negated. Getting this backwards is a standard slip and it inverts the whole assignment.</p><p><strong>Why no gradient, said properly.</strong> Hungarian matching is combinatorial and not differentiable, and it does not need to be, because its output is not a prediction — it is the <em>choice of which ground-truth object each slot is responsible for</em>. That is a label assignment, and nobody differentiates through a label. Once it is fixed, an ordinary loss is computed and backpropagated through everything else.</p><p><strong>Why the two terms have different ranges, which is the part people miss.</strong> There are $N$ slots and usually far fewer objects, so most slots are matched to $\\varnothing$. Those slots still have to learn to say “nothing here” — hence the class term over all $N$. But there is no box to regress towards, so a box term over them would be meaningless; it runs over the matched slots only.</p>",
