  "week": "W6",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: Once $b = 0$ and $\\lVert W_j \\rVert = \\lVert \\phi \\rVert = \\mathbf{1}$, the logit is exactly <strong>$\\cos\\theta_j$</strong>. ArcFace adds a margin to the <strong>angle</strong> of the <strong>true</strong> class only, giving <strong>$s\\cos(\\theta_y + m)$</strong>; the scale $s$ is needed because cosines live in <strong>$[-1,1]$</strong>.</strong> <strong>Follow the normalisation through, because everything else is a consequence.</strong> A softmax logit is $W_j^{\\top}\\phi + b_j$, which in polar form is $\\lVert W_j\\rVert\\lVert\\phi\\rVert\\cos\\theta_j + b_j$ — two lengths, an angle and a bias. Set the bias to zero and both lengths to one, and the logit <em>is</em> the cosine. The two lengths, which were free parameters the network could use to win the classification without separating anything, are gone.</p><p><strong>Then the margin has somewhere to go.</strong> $\\cos$ is decreasing on $[0,\\pi]$, so $\\cos(\\theta_y + m) < \\cos\\theta_y$: the true class is deliberately handicapped, and the only way to recover is to make $\\theta_y$ genuinely smaller than the others by at least $m$. A gap is enforced for every class at once, with no pairs to mine.</p><p><strong>Why $s$ is not cosmetic.</strong> A softmax over values confined to $[-1,1]$ can never be confident: the best achievable probability is bounded well away from one, so the loss can never approach zero and the gradients stay small everywhere. Multiplying by $s$ — typically $30$ to $64$ — restores the dynamic range the softmax needs. Without it the objective barely moves.</p><p><strong>And note the margin is on the <em>true class only</em></strong>, which is why that term leaves the sum in the denominator while the others keep their plain $\\cos\\theta_j$. Adding $m$ to every angle would shift all logits equally, and a softmax is invariant to that — it would do nothing at all.</p>",
