  "num": 34,
  "week": "W8",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: MAE masks about <strong>$75\\%$</strong> of the patches. The encoder sees <strong>the visible patches only</strong>, so $196$ patches become <strong>$49$</strong> tokens. The decoder fills each hole with a single shared <strong>mask token</strong> plus a <strong>positional embedding</strong>, and the loss is <strong>$L_2$</strong> on <strong>the masked patches only</strong>.</strong> <strong>Where $196$ and $49$ come from.</strong> A $224\\times224$ image at patch size $16$ gives $14\\times14 = 196$ patches; a quarter of them is $49$. Attention is quadratic in tokens, so that term falls by about $16\\times$, and the feed-forward term, linear in tokens, by $4\\times$. That is the efficiency, and it exists only because the masked patches never enter the encoder — the asymmetry is the design.</p><p><strong>Why the mask token is shared and the positional embedding is not.</strong> The mask token carries no information by construction: it is the same vector for every hole, a placeholder saying “something was here”. What distinguishes one hole from another is entirely its positional embedding. Remove those and every hole presents an identical input, so the best possible decoder output is the same patch everywhere.</p><p><strong>Why $75\\%$ and not BERT's $15\\%$.</strong> Language is dense in information and images are redundant; at $15\\%$ a model fills a hole by interpolating from its neighbours and never has to recognise anything. Removing three quarters makes local copying impossible.</p><p><strong>And the distinction worth a mark.</strong> Restricting the loss to the masked patches is <em>not</em> the compute saving — that comes from the encoder seeing a quarter of the tokens. It sharpens the objective, by spending no gradient on patches the model was simply handed.</p>",
