  "num": 35,
  "week": "W9",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: <strong>Inductive</strong> transfer means the <strong>tasks</strong> differ, and <strong>target</strong> labels are required. <strong>Transductive</strong> transfer means the <strong>domain</strong> differs, with <strong>labelled</strong> source labels and <strong>no</strong> target labels. <strong>Unsupervised</strong> transfer has <strong>no labels anywhere</strong>.</strong> <strong>The mnemonic:</strong> <em>inductive $=$ different tasks; transductive $=$ different domains; unsupervised $=$ no labels.</em> Two questions settle any case — what varies, and where the labels are.</p><p><strong>The two sub-types of inductive transfer</strong> are worth knowing separately: multi-task learning, where the source data is labelled, and self-taught learning, where it is not. Both are inductive because the <em>tasks</em> differ; the source labels are optional and the target labels never are.</p><p><strong>The vocabulary collision to keep straight.</strong> Unsupervised <em>domain adaptation</em> is exactly transductive transfer, and supervised domain adaptation is exactly inductive transfer — two literatures, one setting. And “unsupervised” means different things on the two sides: unsupervised <em>transfer</em> has no labels at all, while unsupervised <em>DA</em> has fully labelled source data and merely no target labels. An exam question that uses one vocabulary and expects the other is testing precisely this.</p><p><strong>Domain adaptation's place in the picture:</strong> a subfield of transfer learning with the task held <em>fixed</em> and only the data distribution moving.</p>",
