  "week": "W11",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: Every Gaussian in 3DGS carries a <strong>mean</strong>, a <strong>covariance</strong>, an <strong>opacity</strong>, and a view-dependent colour stored as <strong>spherical harmonics</strong>. The covariance is written <strong>$RSS^{\\top}R^{\\top}$</strong> so it stays <strong>positive semi-definite</strong>, and the opacity is passed through a <strong>sigmoid</strong> so it stays in <strong>$[0,1]$</strong>.</strong> <strong>Four numbers per primitive, and nothing else — there is no network.</strong> The mean places it, the covariance gives it an anisotropic shape and orientation, the opacity says how much light it stops, and the spherical harmonics let its colour change with viewing direction, which is 3DGS's answer to the job NeRF does by feeding the direction into an MLP.</p><p><strong>The two reparametrisations are the same idea twice, and the idea is worth more than either instance.</strong> A covariance must be positive semi-definite and an opacity must lie in $[0,1]$; an optimiser taking an unconstrained step will violate both immediately. Rather than constrain the optimiser — project back, or penalise — change variables so the constraint cannot be violated. Optimise a quaternion $R$ and a positive scale $S$ and <em>every</em> reachable value of $RSS^{\\top}R^{\\top}$ is a legal covariance; optimise a real $\\hat\\alpha$ and $\\sigma(\\hat\\alpha)$ is always in range.</p><p><em>Do not constrain the optimiser; reparametrise the constraint away.</em> The same move turns the Mahalanobis matrix $M$ into $W^{\\top}W$ in Week 6, and there it produces the Siamese architecture.</p>",
