  "id": "e06q39",
  "exam": 6,
  "num": 39,
  "week": "W7",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>The detector comes first, and it is the reason ViLBERT is slow.</strong> Region features require running an object detector over the image before any transformer sees anything; roughly $880$ of UNITER's $\\sim\\!900$ ms goes there, and ViLBERT pays the same. Two further costs come with it: the detector's class list is fixed, so anything outside it is invisible, and the background between the boxes is discarded.</p><p><strong>Then embedding, then the per-modality transformers (TRM), then co-attention (Co-TRM).</strong> The dual-stream design processes each modality on its own first and only then lets them exchange queries, which is the definition of dual-stream — against UNITER's single stream, where image and text tokens are concatenated and ordinary self-attention does all the mixing.</p><p><strong>The comparison worth carrying.</strong> Stream count barely matters for runtime: ViLBERT ($\\sim\\!920$ ms, dual) and UNITER ($\\sim\\!900$ ms, single) differ by the cost of the second stream, about $20$ ms. ViLT, which replaces the detector with a linear patch projection, runs in $\\sim\\!15$ ms. Tokenisation is the axis that costs; streams are the axis that does not.</p>",
