  "id": "e07q03",
  "exam": 7,
  "num": 3,
  "week": "W2",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>The 1st is false, and inverting it is the whole idea.</strong> The recurrence is over <strong>depth</strong>, not over the sequence. The same layer is applied again and again to the whole sequence in parallel, so the model keeps the transformer's $O(1)$ path length and its parallelism while gaining the property recurrence gives: an unbounded number of processing steps.</p><p><strong>The 2nd is false, and follows from the 1st.</strong> If the layer recurs, its parameters are <strong>shared</strong> across depth — that is what makes it a recurrence rather than a deep stack. A timestep embedding is added so the layer can tell which iteration it is on.</p><p><strong>The 3rd is the interesting part.</strong> Adaptive Computation Time lets each <em>position</em> decide when it has been processed enough and halt, while others continue. Some tokens are easy and some are not, and a fixed-depth stack spends the same computation on both. This is one of the few places in the course where the amount of computation is input-dependent.</p><p><strong>The 4th is false.</strong> Positional encoding is still needed — attention is still permutation equivariant. The timestep embedding is in <em>addition</em> to it, and encodes depth rather than position.</p>",
