  "id": "e07q08",
  "exam": 7,
  "num": 8,
  "week": "W3",
  "explanation": "<p>The three the lecture names, and they are separate problems with separate answers.</p><p><strong>1st — quadratic cost.</strong> $O(N^2 d)$ with $N = HW/P^2$, so $\\times16$ per doubling of resolution. Swin's windows are the answer.</p><p><strong>3rd — data hunger.</strong> A CNN is given locality and translation equivariance by construction; a ViT must learn both from examples, and learning a constraint costs samples that imposing it does not. Hence ViT's need for JFT-scale pre-training before it beats a comparable CNN, and hence the standing advice to use a CNN when training from scratch on limited data.</p><p><strong>4th — inference efficiency.</strong> Even at equal accuracy the throughput is worse, which is why the second half of that advice is “and on edge devices”.</p><p><strong>The 2nd is false and is the only invented one.</strong> Fine-tuning a ViT is entirely ordinary — indeed the whole pre-train-then-fine-tune pattern is what made ViT work at all.</p><p>Worth adding to the list, though not among the three: no multi-scale hierarchy by construction, which is what makes plain ViT awkward as a detection or segmentation backbone.</p>",
