  "num": 11,
  "week": "W4",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>The 1st is false and the 2nd is the correction.</strong> The entire idea of the projection family is to <em>leave</em> 3D: project the cloud to a 2D image, run an ordinary 2D CNN — with all the maturity, speed and pre-training that brings — and back-project the per-pixel predictions onto the points. RangeNet++ does exactly that with a $5$-channel range image ($x$, $y$, $z$, range, remission) at $64 \\times 2048$.</p><p><strong>The 3rd is a nice piece of engineering realism.</strong> That image is thirty-two times wider than it is tall, because a rotating LiDAR has $64$ beams and thousands of samples per revolution. A standard encoder that downsamples equally in both directions would reduce the height to nothing after a few stages, so RangeNet++ downsamples <em>horizontally</em> only. The shape of the sensor dictates the shape of the network.</p><p><strong>The 4th is EfficientLPS's contribution and it addresses the range view's defining weakness.</strong> An object's apparent size shrinks with distance, so one kernel size cannot suit both near and far. Predicting the <em>dilation factor</em> from range-encoded features gives each position a receptive field scaled to how far away it is — a distance-invariant receptive field, restoring by learning what the projection destroyed. It relies on bilinear interpolation and depthwise separable convolution to stay affordable.</p><p>The trade for the whole family: cheap, low-latency and able to reuse 2D CNNs, against lost spatial information, distortion, occlusion, and no ability to complete unseen geometry.</p>",
  "type": "multi",
