  "id": "e07q12",
  "exam": 7,
  "num": 12,
  "week": "W5",
  "explanation": "<p>$\\phi(\\sum_i \\psi(\\mathbf{x}_i))$ is a sum over the elements, and a sum does not care about order — so the output is unchanged under any permutation of the inputs. That is <strong>invariance</strong>, and it makes deep sets a correct architecture for a set.</p><p><strong>What is missing is the graph.</strong> There is no $A$ anywhere in the formula. Two graphs with identical node features and completely different edges produce identical outputs, so deep sets cannot represent anything about structure. It is the right answer to “here is a set” and the wrong one to “here is a graph”.</p><p><strong>Why not (a).</strong> Invariant and equivariant are different properties and this is the invariant one: $f(PX) = f(X)$, a single vector out. An equivariant map returns one vector per node and satisfies $f(PX) = Pf(X)$ — that is what a GNN <em>layer</em> is, and it is what deep sets is not.</p><p><strong>Why not (d).</strong> A GCN layer is $\\sigma(\\hat A H W)$; the $\\hat A$ is precisely what deep sets lacks.</p><p>The connection worth carrying: PointNet <em>is</em> deep sets — shared per-point MLP, then a symmetric reduction — which is why the lecture can call PointNet a GNN with no edges. The three architectures differ only in which elements may interact: none, some, or all.</p>",
  "type": "single",
