  "id": "e07q14",
  "exam": 7,
  "num": 14,
  "week": "W5",
  "explanation": "<p>A $2 \\times |\\mathcal{E}|$ integer array: the first row lists source nodes, the second targets. The adjacency matrix is $N \\times N$ and almost all zeros; COO stores only the edges that exist.</p><p><strong>Why the saving is the whole reason it is the standard.</strong> A graph with $10^6$ nodes and $10^7$ edges has an adjacency matrix of $10^{12}$ entries — unstorable — and an edge index of $2\\times10^7$ integers, which is nothing. Message passing is then a gather-scatter over that array rather than a dense matrix product, and its cost is $O(|\\mathcal{E}|)$ to match.</p><p><strong>The 4th is false, and the format is if anything more natural for directed graphs.</strong> An entry is an ordered pair, so $(i,j)$ and $(j,i)$ are different entries. An undirected graph is represented by including <em>both</em>, which is a convention rather than a requirement — and one worth remembering, since forgetting the reverse edges silently makes an undirected graph directed and messages flow only one way.</p>",
