  "id": "e07q15",
  "exam": 7,
  "num": 15,
  "week": "W6",
  "explanation": "<p>The four axioms are: $\\mathcal{D}(x,y) = 0 \\iff x = y$ (identity of indiscernibles); $\\mathcal{D} \\ge 0$; symmetry; and the triangle inequality. Dropping the first leaves the other three intact — so the 1st, 2nd and 3rd, which claim the others break, are all false. A pseudo-metric is still non-negative, still symmetric, and still satisfies the triangle inequality.</p><p><strong>What is actually given up</strong>, and why it is given up gladly. The axiom says that two things at distance zero must be <em>the same thing</em>. In a face embedding, two photographs of one person are not the same input and we would very much like them at distance zero. Insisting on the axiom would forbid exactly the behaviour the model is being trained to produce.</p><p><strong>Why the triangle inequality is the one you keep.</strong> It is what makes “close” behave transitively: if $a$ is near $b$ and $b$ near $c$, $a$ cannot be far from $c$. Without it a ranking is not consistent and a cluster has no meaning, and every indexing structure that makes nearest-neighbour search sublinear relies on it to prune. Losing identity of indiscernibles costs none of that.</p>",
