  "id": "e07q16",
  "exam": 7,
  "num": 16,
  "week": "W6",
  "explanation": "<p>\\[ \\mathcal{L} = \\underbrace{y\\,\\mathcal{D}^2}_{\\text{pull}} + \\underbrace{(1-y)\\max\\{0,\\ m-\\mathcal{D}\\}}_{\\text{push}} \\] Once $\\mathcal{D} \\ge m$ the push term is exactly zero, and so is its gradient. That is the hinge, and (c) is why it is there.</p><p><strong>The argument for it.</strong> Without the hinge the loss would keep driving dissimilar pairs apart forever, and “as far as possible” is not a target — embedding space is bounded in any practical sense, so the objective would be unsatisfiable and would spend gradient on pairs that were already fine. The hinge states a criterion instead of an ambition: <em>far enough is finished</em>, and the effort goes where it is still needed.</p><p><strong>And the contrast with the pull term is deliberate.</strong> There the loss is $\\mathcal{D}^2$ with no hinge, because for a similar pair there <em>is</em> an absolute target — zero — and the squared form gives gradient $2\\mathcal{D}$, which is large when the pair is badly wrong and small when it is nearly right. Each half has the shape its own goal requires.</p><p><strong>Why not (a).</strong> $\\max\\{0,\\cdot\\}$ is not differentiable at the kink; the hinge slightly costs differentiability rather than buying it. <strong>Why not (d).</strong> Collapse is the triplet loss's characteristic failure, and it comes from the margin arithmetic rather than from the hinge.</p>",
