  "id": "e07q17",
  "exam": 7,
  "num": 17,
  "week": "W6",
  "explanation": "<p>All four, and together they are the opening argument of the whole lecture.</p><p><strong>1st and 2nd — what is wrong with a classifier.</strong> The set of answers is the set of columns of $W$, fixed when the architecture was defined. A new identity is not a new input, it is a new <em>output</em>, and there is no way to add one without changing the network and retraining. In an open set — where new classes appear after deployment, which is the normal case for faces, products or speakers — that is not a limitation to be worked around but a mismatch with the problem.</p><p><strong>3rd — what an embedding does instead.</strong> It learns a <em>space</em> rather than a set of answers. The identity of the classes is not in the parameters at all; it is in a gallery of stored vectors. Enrolling someone is a forward pass and an append, with no gradient step and no downtime.</p><p><strong>4th — and this is what makes it a complete story.</strong> All three test-time tasks fall out of the same object. <em>Verification</em> is a threshold, $\\mathcal{D} < \\tau$. <em>Ranking</em> is a sort by distance. <em>Recognition</em> is a nearest neighbour in the gallery. One embedding, three tasks, none of them requiring the model to know the class list.</p><p>And note why a pairwise “same or different?” network does not solve it either: it returns a <em>score</em>, not a <em>vector</em>. There is nothing to index, cluster or sort consistently, and answering a gallery query means running the network against every entry.</p>",
