  "id": "e07q18",
  "exam": 7,
  "num": 18,
  "week": "W7",
  "explanation": "<p>Not capacity, not memory — <strong>aligned paired data</strong>. To learn that an image and a caption describe the same thing, you need images and captions that describe the same thing, and somebody or something has to have put them together.</p><p><strong>Why that is the binding constraint.</strong> Unimodal data is effectively free: the web has text, and it has images. What is scarce is the <em>correspondence</em>. CLIP's contribution was as much the $400$M-pair dataset as the method, and the reason so much of the field runs on image–text pairs rather than image–depth or audio–tactile is simply that alt-text exists and the others do not.</p><p><strong>What follows from it, which is the useful part.</strong> Several design decisions in the lecture are consequences: contrastive pre-training is favoured because it needs only pairing and no fine-grained labels; unimodal data is added alongside the paired data because it is not restricted to what happens to be aligned, broadens the domain and acts as a regulariser; and multi-stage training exists because when one modality has far more data than the other, joint training lets the abundant one dominate.</p><p><strong>And the judgement it implies.</strong> Before building a multimodal model, ask whether the outcome will be <em>emergent</em> rather than merely dominated — because if it is dominated, you have paid the alignment cost for nothing.</p>",
