  "id": "e07q19",
  "exam": 7,
  "num": 19,
  "week": "W7",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>The 1st is the problem.</strong> Single-stream models concatenate the modalities into one sequence, so $n$ is the sum of the two lengths and attention costs $O(n^2)$. Adding a video stream, where tokens are the product of spatial and temporal extents, makes this the dominant cost of the model.</p><p><strong>The 4th gives both remedies the lecture names.</strong> VATT drops tokens <em>at random</em> during training, which is crude and works. And the whole efficient-attention literature from Week 3 applies unchanged — windowed attention, factorisation, sparse patterns — because none of it cares what the tokens represent.</p><p><strong>The 3rd is the natural objection and it is false, which is the interesting part.</strong> Dropping tokens at random does not destroy the alignment, for the same reason dropout does not destroy a network: the objective is over the whole sequence, the dropping is resampled every step, and no particular token is required for the correspondence to be learned. It is a regulariser as much as a saving.</p><p><strong>The 2nd is false.</strong> Late fusion does avoid the long joint sequence — each branch runs on its own modality — but at the price of no cross-modal interaction at all, which is the thing the model was built for. It is a different design, not a fix for this one.</p>",
