  "id": "e07q21",
  "exam": 7,
  "num": 21,
  "week": "W8",
  "explanation": "<p>The encoder, and nothing else.</p><p><strong>The rule, and it has no exceptions in the lecture.</strong> The jigsaw classifier, the rotation head, the MAE decoder, SimCLR's projection head, DINO's projection head — all discarded. They exist to give the objective something to be computed on; the representation was always the product.</p><p><strong>Why the projection head in particular is worth naming (option (c) is the near miss).</strong> It is tempting to keep it, since it is trained and it is part of the model that produced the loss. But it is precisely the part built to <em>discard</em> the information the augmentations change, and some of that information is what a downstream task needs. Linear probes on the encoder's output beat probes on the projection's output by a wide margin — which is the measurement that settles it.</p><p><strong>And the corollary about evaluation.</strong> Since only the encoder survives, the proxy task's own accuracy is not the thing being optimised for; it may even have been solved by a shortcut (exam 06, Q20). Pre-training is judged downstream, by linear probing or fine-tuning, and never by how well the proxy was solved.</p>",
