  "id": "e07q23",
  "exam": 7,
  "num": 23,
  "week": "W9",
  "explanation": "<p>Quantisation of the frozen base weights, plus LoRA adapters on top. Two techniques attacking two different terms of the memory bill, which is why combining them is worth a name.</p><p><strong>The arithmetic that makes it necessary.</strong> Training memory is weights $+$ gradients $+$ optimiser states $+$ activations. LoRA removes the gradients and both Adam moments for the frozen weights — three of the four — and cannot touch the weights themselves, because the forward pass needs them. For a 7B model that leaves a floor of about $14$ GB in fp16, and the realistic saving is around $4\\times$ rather than the $64\\times$ the parameter ratio suggests.</p><p><strong>So quantisation attacks the floor.</strong> Storing the frozen weights at 8 or 4 bits instead of 16 cuts the one term LoRA could not, and takes a 7B model to roughly $10$ GB. The adapters themselves stay at full precision, because they are tiny and they are the part being trained.</p><p><strong>Why not (a).</strong> LoRA is usually applied to the attention projections, but which ones is a configuration choice, not what the Q stands for. <strong>Why not (c) or (d).</strong> The rank is a hyperparameter, and the optimiser is ordinary.</p>",
