  "week": "W2",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: Scaled dot-product attention is $\\mathrm{Attention}(Q,K,V) = \\mathbf{\\mathrm{softmax}\\bigl(QK^{\\top}/\\sqrt{d_k}\\bigr)V}$. The scale is <strong>$\\sqrt{d_k}$</strong> because <strong>the dot product's standard deviation grows like $\\sqrt{d_k}$</strong>. Multi-head attention concatenates the heads and applies <strong>$W^{O}$</strong>; the standard sizing is $d_k = d_v = \\mathbf{d_{\\text{model}}/h}$.</strong> <strong>Read the formula as a lookup.</strong> $QK^{\\top}$ scores every query against every key; the softmax turns each row into a distribution; multiplying by $V$ returns a weighted blend of the values. It is a dictionary lookup made differentiable, which is why the softmax cannot be replaced by an argmax (Q5).</p><p><strong>Why $W^{O}$ is not optional.</strong> Concatenating $h$ heads of width $d/h$ gives a vector of width $d$ again, so the shapes already work — but without $W^{O}$ each output dimension would come from exactly one head, and the heads could never combine. $W^{O}$ is what mixes them, and it is $d \\times d$, the same size as any one of the projections.</p><p><strong>The consequence of the standard sizing.</strong> With $hd_k = d$ the total cost $4nd^2 + 2n^2d$ is <em>independent of $h$</em>: per-head work shrinks exactly as fast as the number of heads grows. So multi-head attention is free relative to single-head at full width, and what it buys is $h$ independent attention distributions — the ability to aggregate several low-rank maps into a higher-rank one.</p>",
