  "week": "W4",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: PointNet computes $f(x_1,\\dots,x_n) = \\mathbf{\\gamma \\circ g\\bigl(h(x_1),\\dots,h(x_n)\\bigr)}$, where $h$ is a <strong>shared per-point MLP</strong> and $g$ must be <strong>symmetric</strong>. PointNet uses <strong>max pooling</strong> for $g$. Its T-Net is kept near-orthogonal by the penalty <strong>$\\lVert I - TT^{\\top}\\rVert_F^2$</strong>.</strong> <strong>The theorem, in one line: $f$ is permutation-invariant if and only if $g$ is symmetric.</strong> Everything else about the architecture is a consequence. $h$ must be shared, or the result would depend on which point got which network. $g$ must be symmetric, or the order would survive the reduction. $\\gamma$ acts after the reduction, when the ordering is already gone, so it can be anything.</p><p><strong>Which symmetric functions are available:</strong> $\\max$, $\\min$, $\\mathrm{sum}$, $\\mathrm{mean}$. PointNet picks max, and the choice has consequences — max is robust to outliers and to varying point counts, and it is less expressive than sum, which can count (exam 04, Q13).</p><p><strong>Why the T-Net needs the penalty.</strong> It predicts a $64\\times64$ feature transform, and an unconstrained matrix can apply any distortion it likes — including one that makes the task easier without corresponding to any geometric alignment. $\\lVert I - TT^{\\top}\\rVert_F^2$ is zero exactly when $T$ is orthogonal, so the penalty pushes it towards a rigid motion, which is the transformation class the invariance was supposed to be about.</p><p><strong>And note what the T-Net is not.</strong> It is a spatial <em>transformer</em> — it predicts a matrix and applies it — not a form of attention, and it is not where the permutation invariance comes from.</p>",
