  "week": "W5",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: The unnormalised Laplacian is $L = \\mathbf{D - A}$, which is <strong>symmetric</strong> and <strong>positive semi-definite</strong>. Its quadratic form is $x^{\\top}Lx = \\mathbf{\\sum_{(i,j)\\in\\mathcal{E}}(x_i - x_j)^2}$, so the multiplicity of the eigenvalue $0$ is <strong>the number of connected components</strong> and $\\operatorname{rank}(L) = \\mathbf{N - k}$.</strong> <strong>The quadratic form is the whole object.</strong> Everything else follows from it. It is a sum of squares, so it is non-negative for every $x$ — that is positive semi-definiteness, proved in one line. It is zero exactly when $x_i = x_j$ across every edge, that is, when $x$ is constant on each connected component — so the kernel is spanned by the components' indicator vectors, there are $k$ of them, and the rank is $N - k$.</p><p><strong>Why it is called a Laplacian.</strong> $(L_N H)_i = \\mathbf{h}_i - \\sum_{j\\in\\mathcal{N}_i} \\frac{a_{ij}}{\\sqrt{\\deg i \\deg j}}\\mathbf{h}_j$ is the node's own value minus a weighted average of its neighbours' — a discrete second difference, which is what a Laplacian is. So $x^{\\top}Lx$ small means $x$ is <em>smooth</em> on the graph, and the low eigenvectors are the smooth functions on it.</p><p><strong>Two cross-checks worth carrying.</strong> $L\\mathbf{1} = 0$ always, so even a connected graph has one zero eigenvalue. And $\\operatorname{trace}(L) = \\sum_i \\deg(i) = 2|\\mathcal{E}|$, which must equal the sum of the eigenvalues — a cheap way to catch an arithmetic slip.</p>",
