  "num": 34,
  "week": "W6",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: For a fixed $\\mathcal{D}_p$ and margin $m$, a negative is <strong>hard</strong> when $\\mathcal{D}_n \\le \\mathcal{D}_p$, <strong>semi-hard</strong> when $\\mathcal{D}_p < \\mathcal{D}_n < \\mathcal{D}_p + m$, and <strong>easy</strong> when $\\mathcal{D}_n \\ge \\mathcal{D}_p + m$. Their losses are <strong>at least $m$</strong>, <strong>between $0$ and $m$</strong> and <strong>exactly $0$</strong> respectively.</strong> <strong>All of it comes from reading $\\mathcal{L} = \\max\\{0,\\ \\mathcal{D}_p - \\mathcal{D}_n + m\\}$ as a function of $\\mathcal{D}_n$</strong>: a line of slope $-1$, hitting zero at $\\mathcal{D}_p + m$ and flat after. Put $\\mathcal{D}_n = \\mathcal{D}_p$ and you get exactly $m$; that is the boundary between hard and semi-hard, and it is the number that matters.</p><p><strong>Why, in one line: a collapsed encoder pays exactly $m$ on every triplet.</strong> Every distance is zero, so $\\mathcal{L} = \\max\\{0, 0 - 0 + m\\} = m$. Hard negatives cost <em>at least</em> $m$, so a diet of them makes collapsing the cheaper option and gradient descent takes it. Semi-hard negatives cost strictly less than $m$, so collapse would make things worse, and strictly more than $0$, so there is still a gradient. That narrow window is the entire reason for semi-hard mining.</p><p><strong>And why easy negatives are sometimes called <em>invalid</em> rather than easy.</strong> Their loss is not small, it is exactly zero, and so is their gradient. They cost arithmetic and contribute nothing, and their share grows as training progresses — which is the efficiency argument for batch-hard over batch-all.</p>",
