  "week": "W10",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: Langevin dynamics is $\\mathbf{x}_{t+1} \\leftarrow \\mathbf{\\mathbf{x}_t + \\delta\\,s_\\theta(\\mathbf{x}_t) + \\sqrt{2\\delta}\\,\\eta_t}$. The first term <strong>moves towards regions of higher density</strong> and the second <strong>makes the result a sample rather than a mode</strong>. Without the second, sampling becomes <strong>mode-finding</strong>; convergence needs <strong>$T \\to \\infty$</strong> and <strong>$\\delta \\to 0$</strong>.</strong> <strong>Why the noise is not a perturbation but the point.</strong> Following the score alone is gradient ascent on $\\log p$: it climbs to the nearest local maximum and stops. Run it from a hundred initialisations and you get at most a hundred outputs, one per basin, the same every time. That is optimisation, not sampling — and a generative model exists precisely because many outputs are valid.</p><p><strong>What the balance produces.</strong> Where the density is high the score is weak and the noise dominates, so the walker spreads out; where it is low the score is strong and pulls it back. The stationary distribution of that competition is $p$ itself, in the limit.</p><p><strong>Note $\\sqrt{2\\delta}$, not $\\delta$.</strong> The drift scales with the step and the noise with its square root — the standard scaling for a stochastic differential equation, and getting it wrong changes which distribution the chain converges to.</p><p><strong>The practical complication the lecture then fixes.</strong> The learned score is only accurate where training data was, and sampling starts far from the data. Hence multiple noise levels and annealing from $\\sigma_L$ down to $\\sigma_1$, carrying the state forward, with step size $\\alpha_i \\propto \\sigma_i^2$.</p>",
