  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: The RSSM splits the state into a <strong>deterministic</strong> part $h_t = \\mathbf{f(h_{t-1}, s_{t-1}, a_{t-1})}$ carrying <strong>memory</strong>, and a <strong>stochastic</strong> part $s_t \\sim \\mathbf{p(s_t \\mid h_t)}$ carrying <strong>uncertainty</strong>. Training aligns the inference model <strong>$q(s_t \\mid h_t, o_t)$</strong> with the prior through a <strong>KL</strong> term.</strong> <strong>The problem it solves.</strong> The 2018 World Models paper used a single recurrent hidden state, which had to be the memory of what has happened, the belief about the present, and the distribution over what comes next. Those pull in opposite directions: a memory wants to be reliable and deterministic, and a distribution over futures has to be stochastic. Asking one variable to be both is why it did not scale.</p><p><strong>Why the KL term is the load-bearing part.</strong> There are two ways to get $s_t$: the <em>prior</em> $p(s_t\\mid h_t)$, which predicts it from memory alone, and the <em>inference</em> model $q(s_t\\mid h_t, o_t)$, which also gets to look at the actual observation. Training uses $q$, because it is more accurate. But <strong>imagination can only use $p$</strong> — when the agent dreams forward there is no observation to condition on. The KL is what forces the distribution that will actually be rolled out to agree with the one that had the answer in front of it. Without it, the model would be excellent at filtering and useless at prediction, which is the one thing it is for.</p><p><strong>And the two systems built on it:</strong> PlaNet plans in latent space with model predictive control and replans every step; Dreamer learns a policy and a value function inside the imagination and does no search at execution time.</p>",
