  "id": "e08q05",
  "exam": 8,
  "num": 5,
  "week": "W2",
  "explanation": "<p>Two ideas, and they attack different parts of the parameter count.</p><p><strong>Embedding factorisation.</strong> BERT ties the embedding width to the hidden width, so a $30{,}522$-word vocabulary at $d = 768$ costs about $2.3\\times10^{7}$ parameters in the embedding table alone. ALBERT decomposes it: vocabulary $\\to$ a small $E$, then $E \\to d$. Since $E \\ll d$ the table shrinks by roughly $d/E$, and the argument is that a context-independent token embedding does not need the same capacity as a context-dependent hidden state.</p><p><strong>Cross-layer parameter sharing.</strong> All twelve layers use the same weights, so depth costs nothing in parameters — the same move as the Universal Transformer's recurrence over depth.</p><p><strong>Why not (a).</strong> That is <em>DistilBERT</em>, and the pair is worth keeping straight: DistilBERT is knowledge distillation, ALBERT is embedding factorisation plus sharing. The question is a test of which name goes with which idea.</p><p><strong>And a caveat worth stating.</strong> Sharing reduces <em>parameters</em>, not compute: twelve shared layers still run twelve times. ALBERT is smaller to store, not faster to run.</p>",
