  "id": "e08q09",
  "exam": 8,
  "num": 9,
  "week": "W3",
  "explanation": "<p>Two conditions, and each follows from one of the ViT's limitations.</p><p><strong>Limited data, training from scratch.</strong> A CNN is given locality and translation equivariance by construction; a ViT must learn both from examples. Learning a constraint costs samples that imposing it does not, so below some dataset size the CNN is simply better — and above it the ViT wins, because it is not restricted to exactly the constraints a convolution imposes.</p><p><strong>Edge deployment.</strong> Even at matched accuracy the ViT's inference is less efficient. That is a separate limitation from the data hunger and it does not go away with more pre-training.</p><p><strong>Why not (b).</strong> High resolution is the case that argues <em>hardest</em> against a plain ViT, since attention is quadratic in patches — but the answer there is Swin, not a CNN.</p><p><strong>Why not (d).</strong> A pre-trained checkpoint is exactly what removes the data-hunger objection; it is a reason to reach for the ViT.</p>",
