  "id": "e08q13",
  "exam": 8,
  "num": 13,
  "week": "W5",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>The 1st — residual connections, and why they work here specifically.</strong> Over-smoothing is repeated averaging converging to a fixed point. A residual keeps an un-aggregated copy of $\\mathbf{h}^{(l)}$ in $\\mathbf{h}^{(l+1)}$, so the fixed point of the pure aggregation is no longer the fixed point of the layer. It does not slow the smoothing down; it changes what the iteration converges to.</p><p><strong>The 2nd — Jumping Knowledge, which does something different.</strong> It does not prevent smoothing at all. It lets the final representation be selected or combined from <em>every</em> depth rather than only the last, so each node can effectively use the depth at which it was still informative. That matters because the right depth is not the same for every node — a node in a dense region over-smooths sooner than one in a sparse periphery.</p><p><strong>The 4th is not a cop-out.</strong> Two or three layers really is the standard depth for a GCN, and choosing it deliberately rather than stacking twenty is the first thing to do.</p><p><strong>The 3rd is false and swaps the object.</strong> <strong>Drop</strong><em>Edge</em> removes <em>edges</em> at random each epoch. Removing nodes would be a different operation with a different effect; removing edges slows the mixing, which is exactly the process being over-applied.</p>",
