  "id": "e08q22",
  "exam": 8,
  "num": 22,
  "week": "W8",
  "explanation": "<p>Inpainting, from 2016, and it is the ancestor of MAE — which makes the differences the point.</p><table class='xt'><tr><th></th><th><strong>context encoder</strong></th><th><strong>MAE</strong></th></tr><tr><td>what is removed</td><td>one <strong>large</strong> region</td><td>many random patches, $75\\%$</td></tr><tr><td>what the encoder sees</td><td>the <strong>whole</strong> image, hole included</td><td>the visible patches only</td></tr><tr><td>the loss</td><td>reconstruction <strong>plus adversarial</strong></td><td>plain $L_2$</td></tr></table><p><strong>The 1st is false and describes MAE.</strong> One hole against many is the first difference.</p><p><strong>The 3rd is the second, and it is the one that matters for cost.</strong> The context encoder is a convolutional encoder–decoder over the full image, so it pays for the hole; MAE's encoder never sees the masked patches, which is where its $16\\times$ attention saving comes from.</p><p><strong>The 4th — and why the adversarial term was there.</strong> $L_2$ alone on a large hole produces a blurred average of every plausible filling, because the mean of many valid answers is the minimiser (the same argument as Week 10's case for generative models). The discriminator pushes for a sharp, plausible one. MAE can drop it because with many small holes there is far less ambiguity per hole, and because it is judged on downstream features rather than on how the reconstruction looks.</p>",
