  "id": "e08q23",
  "exam": 8,
  "num": 23,
  "week": "W8",
  "explanation": "<p>Prepend a handful of <em>learnable</em> vectors to the input sequence and train only those; the model's own weights stay frozen and gradients flow through them without being applied to them.</p><p><strong>Why the word “prompt” is slightly misleading.</strong> These are not words. They are continuous vectors in embedding space, most of which correspond to no token at all — so prompt tuning searches a strictly larger space than any amount of hand-writing could, which is why it works better than prompt engineering. The name comes from where they sit, not from what they are.</p><p><strong>Where it fits among the six adaptation strategies.</strong> Linear probing, non-linear probing, adapters, prompt tuning, full fine-tuning, and prompting or in-context learning. Prompt tuning sits in the middle: more capacity than a probe, far fewer trained parameters than fine-tuning, and — unlike adapters — it adds nothing to the architecture, so one frozen model can serve many tasks by swapping a short prefix.</p><p><strong>Why not (a) or (d).</strong> Both describe prompt <em>engineering</em>, in which nothing is trained. <strong>Why not (c).</strong> Fine-tuning one layer is a probing variant, and it modifies the model where prompt tuning does not.</p>",
