  "id": "e08q24",
  "exam": 8,
  "num": 24,
  "week": "W9",
  "explanation": "<p>Two axes, and all four statements are points on them.</p><p><strong>Axis one — how abstract the knowledge is (1st and 2nd).</strong> Early layers learn edges, colour opponency and simple textures, and those are close to universal: almost any natural-image task can use them. Later layers encode object-level semantics, which are only useful if the target task is about similar objects. That is why the depth at which you cut is the practical decision — freeze the general part, retrain the specific part.</p><p><strong>Axis two — how specific the source is (3rd).</strong> ImageNet is deliberately broad, so its features are generic. Cityscapes is one city's street scenes, so its features are domain-specific — excellent for KITTI, which is also street scenes, and much less useful elsewhere.</p><p><strong>4th — and keeping the axes separate is the point.</strong> Task similarity is about the <em>goals and outputs</em>; domain similarity is about the <em>data</em>. They vary independently: medical images $\\to$ a rare disease is high on both and transfers; social-media photographs $\\to$ satellite imagery is low on domain similarity and does not.</p><p>Judging by only one axis is how negative transfer is invited — the bet that “related” means related in the way that matters.</p>",
