  "id": "e08q26",
  "exam": 8,
  "num": 26,
  "week": "W9",
  "explanation": "<p>Once you have a mapping between domains, there are two places to apply it.</p><p><strong>Direction 1 — translate the training data.</strong> Map labelled source images into the target style, train on those, deploy directly. The labels carry over unchanged, because a translation that preserves content preserves what the label refers to. Cost: nothing at inference.</p><p><strong>Direction 2 — translate at inference.</strong> Train on the source as it is, and map each incoming target sample back into the source style before feeding it. Cost: the translation network runs on every input, forever.</p><p><strong>Which to choose.</strong> Direction 1 if you control training and inference latency matters, which is the usual case — CyCADA's GTA5-to-Cityscapes and the whole sim2real line work this way. Direction 2 if the deployed model is fixed and cannot be retrained.</p><p><strong>The caveat that belongs with both</strong> (exam 05, Q24): cycle consistency <em>discourages</em> hallucination, it does not forbid it. Deleting a distant pedestrian costs an $L_1$ penalty of a few pixels, and if the inverse mapping can plausibly put something back, the cycle closes anyway.</p>",
