  "week": "W10",
  "explanation": "<p>\\[ p(\\mathbf{x}) = \\frac{1}{Z}e^{-\\beta E(\\mathbf{x})}, \\qquad Z = \\int e^{-\\beta E(\\mathbf{x})}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{x} \\] Two requirements on a density, and one part of the formula for each.</p><p><strong>1st — positivity, for free.</strong> $e^{u} > 0$ whatever $u$ is, so the network computing $E$ is completely unconstrained and the result is still non-negative. That is why the energy is exponentiated rather than, say, squared or passed through a ReLU: those would also be non-negative and would place awkward constraints on the function.</p><p><strong>4th — normalisation, at a price.</strong> $Z$ makes the thing integrate to one, and computing it means integrating over the whole of data space. For images that is an integral over $\\mathbb{R}^{150528}$, and there is no way to do it. Every other family in the lecture is an evasion of this one integral.</p><p><strong>The 2nd is false, and getting it right is what makes the score work.</strong> $Z$ is a number — the integral has already been taken over $\\mathbf{x}$, so nothing of $\\mathbf{x}$ survives in it. That is exactly why $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}}\\log Z = 0$ and the score deletes it. If $Z$ depended on $\\mathbf{x}$ the trick would fail and there would be no Week 10.</p><p><strong>The 3rd is false.</strong> $E$ may be any real value; only $p$ must be non-negative, and the exponential guarantees that regardless.</p>",
