  "id": "e08q28",
  "exam": 8,
  "num": 28,
  "week": "W10",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>1st and 4th go together.</strong> A separately trained autoencoder compresses a $512\\times512\\times3$ image to a latent of roughly $64\\times64\\times4$ — about $48$ times fewer numbers — and the diffusion process runs entirely in that space. Since every sampling step costs a forward pass over the tensor being denoised, shrinking the tensor by $48\\times$ is what took text-to-image from a research demonstration to something that runs on a consumer GPU.</p><p><strong>Why an autoencoder rather than diffusing at low resolution.</strong> A downsampled image loses detail irrecoverably; a learned latent keeps what the decoder needs to reconstruct sharply, and discards the perceptually irrelevant high-frequency content that diffusion would otherwise spend most of its capacity modelling.</p><p><strong>3rd — and the 2nd is its near miss.</strong> The text conditioning enters through <em>cross-attention</em> layers inside the U-Net: the image latent supplies the queries, the text embedding the keys and values. Concatenating instead would force a fixed text length and give every spatial position the same view of the prompt; cross-attention lets each region attend to the words that concern it, which is what makes spatially specific prompting work at all.</p><p>Note the pattern: this is exactly the cross-attention of Week 7, doing exactly the job it does there.</p>",
