  "id": "e08q30",
  "exam": 8,
  "num": 30,
  "week": "W11",
  "explanation": "<p>All four, and the second is the one that makes this a deep-learning topic rather than a graphics one.</p><p><strong>1st — the obvious applications.</strong> Entertainment, and AR/VR, where a captured scene must be viewable from wherever the headset happens to be.</p><p><strong>2nd — and this is the argument that earns it a lecture.</strong> To predict what a scene looks like from an unseen viewpoint you must have worked out its geometry, its materials and what is occluding what. Novel view synthesis <em>cannot</em> be solved by memorisation, so it is a task whose solution requires understanding — the same logic that makes a good self-supervised pretext task, and the reason this sits comfortably alongside Week 8.</p><p><strong>3rd — simulation.</strong> A reconstructed scene can be driven through, relit, or edited, so capture becomes a way of building training environments. That is why robotics groups care.</p><p><strong>4th — the practical precondition.</strong> It is <em>calibrated</em> views that are needed: poses and intrinsics, usually from COLMAP, which returns a sparse point cloud as a by-product and is often used to initialise 3DGS. Without them a pixel cannot be turned into a world ray and there is nothing for multi-view consistency to be consistent about.</p>",
