  "num": 31,
  "week": "W1",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: A convolution gives translation <strong>equivariance</strong>, and pooling adds translation <strong>invariance</strong>. Padding controls the <strong>output size</strong> and never the <strong>receptive field</strong>. Weight sharing reduces <strong>parameters and memory</strong> but not <strong>FLOPs</strong>, while local connectivity reduces <strong>both</strong>.</strong> <strong>Equivariance against invariance, precisely.</strong> Shift the input and a convolution's output shifts with it — the information about <em>where</em> is preserved, just moved. Pooling then discards position within its window, so a small shift leaves the output unchanged: that is invariance, and it is a deliberate destruction of information. Getting these two the wrong way round is the commonest error on this topic, because both words feel like “the network copes with translation”.</p><p><strong>Why padding cannot touch the receptive field.</strong> $P$ appears in $O = \\lfloor (I + 2P - K^{\\text{eff}})/S \\rfloor + 1$ and in no other formula. The recurrences $R_l = R_{l-1} + (K^{\\text{eff}}_l - 1)J_{l-1}$ and $J_l = J_{l-1}S_l$ do not mention it. Padding decides how many output positions exist, not how far each one can see.</p><p><strong>And why the two savings differ.</strong> Local connectivity removes <em>connections</em>, and a connection is both a weight and a multiply, so both counts fall. Weight sharing makes the remaining weights <em>equal</em>, which collapses the parameter count — a conv layer's size is independent of resolution — but the multiplication still happens at every position, so the FLOPs are untouched.</p><p><strong>Also accepted:</strong> “equivariant” / “invariant”; “how many outputs there are”; “memory” alone for the third; “both parameters and compute” for the last. <strong>Not accepted:</strong> “translation invariance” for the first blank — that is the answer to the second.</p>",
