  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: Fusion produces <strong>one joint</strong> output; coordination produces <strong>one per modality</strong>. CLIP is an example of <strong>coordination</strong>. Of the four possible outcomes, the one that justifies building the model is <strong>emergent</strong>, and the one that hides a useless second modality is <strong>dominated</strong>.</strong> <strong>Count the outputs — it is the reliable test.</strong> Fusion builds a shared representation and answers once. Coordination keeps the representations separate and merely constrains them to agree, so each modality still has its own output. CLIP's two encoders never exchange an activation; the contrastive loss compares their outputs. Coordination.</p><p><strong>Why that classification has a practical consequence.</strong> Because CLIP's encoders are independent, a million images can be embedded once and stored, and a text query answered with a single forward pass. A fusion model must run the joint network on every (image, text) pair, so retrieval over a large collection is impossible with one. The paradigm is not a taxonomy exercise; it determines what the model can be used for.</p><p><strong>The four outcomes, and why two of them are the ones to name.</strong> <em>Inconclusive</em>, <em>dominated</em>, <em>modulated</em>, <em>emergent</em>. Emergent — a percept derivable from neither modality alone, as in the McGurk effect — is the only one that repays the cost, and the cost is aligned paired data, which is the real bottleneck. Dominated is the dangerous one because it looks like success: the aggregate score is fine and one modality is doing all the work, which only an ablation reveals.</p><p><strong>Also accepted:</strong> “a single shared output” / “one output per modality”; “an emergent outcome”; “domination”.</p>",
