  "exam": 8,
  "num": 34,
  "week": "W8",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: Self-supervision manufactures a label by <strong>hiding</strong> part of the data. What is kept afterwards is <strong>the encoder</strong>, and the proxy head is <strong>discarded</strong>. The design requirement is that solving the task must <strong>require high-level understanding of the input</strong>. Pre-training is judged by <strong>downstream performance</strong>, never by <strong>accuracy on the proxy task</strong>.</strong> <strong>“Hiding” is the word to have.</strong> The label was always in the data; you removed something and remembered what you removed. Rotate an image and you know the angle. Mask a patch and you know the pixels. Cut it into tiles and you know the permutation. Nothing was annotated, and nothing needed to be.</p><p><strong>Why the requirement is a requirement and not an aspiration.</strong> A task solvable without understanding will be solved without understanding, and the model will learn the shortcut — chromatic aberration for absolute position, patch statistics for adjacency, edge continuity across a seam. Each was found by someone noticing it, each got a patch, and there is no procedure that enumerates the rest. That unbounded auditing burden is why the field left handcrafted tasks for one generic objective.</p><p><strong>And why the last blank follows from the one before it.</strong> A shortcut makes the proxy score go <em>up</em>. There is no signal inside the proxy's own numbers that anything is wrong, so the proxy score cannot be the measurement. Linear probing asks whether the information is linearly accessible; fine-tuning asks whether the encoder is a good initialisation. Both look downstream, which is the only place the answer lives.</p><p><strong>Also accepted:</strong> “removing” or “withholding” for the first; “the backbone”; “thrown away”; “require semantic understanding” or “not be solvable by low-level cues”; “linear probing or fine-tuning on a real task”.</p>",
