  "id": "e08q35",
  "exam": 8,
  "num": 35,
  "week": "W9",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: Feature extraction <strong>freezes</strong> the backbone and suits <strong>very limited</strong> target data; fine-tuning <strong>trains the whole network further</strong> and suits <strong>sufficiently large</strong> target data. The reason for the split is that <strong>unlocking more layers adds capacity, which a small dataset cannot support</strong>.</strong> <strong>The rule is about capacity against data, and it runs the opposite way to intuition.</strong> The instinct with a hard target task is to train more of the model. With a few hundred labels that is exactly wrong: more trainable parameters means more ways to fit the noise, and the overfitting does not merely fail to help — it <em>destroys</em> the general features you paid a large pre-training run to obtain. You can end up below where a frozen backbone would have left you.</p><p><strong>It is a continuum, not a switch.</strong> Unfreeze the last block; use adapters; use LoRA, which adds a little capacity without touching the original weights at all. The question is always “how much capacity will this much data support”, and the two named options are its endpoints.</p><p><strong>The same judgement, one lecture earlier.</strong> Week 8's advice on adapting a foundation model is to probe first and escalate only if the probe is clearly capacity-limited. Two lectures, one decision rule — and in both the failure mode of the aggressive choice is silent.</p><p><strong>Also accepted:</strong> “keeps the backbone fixed” / “updates every layer”; “scarce” and “plentiful”; “more trainable parameters overfit a small dataset and wreck the pre-trained features”.</p>",
