  "num": 39,
  "week": "W8",
  "explanation": "<p>Cut, shuffle, classify, discard.</p><p><strong>The last step is the one that is easy to leave off, and it is the one that generalises.</strong> The permutation classifier is scaffolding. Every self-supervised method in the lecture ends the same way — the rotation head, the context encoder's decoder, SimCLR's projection head, MAE's decoder, DINO's projection — all thrown away, all for the same reason: the objective needed something to be computed on, and the representation was always the product.</p><p><strong>Why the shuffle step carries a qualifier.</strong> Not any permutations: a subset chosen far apart in Hamming distance, so that no two classes can be confused by getting one tile wrong (Q21).</p><p><strong>And what the task is competing against.</strong> Context prediction is $8$-way — where is this patch relative to that one — and rotation is $4$-way. Jigsaw is the most informative of the three and also the one with the most ways to cheat, which is why it needs patch normalisation, jitter and channel dropping on top.</p>",
