  "id": "e08q40",
  "exam": 8,
  "num": 40,
  "week": "W11",
  "explanation": "<p>Capture, calibrate, optimise, render.</p><p><strong>Step (b) is the one that is not machine learning and is required anyway.</strong> Structure from motion — COLMAP in practice — recovers where each camera was and what kind of camera it was. Without it a pixel cannot be turned into a world-space ray, and with no rays there is no multi-view consistency for the optimisation to exploit. It also returns a sparse point cloud as a by-product, which is what 3DGS initialises its Gaussians from.</p><p><strong>Step (d) is where the two methods differ and nothing else does.</strong> NeRF optimises network weights; 3DGS optimises explicit primitives. The loss is the same photometric comparison against observed pixels, and the supervision in both cases is <em>only</em> pixel colours — no depth, no mesh, no 3D labels anywhere. Geometry is emergent, because one representation has to explain every training view at once.</p><p><strong>And the asymmetry in cost.</strong> Steps (c) and (b) happen once. Step (d) takes hours to days and is per scene. Step (a) — rendering — happens whenever someone moves the camera, which is why its cost is the one that decided which method is used interactively.</p>",
