  "exam": 8,
  "num": 41,
  "week": "W7",
  "explanation": "<p>Ordered by how much interaction each rung permits, and each step up costs parameters.</p><table class='xt'><tr><th></th><th><strong>what meets what</strong></th><th><strong>cost</strong></th></tr><tr><td>additive</td><td>nothing meets — each votes</td><td>linear</td></tr><tr><td>element-wise</td><td>feature $i$ with feature $i$</td><td>linear</td></tr><tr><td>bilinear</td><td>every feature with every feature</td><td>quadratic</td></tr><tr><td>tensor</td><td>the same, <em>plus</em> the unimodal terms and a bias</td><td>quadratic</td></tr></table><p><strong>The distinction between the last two is the appended $1$s, and it is not cosmetic.</strong> $[\\mathbf{x}_A,1]^{\\top}\\!\\cdot[\\mathbf{x}_B,1]$ has a row and a column carrying the bare $a_i$ and $b_j$, so a linear map over it can reach the bimodal terms, the unimodal terms and a bias. Plain bilinear fusion has the cross terms <em>only</em>, so it cannot express “ignore the caption and classify the image” — which is sometimes exactly the right answer.</p><p><strong>Element-wise is weaker than it looks.</strong> Multiplying index by index presumes the two feature spaces are aligned coordinate for coordinate, which nothing arranged. It is cheap and it is a strong assumption.</p><p><strong>And the trap that belongs here.</strong> Concatenating and applying one linear layer is <em>additive</em>: $W[\\mathbf{x}_A;\\mathbf{x}_B] = W_A\\mathbf{x}_A + W_B\\mathbf{x}_B$. If you want interaction you have to multiply somewhere.</p>",
