  "id": "e08q43",
  "exam": 8,
  "num": 43,
  "week": "W1",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Answer: input $\\mathbf{3072}$; $\\mathbf{36{,}864}$ weights</strong></p><p>\\[\\begin{aligned} \\text{input} &= 32 \\cdot 32 \\cdot 3 = 3072 \\\\ \\text{weights} &= 3072 \\cdot 12 = 36{,}864 \\qquad (+\\,12 \\text{ biases}) \\end{aligned}\\]</p><p><strong>Twelve hidden units. Thirty-six thousand weights.</strong> That is the number the lecture wants you to feel: a hidden layer too small to be of any use on the smallest image anyone trains on already costs more parameters than many complete convolutional networks. Scale to a modest $224\\times224$ image and the input is $150{,}528$, so the same twelve units would need $1.8\\times10^{6}$.</p><p><strong>Why it is worse than the count suggests.</strong> Those weights are not merely numerous, they are <em>unshared</em>: the weight looking at pixel $(0,0)$ learns nothing that helps the weight looking at $(0,1)$. A cat in the top-left corner and the same cat in the centre are, to this layer, entirely unrelated inputs, and the network must see both to learn both. Flattening has destroyed the two facts about images that matter — locality, and that a pattern means the same thing wherever it appears.</p><p><strong>The comparison to make.</strong> A $7\\times7\\times3\\times64$ convolution over a $224\\times224$ image costs $9{,}472$ parameters including biases (exam 03, Q43) — a quarter of what twelve dense units cost on an image fifty times smaller, and it sees the whole image. That gap is the argument for convolution in two numbers.</p>",
