  "id": "e09q04",
  "exam": 9,
  "num": 4,
  "week": "W2",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Disentangled attention.</strong> An ordinary transformer adds the content and position embeddings together before any attention is computed, so a single score conflates “these two words are related” with “these two positions are close”. DeBERTa keeps them apart and computes the attention from both a content-to-content and a content-to-position term, so the model can express “this word attends to whatever is two places to its left” separately from “this word attends to the word <em>deep</em>”.</p><p><strong>The other three are the other members of the family</strong>, and telling them apart is the question: (1) is the Compressive Transformer, (2) is ALBERT, (3) is Longformer.</p><p>Worth holding as one list, since they are easy to blur together:</p><table class='xt'><tr><td>DistilBERT</td><td>knowledge distillation from a larger BERT</td></tr><tr><td>ALBERT</td><td>embedding factorisation $+$ cross-layer parameter sharing</td></tr><tr><td>DeBERTa</td><td>disentangled content-versus-position attention</td></tr><tr><td>Longformer</td><td>local window $+$ global tokens, linear scaling</td></tr></table>",
