  "explanation": "<p>$N = 6$ encoder layers and $6$ decoder layers, with $d_{\\text{model}} = 512$, $h = 8$ and $d_k = 64$ — the base configuration of the 2017 paper.</p><p><strong>Why the number is worth knowing rather than looking up.</strong> It anchors every parameter estimate. One encoder layer is $4d^2$ in attention and $8d^2$ in the feed-forward block, so $12d^2 = 3.1\\times10^{6}$; six of them is about $1.9\\times10^{7}$, and the decoder's extra cross-attention adds more. That puts the base model in the range of $6.5\\times10^{7}$ parameters, which is the right order to have in mind when a question asks whether something is feasible.</p><p><strong>And it is a useful reference point for how much the field has moved.</strong> Six layers and $512$ dimensions was the state of the art for translation in 2017; modern language models are two orders of magnitude larger in both. The architecture is essentially unchanged.</p>",
