  "id": "e09q06",
  "exam": 9,
  "num": 6,
  "week": "W3",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>1st — pixels, not patches, and that is the whole distinction from ViT.</strong> iGPT flattens the image into a sequence of individual pixels and runs an autoregressive transformer over it, exactly as GPT does over tokens. It is the literal transplant of a language model to images, and it demonstrates that the architecture is not about language.</p><p><strong>2nd — two objectives were tried.</strong> Autoregressive next-pixel prediction, and a BERT-style masked-pixel objective. Both work; the paper reports both.</p><p><strong>The 3rd is false and belongs to ViT</strong>, which is the important contrast. Pixels give a sequence of length $HW$, so even a $32\\times32$ image is $1024$ tokens and anything larger is impossible — attention is quadratic. ViT's patches cut the length by $P^2$ and are what made transformers practical on real images.</p><p><strong>The 4th is false.</strong> Image generation moved to diffusion models, which is the subject of Week 10. iGPT's value now is as the demonstration that the transformer is modality-agnostic, and as the cautionary example of what happens when you tokenise too finely.</p>",
