  "exam": 9,
  "num": 7,
  "week": "W3",
  "explanation": "<p>$N_{\\text{tokens}} = n_t \\cdot n_h \\cdot n_w$, and full spatio-temporal attention is $O(N_{\\text{tokens}}^2)$.</p><p><strong>Why this is worse than it looks.</strong> The count is a <em>product</em>, so doubling the clip length doubles the tokens and <em>quadruples</em> the attention cost, and doubling the spatial resolution multiplies the tokens by four and the cost by sixteen. A clip of $32$ frames at $14\\times14$ patches is $6272$ tokens and about $4\\times10^{7}$ pairs — for one clip.</p><p><strong>Which is why the four variants exist.</strong> Full spatio-temporal attention scores best on Kinetics and is the expensive one. The factorised encoder wins on Epic Kitchens. Factorised self-attention splits space and time into separate sub-layers. Factorised dot-product attention is the most efficient, because it gives half the heads the spatial neighbourhood and half the temporal one <em>within a single operation</em>, and heads already run in parallel.</p><p><strong>Why not (b) or (c).</strong> Both describe factorisations — real methods, and not the token count of the full model, which is what the question asks.</p>",
