  "id": "e09q08",
  "exam": 9,
  "num": 8,
  "week": "W3",
  "explanation": "<p>All four, and the third is the one that keeps the claim honest.</p><p><strong>1st and 2nd.</strong> The attention terms are $2(hw)^2C$ globally and $2M^2hwC$ windowed, so the ratio is $hw/M^2$ — exactly the number of windows. With $M$ fixed as the image grows, the number of windows grows with it, so this is a change in the asymptotics rather than a constant factor: from quadratic in patches to linear.</p><p><strong>3rd — and it is the caveat most answers omit.</strong> The full costs are $4hwC^2 + 2(hw)^2C$ and $4hwC^2 + 2M^2hwC$. The projection term is identical in both and does not shrink at all. At moderate resolutions it can be the <em>larger</em> of the two, so quoting the window speed-up as the speed-up of the layer would be wrong.</p><p><strong>4th — and without it the whole thing fails.</strong> A fixed partition makes the window boundaries permanent walls: two patches on opposite sides are never in a window together, at any depth, so the model would be a stack of independent local transformers with no global receptive field. Shifting by $\\lfloor M/2 \\rfloor$ between blocks puts the old boundary through the middle of a new window, and alternating the two lets information cross the image while every individual attention stays $M^2$ in size.</p>",
