  "id": "e09q09",
  "exam": 9,
  "num": 9,
  "week": "W4",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Explicit</strong> grids store <em>what is there</em>: occupancy (a binary occupied/free flag) and ternary (which adds an <em>unknown</em> state for space never observed). <strong>Implicit</strong> grids store a function whose <em>level set</em> is the surface: a distance field, or a signed distance field where the sign says inside or outside.</p><p><strong>The 1st is the negation of the 2nd</strong>, and the words are worth pinning down: the surface is stated directly in an occupancy grid and only implied — as the zero crossing — in an SDF.</p><p><strong>The 4th has the ternary state wrong, and the correction matters.</strong> A ternary grid is occupied / free / <strong>unknown</strong>. Free and unknown are different claims: “I looked and there is nothing” against “I never looked”. For a robot deciding where it may drive, that distinction is the whole content of the map, and a binary occupancy grid cannot express it.</p><p><strong>And why the implicit ones win for completion.</strong> An SDF's gradient points towards the surface and its sign says which side you are on, so a network predicting one is fitting a smooth field with a usable derivative everywhere — against an occupancy grid's step function, which is flat almost everywhere and gives a completion network nothing to tell it <em>which way</em> the missing surface lies. TSDF then clamps the values to a band, for memory and for local updates.</p>",
