  "id": "e09q10",
  "exam": 9,
  "num": 10,
  "week": "W4",
  "explanation": "<p>Both address the same weakness — that a <em>single</em> ball radius is a single scale, and a point cloud has no single right scale.</p><p><strong>Multi-scale grouping (MSG).</strong> At each centroid, form several groups at several radii, run a PointNet in each, and <em>concatenate</em> the results. The feature then describes the neighbourhood at all of those scales at once.</p><p><strong>Multi-resolution grouping (MRG).</strong> Combine features from different <em>abstraction levels</em> of the hierarchy rather than different radii at one level. Cheaper, because it reuses work already done lower down.</p><p><strong>Why either is needed.</strong> Ball query gives a fixed <em>physical</em> radius, which is the right choice against $k$NN — but in a sparse region a small ball may contain two points and in a dense one, two hundred. MSG lets the network see both a tight and a loose neighbourhood and decide which is informative, which is also what makes PointNet++ robust to the varying density that input point dropout trains for.</p><p><strong>Why not (a) or (b).</strong> Sampling is farthest point sampling and pooling is max; neither is what M-S-G and M-R-G name.</p>",
