  "exam": 9,
  "num": 11,
  "week": "W4",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>1st — irregular.</strong> There is no grid, so there is no fixed neighbourhood and a standard convolution has nothing to slide over. That is the structural difficulty and the reason the whole family exists.</p><p><strong>3rd — variable density.</strong> A LiDAR scan is dense near the sensor and sparse far away, so the same object is described by a thousand points at ten metres and thirty at fifty. Any operation with a fixed point count sees a different thing at each distance, which is why ball query fixes a physical radius and why input point dropout is used during training.</p><p><strong>4th — sheer size.</strong> Hundreds of thousands of points per scan, which rules out anything quadratic in the point count without an approximation.</p><p><strong>The 2nd is false and is the defining property.</strong> A point cloud is a <em>set</em>: the order in the file is an artefact of how it was written, and any permutation is the same cloud. That is what forces a symmetric aggregation, and it is the first of the three invariances rather than a challenge in the same sense as the others — which is why it is worth noticing that the question lists it as a false statement about <em>order</em>, not about permutation.</p>",
