  "id": "e09q12",
  "exam": 9,
  "num": 12,
  "week": "W5",
  "explanation": "<p>A layer is <strong>anisotropic</strong> when the weight it puts on a neighbour depends on <em>content</em> — the two nodes' features, or the edge's own attributes — rather than only on the graph's shape.</p><p><strong>The dividing line, in one table.</strong> GCN's $c_{ij} = 1/\\sqrt{\\tilde D_{ii}\\tilde D_{jj}}$ is a function of two degrees: fixed the moment the graph is given, before a single feature is read. <em>Isotropic</em>. GAT's $\\alpha_{ij} = a(\\mathbf{x}_i,\\mathbf{x}_j)$ is computed from the features and changes as the representations change. <em>Anisotropic</em>. MPNN goes further and sends a learned vector, and can read the edge.</p><p><strong>Why the distinction earns a name.</strong> It decides whether the layer can treat two structurally identical neighbours differently. In a molecule, two carbon atoms bonded to the same atom look identical to a GCN and can be told apart by a GAT if their features differ — and by an MPNN even if only the <em>bonds</em> differ.</p><p><strong>Why not (a).</strong> Multi-head attention is about how many independent weightings are computed, not about what they depend on; a single-head GAT is already anisotropic.</p><p><strong>Why not (d).</strong> Degree normalisation is precisely the <em>isotropic</em> choice — it is what GCN does instead.</p>",
  "type": "multi",
