  "id": "e09q21",
  "exam": 9,
  "num": 21,
  "week": "W8",
  "explanation": "<p>The same quantity in two units, like metres and feet. $1$ bit $= \\ln 2 \\approx 0.693$ nats, because a bit uses $\\log_2$ and a nat uses $\\ln$.</p><p><strong>The 2nd is false and is the misconception the question exists for.</strong> Both measure information. Nothing about the underlying quantity changes; only the base of the logarithm does.</p><p><strong>The 3rd is false, and the direction is easy to get backwards.</strong> Converting bits to nats <em>multiplies by</em> $\\ln 2 \\approx 0.693$, not by $\\log_2 e \\approx 1.443$. So $8$ bits is $5.545$ nats — a <em>smaller</em> number, because a nat is a larger unit. Check it against a case you know: $\\ln 256 = 5.545$ and $\\log_2 256 = 8$.</p><p><strong>The 4th is false.</strong> $MI \\ge \\log N - \\mathcal{L}$ holds with whichever logarithm you use, provided you use the same one on both sides. The bound's content is unchanged.</p><p>Two values worth recognising on sight, because they recur: $\\ln 4096 = 12\\ln 2 = 8.318$ nats $= 12$ bits, which is a uniformly collapsed DINO run; and $\\ln 65{,}536 = 16\\ln 2 = 11.09$ nats $= 16$ bits, MoCo's queue.</p>",
