  "id": "e09q35",
  "exam": 9,
  "num": 35,
  "week": "W8",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: Contrastive learning needs <strong>many negatives</strong>, and creates <strong>false negatives</strong> that more data makes worse. Self-distillation removes the first problem and inherits <strong>collapse</strong>. That is cured by <strong>centering</strong> and <strong>sharpening</strong> <em>together</em>, because each one is the other's <strong>failure case</strong>.</strong> <strong>Three generations, each fixing the last one's flaw and acquiring its own.</strong> Handcrafted pretext tasks died of <em>shortcuts</em>. Contrastive learning replaced them with one generic objective and needs <em>many negatives</em> — SimCLR's batch of $8192$, MoCo's queue of $65{,}536$ — and manufactures <em>false</em> ones, because two photographs of different dogs are pushed apart by an objective that has no way to know they are both dogs.</p><p><strong>Why more data makes the second worse rather than better.</strong> As the dataset covers more of each class, the chance that a randomly drawn negative shares the anchor's class rises. Every knob that strengthens contrastive learning strengthens this too: a larger $N$ raises the InfoNCE ceiling and draws in more false negatives; a smaller $\\tau$ concentrates the loss on the nearest negatives, which is soft hard-negative mining and therefore also soft false-negative mining.</p><p><strong>And why the two cures are inseparable.</strong> Without negatives, $f(x) = \\mathbf{c}$ is a stationary point — the gradient $(P_s - P_t)/\\tau_s$ is zero when student and teacher agree, and they agree trivially if both are constant. <em>Centering</em> subtracts an EMA of the teacher's batch mean, killing the fixed-peak collapse and pushing towards uniform — which is the other collapse. <em>Sharpening</em>, via $\\tau_t = 0.04 < \\tau_s = 0.1$, pushes away from uniform and cannot touch a constant peaked teacher. Each is exactly the other's failure case, which is why naming one alone is half an answer.</p><p><strong>Also accepted:</strong> “a large number of negatives”; “negatives that are really positives”; “mode collapse”; “the other's blind spot” or “what the other cannot fix”.</p>",
