  "id": "e09q38",
  "exam": 9,
  "num": 38,
  "week": "W4",
  "explanation": "<p>Sample the centroids once, group at several radii around each, encode each group separately, concatenate.</p><p><strong>Why the sampling happens once and the grouping several times.</strong> The centroids define <em>where</em> the network is looking; the radii define <em>how widely</em>. Re-sampling per scale would give different centroids at each scale and nothing to concatenate — the whole point is that one point in the output carries a description of its neighbourhood at several sizes at once.</p><p><strong>Why several radii at all.</strong> A ball query fixes a physical radius, which is right against $k$NN because it means the same thing in a dense and a sparse region. But no single radius is right for a scene containing both a table leg and a wall, and MSG lets the network see both and decide. MRG is the cheaper alternative: combine features from several <em>abstraction levels</em> rather than several radii at one level.</p><p><strong>And the reason the shared PointNet comes before the concatenation.</strong> Each group must be reduced to a permutation-invariant vector <em>before</em> anything is combined; concatenating raw point sets would depend on the order they were listed in.</p>",
