  "id": "e09q39",
  "exam": 9,
  "num": 39,
  "week": "W9",
  "explanation": "<p>Encode both domains, classify the domain, add the task loss, backpropagate through the reversal.</p><p><strong>Why the batch must contain both domains.</strong> The domain classifier needs positive and negative examples of “source”, and the ratio matters — the same balance requirement as importance weighting, since an unbalanced classifier gives a systematically shifted signal.</p><p><strong>Why the task loss is on the source only.</strong> It is the labelled side. The target contributes to the <em>domain</em> loss alone, which is exactly what makes this unsupervised domain adaptation: no target labels anywhere.</p><p><strong>And the step that makes it one pass instead of two.</strong> The forward loss is a plain sum, $\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{task}} + \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{domain}}$. The gradient reversal layer negates what reaches the encoder, so a single optimiser minimising that sum produces a classifier that minimises the domain loss and an encoder that <em>maximises</em> it. The minimax lives in the layer rather than in the training loop.</p><p><strong>The direction to get right:</strong> the classifier is trained normally; it is the <em>encoder</em> that receives the negated gradient.</p>",
