  "id": "e09q40",
  "exam": 9,
  "num": 40,
  "week": "W10",
  "explanation": "<p>Start at the largest noise level, run several steps, descend, stop at the smallest.</p><p><strong>The direction is the thing to get right, and it is $\\sigma_L \\to \\sigma_1$.</strong> At a large $\\sigma$ the noised distribution is broad, so the learned score is trustworthy everywhere — and describes a heavily blurred version of the data. At a small $\\sigma$ it is faithful and reliable only near the data. Descending means the score is only ever consulted where it was trained, which is the entire reason for annealing. Starting small would mean trusting the score in exactly the region it knows nothing about.</p><p><strong>Carrying the state forward (a) is what makes it one procedure.</strong> The sample at the end of level $i$ initialises level $i-1$. Restarting from noise at each level would throw the work away.</p><p><strong>And the step size.</strong> $\\alpha_i = \\delta\\sigma_i^2/\\sigma_L^2$ — proportional to $\\sigma_i^2$, so halving $\\sigma$ quarters the step. The distribution at a smaller noise level has finer structure, and a step sized for the blurred version would stride straight over it.</p><p><strong>What each Langevin step is.</strong> $\\mathbf{x}_{t+1} \\leftarrow \\mathbf{x}_t + \\delta s_\\theta(\\mathbf{x}_t) + \\sqrt{2\\delta}\\eta_t$ — the score towards higher density, the noise to make it a sample rather than a mode.</p>",
