  "id": "e10q01",
  "exam": 10,
  "num": 1,
  "week": "W1",
  "explanation": "<p>Two true statements, and they are the same fact seen from the forward and the backward direction.</p><p><strong>The backward reading (1st).</strong> With $\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{x} + F(\\mathbf{x})$ the derivative is $\\partial\\mathbf{y}/\\partial\\mathbf{x} = I + \\partial F/\\partial\\mathbf{x}$. The $I$ is a path along which the gradient arrives at full strength no matter what $F$'s Jacobian does. Stack fifty blocks and the deepest gradient is a sum over every subset of blocks the path could pass through, one term of which passes through none of them. Vanishing requires <em>every</em> term to vanish, and the identity term cannot.</p><p><strong>The forward reading (2nd).</strong> The block only has to produce the <em>difference</em> between its input and the desired output. If the best thing to do at this depth is nothing, $F$ has to learn $0$, which is easy; without the skip it would have to learn the identity map through a non-linearity, which is not. That is why adding layers to a plain network could make it <em>worse</em> on the training set — a result that makes no sense until you notice the extra layers cannot cheaply do nothing.</p><p><strong>The 3rd is false.</strong> ResNet has BatchNorm in every block. Residuals fix the gradient path; normalisation fixes the scale of the activations. Different problems, both still present.</p><p><strong>The 4th is false, and it is the pleasing part.</strong> The skip is an addition. No weights, no bias, nothing to learn — provided the shapes match. Where they do not, at a change of resolution or width, a $1\\times1$ projection is inserted, and that is the only case in which a residual costs anything.</p>",
