  "id": "e10q02",
  "exam": 10,
  "num": 2,
  "week": "W1",
  "explanation": "<p>Averaging each channel over all positions turns a $C \\times H \\times W$ map into $C$ numbers, and $H$ and $W$ vanish from the answer. The classifier that follows sees $C$ inputs whatever the image size was, so the network runs on any resolution without being rebuilt.</p><p><strong>Why (a) is exactly backwards.</strong> It is the <em>flatten-then-dense</em> alternative whose parameter count is $C\\!\\cdot\\!H\\!\\cdot\\!W$ times the number of classes — in AlexNet and VGG, most of the network. Global average pooling has <em>no</em> parameters at all, which is also why it regularises: there is nothing in it to overfit with.</p><p><strong>Why (b) is a real confusion worth clearing.</strong> A $1\\times1$ convolution mixes across <em>channels</em> at each position and leaves $H$ and $W$ untouched. Global average pooling collapses $H$ and $W$ and leaves the channels separate. They act on orthogonal axes.</p><p><strong>Why (d) is false.</strong> It is a classification-head device; a segmentation head must keep spatial resolution, which is precisely what this destroys.</p><p>The one-line reason it appears in every modern classifier: it makes the head's cost independent of the input, and forces each channel to mean something globally rather than at a position.</p>",
