  "week": "W1",
  "explanation": "<p>This is VGG's central argument, and all four consequences hold at once.</p><p><strong>Same receptive field (1st).</strong> Each $3\\times3$ adds one pixel of reach on every side, so after three layers a unit sees $1 + 3\\times2 = 7$ pixels across. Identical to one $7\\times7$.</p><p><strong>Fewer parameters (2nd).</strong> With $C$ channels throughout, three $3\\times3$ layers cost $3 \\cdot 9C^2 = 27C^2$; one $7\\times7$ costs $49C^2$. About $45\\%$ less, for the same reach.</p><p><strong>Two extra non-linearities (3rd), and this is the part that matters most.</strong> Three linear maps with nothing between them compose into one linear map, so without the ReLUs the deep version would be strictly <em>less</em> expressive, not more. It is the activations between them that make the stack a richer function class than the single wide kernel.</p><p><strong>More activation memory (4th), and this is the cost nobody quotes.</strong> Backpropagation needs the input of every layer to compute that layer's weight gradient, so three layers store three feature maps where one stores one. Parameters fell; the training-time memory footprint rose. Depth is cheap in weights and expensive in activations, which is why gradient checkpointing exists.</p>",
