  "id": "e10q04",
  "exam": 10,
  "num": 4,
  "week": "W2",
  "explanation": "<p>Take $\\mathbf{q}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ with independent, zero-mean, unit-variance components. Then $\\mathbf{q}\\cdot\\mathbf{k}$ is a sum of $d_k$ such products, so it has mean $0$ and variance $d_k$ — typical magnitude $\\sqrt{d_k}$. At $d_k = 64$ the logits are routinely $\\pm 8$ before anything has been learned.</p><p><strong>What that does to the softmax.</strong> A softmax over logits spread by $\\pm 8$ is already almost a one-hot vector, and its gradient is almost exactly zero: $\\partial\\text{softmax}$ contains $p_i(1 - p_i)$, which vanishes when $p$ is peaked. Training would begin in a saturated regime and never leave it. Dividing by $\\sqrt{d_k}$ restores unit variance regardless of the head width, which is what makes the head width a free choice.</p><p><strong>Why not (b).</strong> The softmax normalises, and it does so whatever you scale by. Scaling changes the <em>sharpness</em>, not the sum.</p><p><strong>Why not (c).</strong> The heads are handled by $d_k = d_{\\text{model}}/h$ and by the output projection $W^O$. The $\\sqrt{d_k}$ would be there with a single head.</p><p><strong>Why not (d).</strong> The mask is $-\\infty$ added before the softmax; scaling neither helps nor harms it.</p>",
