  "id": "e10q05",
  "exam": 10,
  "num": 5,
  "week": "W2",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>The construction (1st).</strong> Start with the alphabet as the vocabulary, count adjacent symbol pairs in the corpus, merge the most frequent into a new symbol, repeat until the vocabulary reaches the size you wanted. Frequent words end up as single tokens; rare ones remain in pieces.</p><p><strong>Why the vocabulary can be fixed <em>and</em> complete (2nd, 4th).</strong> The characters are never removed, so any string can be spelled — in the worst case one character at a time. That is the property that makes the unknown-token symbol unnecessary: there is no input BPE cannot represent, only inputs it represents inefficiently. A word-level vocabulary must choose a cut-off and send everything past it to <code>[UNK]</code>, which destroys information irreversibly.</p><p><strong>Why the 3rd is false, and it is the tell for whether you have the idea.</strong> “Unbelievably” is likely to become <code>un</code> + <code>believ</code> + <code>ably</code>. Splitting is not a failure mode — it is the mechanism. One token per word is exactly what BPE gives up in order to get an open vocabulary out of a closed one.</p><p>The trade the whole idea rests on: character-level is complete but produces long sequences; word-level is short but incomplete. Subwords are the compromise, and the merge count is the dial between them.</p>",
