  "id": "e10q06",
  "exam": 10,
  "num": 6,
  "week": "W2",
  "explanation": "<p>Each token is represented by <em>two</em> vectors: a content vector and a relative-position vector. The attention score is then a sum of interaction terms — content-to-content, content-to-position and position-to-content — rather than one dot product between vectors that have had position added into them.</p><p><strong>Why separating them helps.</strong> In the original transformer the position is <em>added</em> to the embedding, so a single vector carries both what the token is and where it sits, and one dot product has to serve both questions. Whether “bank” should attend to “river” is a content question; whether it should attend to the token three places to its left is a position question. Disentangling lets the model answer them with different parameters.</p><p><strong>Why (a) is the thing being fixed</strong>, not the fix — that is the original design.</p><p><strong>Why (b) is false and would be fatal.</strong> Attention is permutation-equivariant; strip position out and the model cannot tell a sentence from its shuffling. DeBERTa uses <em>more</em> positional machinery, not less.</p><p><strong>Why (c) is false.</strong> It belongs to a different family of hybrids; DeBERTa's change is inside the attention score.</p><p>Note the recurring theme: the improvement is to <em>relative</em> position, as in Transformer-XL and Swin's relative position bias. Absolute encodings tie a token to an index; relative ones encode the distance, which is what the linguistic relationship usually depends on.</p>",
