  "id": "e10q07",
  "exam": 10,
  "num": 7,
  "week": "W3",
  "explanation": "<p>token: <span>all four</span>]</p><p>All four are true, and the fourth is the one people hesitate over.</p><p><strong>Learned and prepended (1st).</strong> A single vector $\\mathbf{x}_{\\text{class}}$, a parameter of the model, placed in front of the $N$ patch embeddings to give a sequence of length $N+1$. Borrowed straight from BERT's <code>[CLS]</code>.</p><p><strong>It gets a position embedding too (2nd).</strong> $E_{\\text{pos}} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{(N+1)\\times D}$ — the table has one row per sequence element and the class token is a sequence element. Harmless: its “position” is a constant, and the model learns whatever it wants that row to mean.</p><p><strong>Attention is bidirectional and unmasked (3rd).</strong> There is no causal mask in an encoder, so the class token reads every patch and every patch reads it. That is what lets it accumulate a summary and, in the other direction, broadcast global context back to the patches.</p><p><strong>Its output is the classification input (4th).</strong> The head is an MLP on that one output vector.</p><p>The obvious objection, worth having an answer to: why not just average the patch outputs? Because a learned token can attend <em>selectively</em> — it can weight the two patches containing the object and ignore the sky — where an average cannot. Global average pooling over patch outputs does work about as well in practice, which is a nice illustration of how little separates the two.</p>",
