  "id": "e10q09",
  "exam": 10,
  "num": 9,
  "week": "W3",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>The Hungarian matching (1st, 2nd).</strong> DETR emits a fixed set of $N$ predictions and the image has some smaller number of objects. Before the loss can be computed, each ground-truth box must be assigned to a prediction, and the assignment chosen is the one minimising total matching cost — solved exactly by the Hungarian algorithm. It is recomputed every step, because as the model changes, which query is best placed to explain which object changes with it.</p><p><strong>The no-object class (3rd).</strong> The $N - |\\text{objects}|$ unmatched predictions are trained towards $\\varnothing$. That is what stops the model from emitting $N$ boxes on every image, and it plays the role that background does in an anchor-based detector.</p><p><strong>Why the 4th is false, and it is the whole point of the design.</strong> The matching is recomputed, so relabelling the ground truth in a different order changes which prediction each box is assigned to and leaves the loss value identical. The loss is a function of the two <em>sets</em>. That permutation invariance is what removes the need for anchors and for non-maximum suppression: duplicates are impossible because a second box explaining the same object is unmatched, and unmatched means $\\varnothing$.</p>",
