  "num": 13,
  "week": "W5",
  "explanation": "<p>$\\hat{A} = \\tilde{D}^{-1/2}\\tilde{A}\\tilde{D}^{-1/2}$ with $\\tilde{A} = A + I$.</p><p><strong>The tilde is the self-loop (1st).</strong> Without $+I$, a node's new representation would be built from its neighbours and <em>not</em> from itself — it would forget what it was. Adding the identity is the cheap way to include the node in its own neighbourhood, and it is why $\\tilde{D}_{ii} = \\deg(i) + 1$.</p><p><strong>The normalisation is about scale (2nd).</strong> With raw $A$, a node with a hundred neighbours sums a hundred vectors and one with two sums two. The activations then differ by a factor of fifty for reasons that have nothing to do with the features. Symmetric normalisation divides each edge by $\\sqrt{\\tilde{d}_i \\tilde{d}_j}$, so degree stops dominating.</p><p><strong>The fixed point is the cost (3rd).</strong> $\\hat{A}$ is a smoothing operator, and applying a smoothing operator repeatedly converges. Its dominant eigenvector is proportional to $\\sqrt{\\deg + 1}$, so a deep GCN's representations encode degree and nothing else. Note the precision: they do not become <em>identical</em> — that is the limit of the random-walk normalisation $\\tilde{D}^{-1}\\tilde{A}$. Saying “they all become the same vector” is the common half-right answer.</p><p><strong>Why the 4th is false.</strong> Permute the nodes and $\\hat{A}$ is permuted the same way, so the output is permuted and nothing else changes. Equivariance is structural here, not something the normalisation could break.</p>",
