  "exam": 10,
  "num": 14,
  "week": "W5",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Sampling (2nd).</strong> A node's two-hop neighbourhood in a social graph can be most of the graph, so GraphSAGE samples a fixed number at each hop — say $25$ then $10$. The cost per node becomes a constant instead of a function of the degree distribution, which is what makes mini-batch training possible on a graph that does not fit in memory.</p><p><strong>The aggregators (4th).</strong> Mean, LSTM and pooling, in the paper. The LSTM one is the interesting case: it is <em>not</em> permutation invariant, so it is applied to randomly permuted neighbourhoods to average the order dependence away. A deliberate breach of the invariance, bought back statistically, in exchange for more expressive power.</p><p><strong>Why the 1st and 3rd are false, and they are the same mistake.</strong> GraphSAGE learns <em>aggregator functions</em>, not one embedding per node. Those functions apply to any neighbourhood, so a node that appears after training — a new user, a new paper — gets an embedding from one forward pass. That is what <em>inductive</em> means, and it is the property the paper is named for (SAmple and aggreGatE). The alternative, transductive methods that learn a lookup table of node embeddings, must be retrained when the graph grows.</p>",
