  "num": 15,
  "week": "W5",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>The coefficient depends on both endpoints (1st).</strong> $e_{ij} = \\text{LeakyReLU}(\\mathbf{a}^\\top[W\\mathbf{h}_i \\,\\Vert\\, W\\mathbf{h}_j])$, then a softmax over $j \\in \\mathcal{N}(i)$. So how much $j$ contributes to $i$ depends on what both of them contain — where GCN's weight $1/\\sqrt{\\tilde d_i \\tilde d_j}$ depends only on the <em>structure</em> and would be the same if every feature vector were replaced.</p><p><strong>The softmax keeps the graph essential (3rd).</strong> It is normalised over the neighbourhood, not over all nodes. That is what separates GAT from a transformer: a transformer's softmax runs over everything, so it needs no graph and costs $O(N^2)$; GAT's runs over the edges, so it needs the graph and costs $O(|\\mathcal{E}|)$. Attention over a given sparsity pattern rather than attention discovering its own.</p><p><strong>The head convention (4th).</strong> Concatenate in the hidden layers, so $K$ heads of width $F'$ give $KF'$ features; average at the output layer, where concatenating would multiply the number of classes.</p><p><strong>Why the 2nd is false.</strong> Equal weights are precisely what GAT replaces. A GCN can be recovered as the special case where every learned coefficient happens to come out equal.</p>",
