  "id": "e10q16",
  "exam": 10,
  "num": 16,
  "week": "W6",
  "explanation": "<p>Within a structured batch of $P$ classes $\\times$ $K$ samples, each element in turn is the anchor, and only its <em>hardest</em> positive (furthest same-class sample) and <em>hardest</em> negative (nearest different-class sample) are used. One triplet per anchor, $B$ per batch.</p><p><strong>Why fewer is better here.</strong> Batch-all forms all $B(K-1)(B-K)$ triplets, and the overwhelming majority already satisfy the margin, so they contribute exactly zero to the loss and zero to the gradient — but they are still averaged in, which drives the mean loss towards zero and dilutes the signal from the few triplets that matter. Batch-hard keeps only triplets that are by construction active.</p><p><strong>Why (b) is exactly backwards.</strong> Batch-all uses far more; that is what makes it batch-all.</p><p><strong>Why (c) is false.</strong> The margin is what defines hardness. Remove it and there is nothing to be hard with respect to.</p><p><strong>Why (d) is false.</strong> A memory bank belongs to MoCo-style contrastive learning. Batch-hard mines within the batch, which is why the batch must be <em>structured</em> — randomly drawn batches would often contain no positive pair at all.</p><p>The caveat to remember alongside it: the hardest negative in the whole dataset is usually a labelling error or a genuine near-duplicate, and training on those collapses the encoder. Mining hard <em>within a batch</em> is a soft version of semi-hard mining, and that is why it works where global hard mining does not.</p>",
