  "exam": 10,
  "num": 17,
  "week": "W6",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Normalise, and the logit becomes an angle (2nd).</strong> Set $\\lVert W_j \\rVert = 1$ and $\\lVert \\phi \\rVert = 1$. Then $W_j^\\top\\phi = \\cos\\theta_j$ exactly, and the classifier's score for class $j$ <em>is</em> the cosine of the angle to that class's direction.</p><p><strong>The margin lives in the angle (3rd).</strong> The target logit becomes $\\cos(\\theta_y + m)$. Because the margin is added <em>inside</em> the cosine it corresponds to a constant angular gap on the hypersphere, the same everywhere — unlike a margin subtracted from the cosine, whose effect on the angle depends on where you are on the curve.</p><p><strong>The scale is a necessity, not a knob (4th).</strong> Cosines are confined to $[-1, 1]$, so the largest logit gap the softmax can ever see is $2$, and $e^2/(e^2 + \\ldots)$ can never approach $1$. The loss would be bounded away from zero for a perfect classifier. Multiplying by $s \\approx 64$ restores the dynamic range.</p><p><strong>Why the 1st is false, and it is the reason ArcFace is used at all.</strong> There is no mining and there are no triplets. It is an ordinary softmax over classes, trained on single images with class labels, with the geometry changed so that the learned features are angularly separated. That is the whole appeal: the discriminative power of metric learning without the sampling machinery.</p>",
