  "id": "e10q18",
  "exam": 10,
  "num": 18,
  "week": "W6",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>The definition (1st).</strong> The label space at test time is not the label space at training time. A face-recognition system is deployed and new people join; a retrieval index gains products daily. The identities you must handle were unknown when the weights were fixed.</p><p><strong>Why a classifier is structurally the wrong tool (3rd).</strong> A softmax over $C$ known classes is a probability distribution over those $C$ — it sums to one by construction. Present a class it has never seen and it must still distribute all of its mass among the known ones, usually confidently. Not a calibration bug to be fixed with temperature: the output space has no symbol for “none of these”.</p><p><strong>Why the 2nd is false.</strong> There is no number of classes at which the world stops producing new ones. Every additional class also makes the softmax larger and slower and demands retraining of the final layer.</p><p><strong>Why the 4th is false, and it is the tempting one.</strong> A “background” or “unknown” class requires training examples of <em>everything that is not a known class</em>, which is not a sampleable set. It also makes the decision one-versus-rest against an incoherent rest.</p><p>The remedy the lecture gives: stop classifying. Learn an <em>embedding</em> in which distance means similarity, and at test time compare the query against a gallery. Adding an identity becomes adding a row to the gallery — no retraining, no architecture change — and rejecting an unknown becomes thresholding the nearest distance.</p>",
