  "id": "e10q19",
  "exam": 10,
  "num": 19,
  "week": "W7",
  "explanation": "<p>Each modality is encoded independently to a decision or a high-level feature, and only those are combined — by averaging the logits, by voting, or by a small joint head.</p><p><strong>What it buys.</strong> Modularity. The encoders can be trained separately, replaced separately, and the system degrades gracefully when one modality is missing at run time, because the others still produce their own decisions.</p><p><strong>What it costs, and this is the exam-worthy half.</strong> Cross-modal interaction. If deciding whether a caption matches an image requires relating <em>this word</em> to <em>that region</em>, late fusion cannot: by the time the two representations meet, each has been compressed to a summary and the individual elements are gone.</p><p><strong>Why (a) is early fusion</strong> — concatenating raw inputs before any encoder. Maximum interaction, and every failure that follows from forcing one encoder to handle two very different statistics.</p><p><strong>Why (b) is hybrid or intermediate fusion.</strong></p><p><strong>Why (c) is a training schedule</strong>, not a fusion point at all.</p><p>The rule of thumb: fuse early when the modalities are tightly coupled and aligned (RGB and depth of the same scene); fuse late when they are loosely related or may be missing.</p>",
