  "id": "e10q20",
  "exam": 10,
  "num": 20,
  "week": "W7",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>The objective (2nd, 3rd).</strong> A batch of $N$ image–caption pairs gives an $N \\times N$ similarity matrix. The $N$ diagonal entries are the true pairs and the $N^2 - N$ off-diagonal entries are negatives, and a symmetric cross-entropy is applied across rows and across columns. The batch supplies its own negatives, which is why CLIP's batch size ($32{,}768$) is part of the method rather than an implementation detail.</p><p><strong>Why the 1st and 4th are false, and they are one mistake.</strong> CLIP is a <em>coordination</em> model: two encoders, two separate output vectors, joined only by the constraint that matching pairs land near one another in a shared space. There is never a single vector describing the image and the caption together. A fusion model would build one — and would then be unable to do what CLIP is famous for, since retrieval requires embedding a million images once and comparing each new query against them by dot product. Fusion would need a forward pass per <em>pair</em>.</p><p>The distinction is worth being able to state in one line: fusion produces <em>one</em> representation of several modalities; coordination produces <em>several</em>, held in correspondence. Zero-shot classification then falls out — write “a photo of a {class}” for each candidate label, embed the sentences, and take the nearest.</p>",
