  "exam": 10,
  "num": 21,
  "week": "W8",
  "explanation": "<p>All four are true, and together they are the whole design.</p><p><strong>The queue decouples $N$ from the batch (1st).</strong> SimCLR's negatives are the other elements of the batch, so more negatives means a larger batch and the activations of every one of them must be held for the backward pass — which is why it needs TPU pods. MoCo stores $65{,}536$ <em>feature vectors</em> with no gradient attached: $65{,}536 \\times 128 \\times 4$ bytes, about $32$ MB, on any GPU.</p><p><strong>Momentum encoder (2nd).</strong> $\\theta_k \\leftarrow m\\theta_k + (1-m)\\theta_q$ with $m = 0.999$.</p><p><strong>Why the momentum is needed (3rd).</strong> The queue is a rolling window over the last several thousand batches, so the oldest keys in it were produced by an encoder thousands of steps out of date. If the key encoder moved as fast as the query encoder, the queue would be a mixture of incomparable representations and the dot products would be meaningless. A very slow encoder makes the whole queue approximately consistent. The ablation is striking: $m = 0.999$ works, $m = 0.9$ degrades badly, $m = 0$ fails outright.</p><p><strong>No gradient into the queue (4th).</strong> Keys are detached — that is exactly what makes them cheap, and it is why the key encoder cannot be trained by backpropagation and must be updated by the moving average instead. The four statements are a chain: cheap negatives require detached keys, detached keys require a separate encoder, a separate encoder requires consistency, and consistency requires momentum.</p>",
