  "id": "e10q22",
  "exam": 10,
  "num": 22,
  "week": "W8",
  "explanation": "<p>The head is a small MLP $g$ mapping the backbone's output $h$ to $z = g(h)$; the contrastive loss is applied to $z$, and $h$ is what is kept.</p><p><strong>Why throwing away the layer you trained is right.</strong> The loss demands invariance to the augmentations — colour jitter, cropping, grayscale. Applied directly to $h$, it would force $h$ to discard colour, because colour is what the augmentation destroys. But colour is useful for almost every downstream task. Interposing $g$ lets <em>it</em> absorb the invariance: $h$ may keep the colour information, $g$ may drop it, and the loss is satisfied. The measurement in the paper is unambiguous — linear probing on $h$ beats linear probing on $z$ by a wide margin, even though $z$ is what was optimised.</p><p><strong>Why the 1st is false</strong> — it is the opposite of the finding, and the finding is the point of the ablation.</p><p><strong>Why the 3rd is false.</strong> It does reduce the dimension, but that is incidental; a head that kept the dimension would give the same benefit.</p><p><strong>Why the 4th is false.</strong> It is discarded, and a fresh task head is attached to $h$.</p><p>The general lesson, which recurs in MAE's decoder and in DINO's head: <em>the part of the network that serves the pretext task is not the part you keep</em>. Build somewhere for the task-specific distortion to live, and then remove it.</p>",
