  "exam": 10,
  "num": 23,
  "week": "W8",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>The high mask ratio (1st).</strong> About $75\\%$, against BERT's $15\\%$. Images are far more redundant than text: at $15\\%$ a patch can be filled in by interpolating its neighbours, which teaches nothing about objects. Removing three quarters makes the task require some understanding of what is depicted.</p><p><strong>The decoder is discarded (4th).</strong> It is deliberately lightweight and exists only to make the pretext task well posed. The encoder is the product.</p><p><strong>Why the 2nd is false, and it is the efficiency trick.</strong> The encoder is applied to the <em>visible patches only</em> — one quarter of the sequence. Attention is quadratic, so processing a quarter of the tokens costs about a sixteenth as much, and that is what makes MAE pre-training cheap enough to be practical. Mask tokens are inserted only at the decoder, together with position embeddings so it knows where the holes were.</p><p><strong>Why the 3rd is false.</strong> The loss is computed on the masked patches alone. Including the visible ones would let the model spend capacity on copying its own input, which is free and uninformative.</p><p>Both false statements describe BERT's arrangement, which is where the confusion comes from: BERT does feed mask tokens through the encoder. MAE's two changes — much higher ratio, encoder sees only what is visible — are what adapt the idea to a redundant, continuous modality.</p>",
