  "id": "e10q24",
  "exam": 10,
  "num": 24,
  "week": "W9",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>The two remedies named (3rd, 4th).</strong> <em>Rehearsal</em> keeps a small memory of earlier examples — or generates them from a model of the old data — and mixes them into each new batch, so the old task keeps contributing gradient. <em>Regularisation</em>, of which EWC is the canonical case, estimates how much each parameter mattered for the old task (via the Fisher information) and adds a quadratic penalty on moving it, so learning the new task is steered through the directions the old task did not care about.</p><p><strong>Why the 1st is inverted, and it is the trap.</strong> Forgetting is <em>plasticity</em> winning: the network is too willing to change. Stability winning is the opposite failure — a model so protected that it cannot learn the new task at all. Both are failure modes of the same dilemma, and mixing up which is which makes the remedies look arbitrary.</p><p><strong>Why the 2nd is false, and worth thinking through.</strong> A smaller learning rate slows the forgetting and slows the learning by the same factor. It moves along the trade-off curve rather than improving it. What the real methods do is make the trade-off <em>selective</em> — protect the parameters that matter and leave the rest free — which a global learning rate cannot express.</p><p>The third family, for completeness: parameter isolation — give each task its own subnetwork, by masking or by growing the model. Forgetting becomes impossible by construction, and the cost is a model that grows with the number of tasks.</p>",
