  "id": "e10q25",
  "exam": 10,
  "num": 25,
  "week": "W9",
  "explanation": "<p>Pre-training is supposed to help. Negative transfer is when it does the reverse — the model initialised from the source ends up worse than one trained from scratch — because the features the source learned are wrong for the target and the optimiser must first undo them.</p><p><strong>When to expect it.</strong> Distant domains (natural images to medical scans or to LiDAR range images), and abundant target data, where whatever the source offered is worth less than the freedom to learn the right features directly. The rule of thumb is that transfer helps most exactly where labels are scarcest, which is also where it is hardest to detect that it has hurt.</p><p><strong>Why (a), (c) and (d) are other things with their own names.</strong> (a) is distillation or model compression. (c) is domain-adversarial training, a <em>method</em>, and its gradient reversal is deliberate rather than a failure. (d) is catastrophic forgetting — which is about the <em>source</em> task degrading, whereas negative transfer is about the <em>target</em> task degrading. Same two tasks, opposite direction of harm, and they are routinely swapped.</p><p>Where it sits in the week's argument: transfer learning is offered as one of the three escapes from the vicious cycle, and negative transfer is the reason it is an escape with a condition attached rather than a free one.</p>",
