  "id": "e10q26",
  "exam": 10,
  "num": 26,
  "week": "W10",
  "explanation": "<p>The problem: the ELBO contains an expectation over $\\mathbf{z} \\sim q_\\phi(\\mathbf{z}\\mid \\mathbf{x})$, and $\\phi$ is what you are differentiating with respect to. Sampling is not a differentiable operation, so backpropagation stops at the sampler.</p><p><strong>The fix (3rd).</strong> Write $\\mathbf{z} = \\boldsymbol{\\mu}_\\phi + \\boldsymbol{\\sigma}_\\phi \\odot \\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}$ with $\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon} \\sim \\mathcal{N}(0, I)$. The randomness is now an <em>input</em> drawn outside the graph, and everything between $\\phi$ and $\\mathbf{z}$ is an affine function of it. The gradient flows through $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and $\\boldsymbol{\\sigma}$; nothing has to be differentiated through a random draw, because there is no longer a random draw in the path.</p><p><strong>Why the 1st is false.</strong> The KL is a separate term of the ELBO and has a closed form for Gaussians. The trick concerns the reconstruction term's expectation.</p><p><strong>Why the 2nd is false.</strong> The decoder was already deterministic given $\\mathbf{z}$. What was stochastic was $\\mathbf{z}$ itself, and it still is — the randomness was relocated, not removed.</p><p><strong>Why the 4th is false, and it is the reverse of the truth.</strong> A Gaussian is precisely the case where the trick is available, because a Gaussian is an affine transform of a standard normal. Distributions without such a form — discrete latents above all — are exactly the ones that cannot use it, and need Gumbel-softmax or a score-function estimator instead.</p><p>Worth noting even though it was not one of the options: the alternative estimator, REINFORCE, works for any distribution and has variance so high that it is usually unusable. The trick is valued for the variance reduction as much as for existing at all.</p>",
