  "id": "e10q31",
  "exam": 10,
  "num": 31,
  "week": "W1",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: Momentum accumulates <strong>past gradients</strong>, so progress along a consistent direction <strong>builds up</strong>. Adam divides in addition by <strong>a running estimate of each gradient's magnitude</strong>. That makes the effective step <strong>smaller</strong> for a parameter whose gradients are large, and what both of them cost is <strong>extra state stored per parameter</strong>.</strong> <strong>Momentum is an exponential average of the gradient, and that is what makes it useful in a ravine.</strong> Across a narrow valley the gradient flips sign every step and the averaged contributions cancel; along the valley floor it points the same way each time and the contributions add. So the same mechanism damps the oscillation and accelerates the useful direction, without needing to know which is which.</p><p><strong>Adam's second moment is a per-parameter scale, not a second learning rate.</strong> Dividing the step by $\\sqrt{\\hat v_t}$ means a parameter whose gradients are consistently large takes small steps and one whose gradients are tiny takes comparatively large ones. That is why Adam works without tuning when different layers or embeddings see wildly different gradient magnitudes — sparse embeddings being the standard example.</p><p><strong>And the cost is memory, which matters at scale.</strong> Momentum stores one extra tensor the size of the parameters; Adam stores two. Training a model with $N$ parameters under Adam needs roughly $3N$ floats of optimiser and weight state before a single activation is stored, which is why optimiser state is a line item in every large-model memory budget and why SGD with momentum survives where memory is tight.</p><p><strong>Also accepted:</strong> “an exponential moving average of the gradients”; “accelerates” or “is reinforced”; “the square root of an average of squared gradients” / “the second moment”; “smaller”; “one or two extra tensors per parameter” or “optimiser memory”.</p>",
