  "id": "e10q32",
  "exam": 10,
  "num": 32,
  "week": "W3",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: A plain ViT attends <strong>globally</strong>, at a cost that grows <strong>quadratically</strong> in the number of patches. Swin attends <strong>within a fixed-size window</strong> instead, which makes the cost <strong>linear</strong>. The price is that information crosses the image only <strong>over several blocks</strong>, which is why the partition is <strong>shifted</strong> in every other block.</strong> <strong>Why the cost really is linear, which is the part worth being able to derive.</strong> Global attention scores $hw$ patches against $hw$: $(hw)^2$ pairs. Window attention scores each patch against the $M^2$ in its own window: $hw \\cdot M^2$ pairs. The crucial move is that $M$ is a <em>constant</em> — held at $7$ whatever the image size — so the second expression is linear in $hw$ while the first is quadratic. The speed-up factor $hw/M^2$ is the number of windows, and it grows with the image, which is exactly the regime where it is needed.</p><p><strong>Why the shift is not optional.</strong> A fixed partition makes the window boundaries permanent walls: two patches either side are never in a window together at any depth, so the network would be a stack of unrelated local transformers. Displacing the grid by $\\lfloor M/2 \\rfloor$ puts the old boundary through the middle of a new window, and alternating W-MSA and SW-MSA lets information cross the whole image within a few blocks.</p><p><strong>The general shape of the trade.</strong> You give up the guarantee that any pair can interact in <em>one</em> layer, and get it back over depth — which is precisely the bargain a CNN has always made. Swin is a ViT that has re-accepted locality as an inductive bias.</p><p><strong>Also accepted:</strong> “over all patches” / “everywhere”; “with the square of”; “inside local windows”; “linearly” or “proportionally”; “after several layers” or “gradually, with depth”; “displaced” or “moved by half a window”.</p>",
