  "id": "e10q33",
  "exam": 10,
  "num": 33,
  "week": "W4",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: PointNet is invariant to permutation because it applies <strong>a shared per-point MLP</strong> followed by <strong>a symmetric function — max pooling</strong>. Its limitation is that it has no <strong>local structure</strong>. PointNet++ restores that by <strong>applying PointNet recursively on neighbourhoods</strong>. The T-Net is there to handle <strong>rigid transformations of the input</strong>, and its output is regularised towards <strong>an orthogonal matrix</strong>.</strong> <strong>Why that pair of operations gives invariance, and why both halves are needed.</strong> The MLP is applied to each point independently with the same weights, so relabelling the points relabels the outputs and nothing else. Max pooling over the point axis then discards the labelling entirely, because the maximum of a set does not depend on the order it was written in. Either half alone fails: a per-point MLP followed by concatenation is order-dependent, and max pooling over raw coordinates throws away everything.</p><p><strong>Why max rather than sum or mean.</strong> Max selects; sum and mean accumulate. Empirically max makes each output dimension behave like a detector for one kind of local structure, and the resulting representation is robust to points being added or dropped — important for a sensor whose point count varies shot to shot.</p><p><strong>The limitation, stated precisely.</strong> Every point is processed alone and then everything is pooled at once. There is no intermediate scale, so PointNet has a global descriptor and per-point features and nothing in between — no notion of “this cluster of points is a wheel”. PointNet++ builds that by sampling centroids, grouping with a ball query, and running a small PointNet on each group; recursion supplies the hierarchy a CNN gets from strided layers.</p><p><strong>And the orthogonality penalty is not decoration.</strong> The T-Net predicts a $64\\times64$ feature transform, and $\\lVert I - AA^\\top\\rVert_F^2$ pushes it towards a rotation. Without it, the network can and does learn a degenerate transform that collapses the feature space — an easy way to reduce the loss that destroys the representation.</p><p><strong>Also accepted:</strong> “a shared MLP on every point”; “max pooling” or “a symmetric aggregation”; “no local neighbourhood structure”; “running PointNet on grouped neighbourhoods, hierarchically”; “pose” or “a rotation of the whole cloud”; “orthogonality” or “a rotation matrix”.</p>",
