  "id": "e10q34",
  "exam": 10,
  "num": 34,
  "week": "W7",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: A modality becomes usable by a transformer as soon as it can be <strong>tokenised</strong>, which is why the expensive design decision is not the <strong>architecture</strong> but the <strong>tokeniser</strong>. RT-2-X turns a robot's actions into a modality by <strong>discretising the action space into bins and treating each bin as a token</strong>, so that all of the model's <strong>web-scale language pre-training</strong> carries over unchanged.</strong> <strong>Why the architecture stopped being the interesting part.</strong> A transformer places no constraint on what a token <em>means</em> — only that its input is a sequence of vectors. Images become patches, point clouds become a serialised sequence along a space-filling curve, a diffusion timestep becomes a sinusoidal embedding, a graph becomes nodes plus an adjacency mask. Four modalities, one architecture, four different tokenisers.</p><p><strong>Why the tokeniser is where the cost went.</strong> Week 7's runtime comparison is a table of tokenisers, not of architectures: region features from a detector cost around sixty times what a linear patch projection costs, for the same downstream transformer. Choosing how to turn the raw signal into vectors decides the compute budget, the resolution, and what information survives at all.</p><p><strong>Why RT-2-X is the strongest example.</strong> An action is not a perception. It is an <em>output</em>, continuous, in units of joint angles. Discretising each dimension into bins and assigning the bins vocabulary entries means the model emits actions with the same machinery it uses to emit words — so all the language pre-training transfers, because the model does not know it has stopped writing sentences. No architectural change was needed for robotics; a tokenisation was.</p><p><strong>Also accepted:</strong> “turned into a sequence of vectors” / “embedded as tokens”; “the model” or “the transformer”; “how you tokenise it” or “the input encoder”; “binning the continuous actions and treating the bins as vocabulary entries”; “vision and language pre-training”.</p>",
