  "id": "e10q35",
  "exam": 10,
  "num": 35,
  "week": "W9",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: Continual learning has to balance <strong>stability</strong> against <strong>plasticity</strong>. Too much of the first gives <strong>a model that cannot learn the new task</strong>; too much of the second gives <strong>catastrophic forgetting</strong>. The three families of remedy are <strong>regularisation</strong>, <strong>rehearsal</strong> and <strong>parameter isolation</strong>.</strong> <strong>Why it is a dilemma and not a bug.</strong> The same weights encode every task. A weight that matters to task A must move to learn task B, and moving it damages A. There is no setting of the learning rate that escapes this, because a global learning rate trades the two off uniformly: halve it and you halve the forgetting and halve the learning. What the real methods do is make the trade-off <em>selective</em>.</p><p><strong>How each family does that.</strong> <em>Regularisation</em> (EWC and relatives) estimates per-parameter importance — the Fisher information — and penalises movement in the directions that mattered, steering the update through the null space of the old task. <em>Rehearsal</em> keeps or generates old examples and mixes them into the new batches, so the old task keeps supplying gradient; the cost is memory and, if the data is sensitive, a privacy problem. <em>Parameter isolation</em> gives each task its own subnetwork by masking or by growing, which makes forgetting impossible by construction and makes the model grow with the number of tasks.</p><p><strong>Where this sits in the week.</strong> Ordinary deep learning is isolated, label-hungry, compute-hungry and <strong>static</strong>. The first three are costs you can pay. The fourth is a mismatch with a world that changes, and continual learning is the only one of the three escapes that attacks it directly — transfer reduces the labels a new task needs, domain adaptation removes the need to relabel when only the data has moved, and neither makes a deployed model able to keep learning.</p><p><strong>Also accepted:</strong> “remembering” / “learning” for the first two; “rigidity” or “it cannot adapt”; “forgetting the earlier tasks”; “replay” for rehearsal; “dynamic architectures” or “a separate subnetwork per task” for the third.</p>",
