  "id": "e10q36",
  "exam": 10,
  "num": 36,
  "week": "W10",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Filled in: Diffusion's forward process needs no learning because <strong>it is a fixed noise schedule with a closed form for any $t$</strong>. Training is a regression onto <strong>the noise that was added</strong>, evaluated at <strong>a single randomly drawn timestep</strong> rather than along a whole trajectory. Sampling can be changed without retraining because the network was only ever asked <strong>one local question — what noise is in this input, at this level</strong>.</strong> <strong>The closed form is the reason the whole thing is trainable.</strong> Composing $t$ Gaussian steps gives another Gaussian: $q(\\mathbf{x}_t \\mid \\mathbf{x}_0) = \\mathcal{N}(\\sqrt{\\bar\\alpha_t}\\,\\mathbf{x}_0, (1-\\bar\\alpha_t)I)$ with $\\bar\\alpha_t = \\prod_{s\\le t}(1-\\beta_s)$. So a training step can jump straight to any noise level in one operation. Without it you would have to simulate up to a thousand steps to construct one training example, and diffusion would be a curiosity rather than a method.</p><p><strong>Why a single random $t$ suffices.</strong> The loss is an expectation over $t$, and one uniform draw per example is an unbiased estimate of it. Over a training run every level is visited many times. This is what makes the objective embarrassingly simple: draw an image, draw a $t$, draw noise, add it, predict it, take a step.</p><p><strong>And the decoupling that follows.</strong> $\\varepsilon_\\theta$ was never told how samples would be produced. It answers a local question, so <em>any</em> procedure that needs the score at a point may consult it — ancestral sampling in a thousand steps, a DDIM in fifty, a probability-flow ODE solver in thirty. That is why the sampler literature moved faster than the training literature: a better solver is a drop-in improvement to every model already trained. Contrast a GAN, where the generator <em>is</em> the sampler, and improving how you draw samples means retraining.</p><p><strong>Also accepted:</strong> “the noising is fixed and analytic” / “adding Gaussian noise needs no parameters and composes in closed form”; “the added noise $\\varepsilon$” or “the score”; “one timestep sampled uniformly”; “a local denoising question, independent of the trajectory”.</p>",
