  "week": "W1",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Answer: $56\\times56\\times256$; $\\mathbf{295{,}168}$ parameters; $\\approx\\mathbf{9.25\\times10^{8}}$ MACs</strong></p><p>\\[\\begin{aligned} \\text{output} &= 56\\times56\\times256 \\quad (\\text{stride }1,\\ \\text{padding }1 \\Rightarrow \\text{resolution unchanged}) \\\\ \\text{parameters} &= 3\\cdot3\\cdot128\\cdot256 + 256 = 294{,}912 + 256 = 295{,}168 \\\\ \\text{MACs} &= 294{,}912 \\times 56 \\times 56 = 924{,}844{,}032 \\approx 9.25\\times10^{8} \\end{aligned}\\]</p><p><strong>The two quantities depend on different things, and that is the lesson.</strong> The parameter count is $k^2 C_{\\text{in}}C_{\\text{out}} + C_{\\text{out}}$ — the $56\\times56$ never appears. The same layer on a $224\\times224$ map still has $295{,}168$ weights. The MAC count is the parameters (without bias) times the number of positions, so it <em>does</em> scale with the image: sixteen times more at $224\\times224$.</p><p><strong>One bias per output channel.</strong> $256$, not $256\\times56\\times56$ — the same weight-sharing fact seen from the bias side.</p><p><strong>And the number to keep for perspective.</strong> Nine hundred million multiply–accumulates for <em>one</em> layer of a mid-sized backbone. A ResNet-50 forward pass is about $4\\times10^{9}$, so a single layer like this is a quarter of the whole network's arithmetic — which is why the channel count at high resolution is the first thing to look at when a model is too slow.</p>",
  "type": "numeric",
