  "week": "W3",
  "explanation": "<p><strong>Answer: $\\mathbf{197}$ and $\\mathbf{577}$ tokens; the attention matrix grows $\\approx\\mathbf{8.6\\times}$</strong></p><p>\\[\\begin{aligned} 224/16 = 14 &\\Rightarrow 14^2 = 196 \\text{ patches}, \\quad +1 = 197 \\\\ 384/16 = 24 &\\Rightarrow 24^2 = 576 \\text{ patches}, \\quad +1 = 577 \\\\ 577^2 / 197^2 &= 332{,}929 / 38{,}809 \\approx 8.58 \\end{aligned}\\]</p><p><strong>The exponents are what make this hurt.</strong> The side length grew by $384/224 = 1.71$; the patch count by $1.71^2 = 2.94$; the attention matrix by $1.71^4 \\approx 8.6$. Fourth power in the image's linear size. Doubling the resolution would be a factor of sixteen.</p><p><strong>Which is why a plain ViT is not a high-resolution backbone.</strong> Detection and segmentation want $1024$ pixels or more, and $1024/16 = 64$ gives $4097$ tokens and $1.7\\times10^{7}$ pairs per layer per head. Swin's window attention exists for this exact reason, and it is also why fine-tuning a ViT at higher resolution requires interpolating the position embedding table — the number of rows has changed.</p><p><strong>The class token is a rounding error, and mentioning it is still right.</strong> One extra token of $577$; but the question asked for the sequence length, and the sequence length includes it.</p>",
